      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>0"111E troubles that trouble us the most are the troubles that never happen. , Tao man who thinks life is a joke sometimes wonders where the laugh comes in. SOUR men are born to bachelorhood, some achieve bachelorhood and some have bachelorhood thrust upon them. TEACHER : " What is the proper time to gather apples?" Pupil: "When the big dog isn't in the orchard."</p>
        <p>"You are the world to me," he whispered. " All right," she answered. "You can be the sun. rm going to marry your father." pAT an examination of students one young gentleman, bein.; asked to describe Henry VIII., replied : "He was a professional widower." Miss SUMMIT "Mr. Travers just sent me these lovely violets. He is so generous, and I know he couldn't afford it. The poor, dear bog, I don't see where he gets the money from." Dasbaway (savagely): know where he gat the money from In this ease."</p>
        <p>, I—A NICE new umbrella is used up when it is used at all.</p>
        <p>EXPLAINED.--Jonee : "What has made the tele- phone so successful, do you imagine ?" Brown: "I presume it is owing to the fact that it is run on sound priuciples." . .</p>
        <p>t THE WomAN's WAY. —Friend : "Do you per- mit your wife to have her own way?" Husband (positively) : "No, sir. She has it without my permission." Wur is a Rugby football like a drunkard? Because it gets many drops at the bar, and is quits tight. It is always found in a scrimmage, and is often run in. After many tries it is at last carri3d home.</p>
        <p>----- Fulness under the eyes denotes language. When the fulness is large and discoloured and hurts, this denotes that the man has bees using too much of it. _ _</p>
        <p>KIND GENTLEMAN (picking up boy) : "That was a very bad fall, my little man. Why didn't you cry?" Small Boy: "I didn't know anybody was looking." _ .</p>
        <p>HARLEY : But if I have the heroine die in the first act, what the deuce shall I do in the second, third and fourth acts? Lumley: Don't bother about that; the audience won't stay after the first.</p>
        <p>YOUNG MOTHER: "Wake up! Quick! Quick! You must run for the doctor." Young father: " Eh? What's the matter ?" Young Mother: ,"Baby has stopped smiling in her sleep." THE unexpected is the necessary element of humour. That's why a dog enjoys having a tin. can tied to his tail.</p>
        <p>Is you want to see the lateit wrinkle in clothes sit down on the tail of a damp coat. a BUTCHER (TO ARTIST) : "lam not quite satisfied with the portrait. The right side of the chest should bulge somewhat—that is where .I. keep my pocketbook." _ . .</p>
        <p>MRS. O'CALLAGHAN : "I want to get a pair of shoes for my little boy." Shopman "French kid?" Mrs. O'C. (indignantly): " Indade not; he's my own son—born and bred in Oireland."</p>
        <p>CHANGED THE TOOLS.—" Can you split wood with dexterity ?' she asked of the tramp who was looking for a job. " No'm, I allus uses an axe," was the unexpected answer. CUSTOMER (to drygoods clerk) : "You have called me a liar; you must take that back." Clerk : "We never take anything back. But I can change it for you; how would you like to be called a thief?" t TIME AlissrENT.—He (bitterly) : Pshaw All women are alike. She: Then why in the world do 'you spend so much time trying to find the one you want to marry. SIGNIFICANT.—He (after three hours' talk) : " DJn't I give you some thoughts, Miss Helen ?" She (promptly) ; "Yes, Mr. Lambskin, but I shouldn't like to tell you what they were."</p>
        <p>BRicGs : "I see that Granby had to send back the marble statue of his wife that he ordered." Griggs : " Why? Didn't she like it?" Briggs: "No; she wanted one wearing a sealskin cloak." " iIF,LLO, old man, have you any luck shoot- ing?" "I should say I did ! Aho t seventeen ducks in one day," " Were they wild?" "Well —no—not exactly; but the farmer who owned them was."</p>
        <p>LUCKY SHE DIDN'T WRITE " ? "—The shortest love letters on record are said to be the following: —"Dear Clara: ? Tom." "Dear Toni: I will. Clara." How TO GAIN' HER MOTHER'S CONSENT. Ethel : George, mother was looking when you kissed me last night. George: "Oh, how did she take it? Ethel : Well, she said she felt satisfied now you mean buidness, HARSH PUNISHMENT. Mr. Scrubbs (indig- nantly) : "Sir, I have just discovered that your son has engaged himself to two of my daughters." Mr. Grubbs (atupified 'The young rascal! He should be compelled to marry them both."</p>
        <p>CURE WORSE THAN DISEASE.—BiIIy: "I Understand you've bought a dog to keep burglars away?" Freddy: "Yes." Billy: "You are not troubled any more at night, then, I suppose?" Freddy: "Only by the dog." Banbox : "I understand the Prince of Wales was on our train from London to Paris?" Mr. Ls.psusling : "I didn't see him to know him. I guess he must have been travelling impromptu." MR. N. PECR : "I should think you would be ashamed to wear the hair of another woman on your head." Mrs. N. Peck : " Shame yourself, for you wear the skin of another calf on your feet,</p>
        <p>TEACHER " Try to remember this: Milton, the poet, was blind. Do you think you can remember it?" Pupil : "Yes, ma'am." Teacher: " Now, what was Milton's great misfortune?" Pupil : "He was a poet." FORGOT WiTiLE HE WAS REMEMBERING.—" Did you ever try the advice, 'Think before you speak ? '" asked the young man who wants to do good in the world. "Yea," replied Willie Wishington. "And how did it work ? " "I for- got what I was going to say." "Din you destroy this feather duster?" asked Freddie's mother. Yes'm," answered Freddie, "I wanted to he an Indian chief." "But don't you know that feather dusters cost money ? " queried his mother. " I did," said Freddie, "but Indian chiefs don't think of such things."</p>
        <p>AFTER DINNER. - "I'm after my dinner," growled the tramp, sticking his head in the kitchen door about 3 o'clock in the afternoon. "And you're after ours, too," cheerily chirruped the cook, throw- ing a stove lid at him; "we dine at 1 p.m. sharp." . _ _ _</p>
        <p>KEEPING WARE. —Mr. Citimann : "I should think a country house with its exposed walls would be rather uncomfortable in winter." Mr. Suburb : Y-e-s ; Mit no one need be cold in a country house." "How do you manage it?" "Oh, we keep ourselves warm, carrying coal and poking fires, you know." RE PRI tRABLY SWORE.—Stranger: You are the only gentlernsn in the room. Mr. Frocks: In what way, sir? Stranger: When I tripped in the dance, and went sprawling on the floor, tearing my fair partner's dress, you were the only one in the room who did not laugh. Mr. Frocks; The lady is my wife, and I paid for the dress. EVERYTHING WARRANTED.—EuIIfinch : "Have you any indelible ink l" Clerk : "Oh, yes." Bullfinch : " Warranted absolutely indelible ?" Clerk : "I can guarantee it absolutely. Anything snore today ? " Bullfinch : " No." Clerk : " Wculdn't you like an eraser? Here's a very fine ink eraser that I can guarantee will erase indelible ink in a second."</p>
        <p>ENTIRELY INADEQATE.—" Mamma, is it swearing to say that these s ockings ought to be darned ?" " Certainly not, Willie." Willie looked the stockings over again, noted the gaping holes in the knees and the frazzled condition of the heels, laid them down, and shook his head. "No," he said, "there's no use of my sayin' it. It wouldn't be half strong enough." TOMMY WANTED TO KNONY.—Little Tommy stares at the scientific soprano, who opens her mouth very wide and does trills and many other wonderful things- The conductor waves him baton -excitedly just behind l'er, and the noise gets louder and louder. Finally Tommy can stand it no longer. "Mamma," he whispers audibly, "what is that man bitting the lady with the stick for?" "Hush, Tommy ; he isn't hitting her." "Well, then, mamnia, what makes the lady cry</p>
