        <head>LADIES' LETTER'</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER'</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>. - Aileen Christina of Spain is brlnging her influenc.alii; bear against the national pastime of bull-fighting. Lady Jenne is one of the busiest women of the day, and ever ready to interest herself in the cause of charity or good .work. Madame Antoinette Stirling, even when singing bet ' fore the public, always declims to wear evening dre. "'the great singer is-- such a believer -in teetotalism that- ' not only does she exclude wines and spirits from her I, , table, but tea and coffee are barred, as well. ' A Missouri girl recently adopted a peculiar Method of consulting the oracles of fate as to her prospective matri- [ monial venture. She wrote the names of her three 1 admirers on as many eggs and placed them under a sit- ting hen. The lucky swain whose name adorned the egg- thati a s first hatched received the maiden' a band. La es of Honolulu are excellent horsewomen. They ride tride, and wear bifurcated flowing skirts, four yards ivide. Straps of tape, through which the feet are slipped, keep the dress in pace When the feet are in the stirrups. The effect a this cnstom is a much easier and more elegant seat • _ Thimnodem practice of putting women choristors into surpliees has been sternly fortpdden by the Bishop pf Newatk, one of America's: prelAtes. His reason,i,s,thilt, gowned in snowy white, women look so fascir 'Hog that they would distract the attention of the song] ltion.</p>
        <p>As the weather gets colder tailor-made gowns will be well to the fore. But these will be seen to have lost their severity of outline, and are hardly recognisable as emanating from the hands of the tailor. The simplicity of tight-fitting sleeves hag given place to the bouffant style usually to be found In frocks which do not aspire to being tailor-built. The unadorned skirt is unadorned no longer, but boasts of rows of braid, fanciful galons, or applica- tions of cloth worked in gold or silver. These trimmings, too, are reproduced with much elabora- tion on the bodice.</p>
        <p>Toques and small hats promise to be popular this autumn, and wings much used as a trimming. A new toque has the crown composed of smooth black feathers and the brim of puckered green velvet. Height is given by two grey and white birds, divided by a full black. A hat of tan, with black Mercury wings, and rosettes of satin looks pretty. A capital method of renovating a summer silk petticoat, upon which the lace flounces have be. come very dilapidated, is to substitute for these one flounce made of alternate rows of thick cream- coloured insertion and black ribbon. This looked very well on a black satin petticoat, and, of course, the same fashion could be carried out with coloured ribbons on brocaded or fanciful skirts; it should be borne in mind, though, that the lowest row of this trimming should be the ribbon, for ribbon has not that unpleasing habit of wearing out when kicked by the heel of the boot, which is a decided characteristic of lace.</p>
        <p>Pretty blouses prepared for autumn wear may be fashioned in cashmere, delaine, crepe, silk, or any thin material, with the trimmings in velvet or satin in any contrasting shade. Last week I saw a lovely blouse of yellow silk—almost buttercup— trimmed with row upon row of white insertion, which formed a yoke. The sleeves were puffs ot the same silk, arranged so that each puff became smaller and smaller, till it reached the wrist, and each one of these lovely puffs was held in place by a band of white insertion.</p>
        <p>A capital cape is of Harris tweed, in a rather . large fawn and brown check, unlined, and arranged with a short but fully-pleated shoulder-frill of the same material, and a deep turn-over collar. This is so arranged that it may be turned up at will, and buttoned across under the chin witka narrow flap of the same tweed. A pretty dress for a fair girl of nine or 10 is of cinnamon-brown diagonal serge (brown 'is to be much worn this season), the full skirt is bound with a band of deep turquoise blue velvet, fiaished with a tiny brown cord, and is gathered into a yoke of the same, which is deeply scalloped round the edge, and corded to match the skirt trimming. Scalloped epaulettes of the velvet fall over the tops of the wide sleeves, which are of the serge, with velvet cuffs, scalloped at the edge next the elbow. Double skirts are decidedly fashionable for the dresses of older girls—and gowns of tan or golden brown serge, made in this style and trimmed with black military braid, are both useful and stylish. The two deep flounces which form the skirt are edged with rows of braid, and the bodice has a pelerine frill and deep corselet-belt trimmed to match. The new pelerine frills, by the way, can scarcely claim to be called frills, as they are almost, if not quite, plain and without fulness.</p>
        <p>A very pretty frock for a girl of six is made 'of shot silk, with a plcated yoke and full back and velvet cuffs, and shoulder cape falling over puffed sleeves. _</p>
        <p>Very deep flounces %re becoming quite fashion- able, and the double skirts are so arranged that the lower skirt has almost the effect of a deep flounce. An excellent afternoon gown can be made by cutting tho full bodice short to the waist, and finishing with a corded hem. The waistband is composed of white cotton webbing, over which is laid a ribbon or piece of silk and a black insertion lace. Round the neck is a gathered frill of black lace, about seven or eight inches deep, for whiCl two yards and a quarter are required, and the collar is covered with lace insertion. The small sleeves are made with a double pouff of the material. The width of the skirt can be materially increased in appearance by a flounce of black lace about 14 to is inches deep, and this is put on very full, there- fore five yards should be allowed for it. A grey, thin cloth of beige is extremely pretty made in this s'yle ; and if the wide lace is not desired on the skirt, make the deep flcunce of the material with a band of lace insertion at the edge and as a heading; or gather the top of the flounce, and put a narrow lace at the edge. Yours very sincerely, JENNII3I.</p>
        <p>A Japanese lady has written a novel in 90 volumes. Princess Beatrice is able to play the most difficult music at sight. . .</p>
        <p>Mrs. Bariatt Browning is encouraging window garden- ing in the beautiful town of Azolo.</p>
        <p>Aegat b, How to Cook Vegetable Marrow. First of all vegetable marrow should be cut into four pieces or more if desirable, and the seeds removed. then these should be Washed in cold water and plunged into boiling water just long enough before being wanted for table.. All vegetables spoil it allowed to boil too long. When vegetables sink they are sufficiently cooked. About twenty minutes after putting the marrow into the pan, look and see if the vegetable has sunk, and, if so, take it up immediately. Make a white sauce and pour over the marrow before sending it to table.</p>
        <p>Gruel. Gruel is very easy to make, yet if it is not done pro- perly it is not at all palatable. A little oatmeal should be put into a basin, and boiling water poured it. Keep stirring all the while the water is being added. Add sugar to taste and milk to cool the gruel. On a bottle of nit! e it will say how much should be given in case of colds. The quantity of oatmeal used, of course, depends upon the patient. He may take it thick or he may like it thin, and he may be able to take much or very little.</p>
        <p>Vermicelli Pudding. Vermicelli is a nourishing food (says Mrs. Beeton in her "Book of Household Management ") and means little worms, which in form it resembles. Four ozs. of ver- micelli, 3.1 pints of milk, 3 oz. butter, 3 oz. sugar and 4 eggs make sufficient pudding for five or six persons, Seasonable always,. Boil the vermicelli in the milk un- til it ft; tender, stirring in the other ingredients. Cream may be added (11 pint if obtainable). Flavour with grated lemon-rind, essence of bitter almonds, or vanilla. Run into a puff paste, edged buttered pie dish, and bake in a moderate over for about I of an hour.</p>
