        <head>A. B. C. Guide To Health.</head>
        <p>A. B. C. Guide To Health.</p>
        <p>Some time ago, in New York, a card, on which were printed the following lines, was gratuitously distributed among the tenement houses in the lower wards of the city - As soon as you are'up, shake blankets and sheet; Better be without shoes than sit with wet feet ; Children, if healthy, are active, not still ; Damp sheets and damp clothes will both make you ill ; Eat slowly, and always chew your food well ; Freshen the air in the 'lowa where you dwell; Garments must never be made to be tight ; Homes will be healthy if airy and light; If you wish to be well, as you do, Poen° doubt, Just open the windows before you ge out; Keep your rooms always tidy and clean, Let dust on the furniture never be seen; Much illness is caused by the want of pure air, Now to open your windows be ever you care; Old rags and old rubbish should never be kept; People should see that their floors are well swept; Quick movements in children are healthy and right; Remember the young cannot thrive without light. See that the cistern is clean to the brim ; Take care that your dress is all tidy and trim ; Use your nose to find out if there be a bad drain; Very sad are the fevers that come in its train. Walk as much as you can .vithout feeling fatigue ; Xerxes could walk full many a league; Your health is your wealth ,which your wisdom must keep Zeal will help a good cause, and the good you will reap.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>MY OLD SCHOOLMASTER.</head>
        <p>MY OLD SCHOOLMASTER.</p>
        <p>A VIVID RECOLLECTION.</p>
        <p>When he intended to thrash a boy my old schoolmas- ter, Dr. Switchback, used to ask—" Will yen take it now, or at four o'clock P" Most of the boys, being a bit timid„, used to put it off till four; one or two, with strong nerves, preferred to have it right off the reel and get the painful ceremony over. Dr. Switchback was I accustomed to employ a fresh cane for each culprit, and he always handed a penny to the boy whose name was down for a licking, and made him purchase his own instrument of torture. " Get a good swishy one," he would say, "and not too thick; "for this pedagogue had found by experience that thick, unbendable canes did not produce such lively sensations as canes resembl- ing wands of willow—or, rather, to use a fitter compari- ' son weeping willow. When the thrashing was over and the culprit was rubbing his tender hide, Dr. Switch- back (who must have been a humorist) would give the boy a penny and tell him to go out and buy some sweet- stuff wherewith to console himself. There was one boy with a hide like a rhinoceros, who never cried beneath the cane. He was so unruly that his mother asked the doctor to break him in. Be used to be thrashed regu- larly every day at 11 a.m, to the minute. If he had not misbehaved himself at that hour be was sure to after- wards. I remember one lad who could not learn the verb " Amo." "I'll teach you," the doctor used to say to him, as he laid the dunce, face downwards, across hi knee and uplifted the cane. Now follow me : .. . . .</p>
        <p>" Amo r(swish). Ama (swish) mus (swish). AIMS (swish). Ama (swish) tis (swish). Amat (swish). Amant " (swish, swish, swish). In the evening the same boy would he kept in to write out the whole conjugation of this interesting verb fifteen or twenty times. When he had nearly got to the end of his task, the doctor would, in nine cases out of ten,send him a slice of hot plum-pudding from the dinner table. Although Switchback had his good points, nearly all the boys were longing tc," pay him out "for the various liberties which he had taken with their highly sensitive skins. They sometimes revenged themselves on his pet dog, a Skye terrier, which came in for sundry kicks when the doctor was well out of sight. This animal, at a certain hour of the day, used to have a dish of hot stew placed for him in the playground. To the natural elements of this stew the boys were wont to make sun- dry sly additions, such as cigar ends, and compound rhubarb pills. The unlucky animal frequently ate the whole concoction, and looked decidedly upset at the end of it.—Chums.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
