      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>WHICH INEBBIATES AND DOESN'T CHEER.</head>
        <p>WHICH INEBBIATES AND DOESN'T CHEER.</p>
        <p>It is curious to note what strange expedients women who have a weakness for strong drink will resort to in order to satisfy their craving, while at the same time avoiding public notice. A lady who asked for a cup of tea at the refreshment bar of a big London railway station recently made a discovery which startled her considerably. "A cup of gin, did you say ?" queried the attendant. "No, certainly not !" was the indignant reply. "1 asked for a cup of tea, and I'll thank you to let me have it." "I beg your pardon, I'm sure," said the barmaid, " but the fact is I did not quite hear what you asked for, and ladies often come in here and ask for gin in cups." They do this because they don't like other customers to know that they are drinking spirits, and casual observers, seeing them with refreshments in cups, naturally conclude that they are having too or coffee." Whereat the lady expressed astonishment.— From " Cassell's Saturday Journal" for October.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>ENORMOUS DECLINE IN SALT • EXPORTS. HAMBURG LEADS LIVERPOOL.</head>
        <p>ENORMOUS DECLINE IN SALT • EXPORTS. HAMBURG LEADS LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>Probably for the first time in the history of commerce the salt exports from the Mersey to the East were over- stepped last month by the exports from Hamburg. The difference is slight, 12,000 tons from Liverpool as against 12,100 from German port, but the fact is worthy of note, and should not fail to convey its lessan to the com- munity. As compared with the first nine months of last year, this rear's total exports from the Mtirsey show a falling off of something like 25 per cent., a truly startling occurrence. We have so often traced the decline Of the Mersey salt trade to the mismanagement, apathy, and over capitalisation of the Salt Union, that it, would seem waste of time to say more, but in view of the above re- markable ficures, we have no hesitation in asking that the Government should at once institute an inquiry into the whole subject.—" Liverpool Journal of Commerce."</p>
        <p>A nephew oF the Duke of Richmond earns his living by running a 9oriEt's establithment at Swiss Cottage.</p>
