      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>DUIY OF THE PRESS.</head>
        <p>THE DUIY OF THE PRESS.</p>
        <p>is the duty of every individual, and especially the • rresa, to distribute news and facts which tend to pro- mote general welfare ; and by this means only will they reach all classes. Nothing is more deserving of publicity than the proper means or remedies which will ensure Pnblic health, and prevent the ravages of prevalent chronic diseases from which thcusan.ls lead a life of suf- fering and pain, because the proper means and remedies re not known Prominent among the remedies thus belegerving of mention is Warner's Safe Cure. We again eaLg before our read-rs a marvellous cure effected in the one of our citizens, Mrs Emily Snell, of 40,North- biorsham, who writes—" For fifteen years my life ,as a daily burden. I suffeied from inflammation of the bladder and indigestion ; burning pains in groin and back, excruciating pains in the pit of the stomach, eta df u 1 aching of the eves, coldness of the extremities, r ansd ?sing flatulencv,pain and burning during urination, uad taste in the aiouth. I was extremely nervous ;;low spirited,gradually growing weaker and weaker, 1:"13 great loss of flesh. After all these years of suffer- 'fg and fruitless attempts to better my condition,' was red k - -Y Warner's Safe Cure. I can walk long distances 000! without pain or inconvenience. Have regained my / ff. and my natural life vigour has returned, so now 'uJoY life, and follow my domestic occupations with nninfort and ease."</p>
