      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>„,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TV )WN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A monthly meeting of this Council was held in the Town Hull on Tuesday evening. The Mayor (Alderman Greenall) presided, and there were present Aldermen Francomb, Ilarrison, Sutton, Wright, B lackh unit; Councillors Roberts, Bolton, Hutchinson, ft. T. Fairclough, Shaw Gieer., Cannel!, Smethurst, Hill, Muir, Ashton, Plumpton, Flood, Wilkinson, Beaman, Greening, Tinsley, Heaton, fiepheid, Shaw; Messrs. Whittle (town clerk), Longdin (borough surveyor), Fairhurst (borough treasurer), and S. Wilson (assistant town Clerk). THE HEALTH OF THE MAYOR, ! Before the ordinary business of the meeting was en- tered upon, Alderman HainasoN said he wished to express net only his own but he was sure the sympathies of every member of the Council, and their delight on again seem," the Mayor occupy the chair in apparently an ex- cellent state of health. (Hear, hear.) He. hoped he would go on enjoying an uninterrupted period of health until the termination of his period of office and after that for the remaining portion of his life. (Hear, hear.) The MAYOR said he was extremely obliged to Alderman Harrison for the very kind remarks he had made. He ' had come back restored to heal h and strength, and he hoped able to go on with his work. (Hear, bear.) THE WATCH COMMITTEE AND THE MAGISTRATES.—A RESOLUTION WITHDRAWN. The minutes of the Watch Committee contained the following resolution :—" That this Committee regret that the magistrates appear to have acted with a want of courtesy by issuing instructions to and employing the police without in any way consulting the Watch Conn- mittee and that such instructions be not acted upon with- out first consulting with this Committee." The MAYOR, in moving the adoption of the minutes, said he wished to explain that the resolution was for the ptesent withdrawn in consequence of the licensiag jus- tices having requested an interview with the Watch Committee on the subject of the resolution, and he there: fore proposed that the minutes of the Committee be ap- proved with the exception of the resolution referred to. Alderman HARRISON seconded. Councillor FLO( D: Was this resolution carried by the' unanimous voice of the Watch Committee? The MAYOR: I am advised that it is not now before the Council, and not open to discpsion. Councillor FLOOD: I am merely asking a question as to why. it is on the agenda paper at all. The MAYOR: It is not open to discussion. Councillor FLIOOD : I call that burking the question. Councillor Morn : There are very few questions passed unanimously by -any Committee. The minutes were then passed with the exception of resolution.</p>
        <p>THE HEATH COMMITTEE. Alderman SUTTON, HI moving the adoption of the minutes, said that the committee had no difficulty in passing the recommendations of Dr. Gornall,the Medical Officer with regard to re-arrangement of the duties of the Sanitary inspectors inasmuch as they were simply carrying out an order of the Local Government Board. Councillor FLOOD seconded the adoption of the minutes, which were confirmed. SANITARY WORKS CCIIMITTEE—THE IMPROVEMENTS AT</p>
        <p>LONGFORD. Councillor FLOOD, in moving the adoption of the minutes of the Sanitary Works Committee, said that he believed that the new furnace at Longford depot which was the invention of Councillor Beaman and Mr. Dees, the manager at Longford, would be the mean, of des- troying the smells for that place which had been the cause of a great deal of complaint. (Hear, hear). Councillor ROBERTS, U seconding the proposal, said that he had had an opportunity of studying the plans of the new furnace from an outsider's point of view, and as far as he was able to judge he had every reason to believe that it would fully carry out all the anticipa- tions of Mr. Deas and Councillor Beaman, who were responsible for its creation. (Hear, hear). Councillor MUIR said he had I een to Longford depot and found that they were going on on very well there with their new furnace. On inquiry he had been ,informed that no royalty would be charged on the patent and he had been assured that the money that had been spent would be well spent. Councillor H. T. FAIRCLOUGH reminded the Councillor that Councillor Beaman said some time ago that a sur- prise was in store for the Council regarding Longford He wondered whether the patent was the surprise? (Hear, hear, and laughter). Councillor Minn : lam sorry Councillor Beaman is not here to answer the question. (Councillor Beaman had not at that time put in an appearance.) The minutes were then confirmed. PAVING AND SEWERAGE COMMITTEE. Alderman WRIGHT proposed the adoption of the minutes of this Committee. He had nothing special to say except that the paving oh Cairo-st. and Friar's-green was being proceeded with. He also wished to remark that while the distress prevailed, the Committee had taken steps to find employment for the poor men. They had now at work in this one department, 163 men. He also wished to mention what would be no doubt gratify- ing to the Council as it was to the Committee, and which was also due to the men, and that was that the department was getting work done as well and cheaply as they could otherwise have'got it done. (Hear, hear.) They put the men to work at what they could earn their money which was 3s. per day, acid the department was not losing anything by it. Councillor -Lbranix seconded the adoption of the minutes. iher of the Executive</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS as a mein. Committee of the Relief Fund, look the opportunity of thanking the Council aid officials of the Corporation for the very ready manner in which they had responded to the request to find the men work. (Hear, hear.) He thought it was only right that he should bear that tes- timony to them. (Hear, hear.) The minutes were ad ipted. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE. Councillor SHAW-GREEN moved the adoption of the minutes of the School Attendance Committee. In doing so, he read a fetter from Mr. E. Phelps, H.M I. of schools for the district, arranging for holding a local examina- tion for labour certificates. Councillor SmErnonsr seconded the adoption of the minutes which was agreed to.</p>
        <p>TOWN HALL, PARK AND BATHS COMMITTEE. Councillor Bouroli moved the ad )ption ot the minutes of this Committee. He was pleased to mention that the income of the new baths had increased more than £5O, and it this cL.ntinued the baths would be a great success. Councillor Mom seconded the motion. Councillor ItonnaTs asked if it was necessary that the baths should not he opened until eight o'clock in the mornings now. He pointed out that this was a very in- convenient time for most peal le who wished to take a "dip "in the mornings, especially as most of them had to be at business at nine o'clock. While the mornings were so light could not the Laths be opened at seven o'clock, he asked. Councillor BOLTON said that they had given notice to the manager to open the baths at 7-30 until the next meeting of the Committee, but he had no objection to their being opened at 7 o'clock, and agreed to that suggestim. GAS COMMITTEE. Councillor R. T. FAIRCLOUGH moved the adoption of the minutes of this 'Committee. The new gas holder which they had purchased he was pleased to say was in every way satisfactory. Therr was a minute to the effect that the cottages in Chrimes-yard were to be de- molished. This he pointed out because of their insanit- ary condition. He had no doubt the Council would be glad to hear that so far during the coal strike they had gone on satisfactorily is regard to their coal supply. , They had had to buy some coal, but if the preseet price of coal confirmed there would have to be an increase in the price of gas. They would have had a difficulty in keeping up the supply if they had not had a supply of cannel. The cannel Was bought because they had in view the coal strike, and for other reasons. Councillor Hurnfion seconded tile adoption of the minutes. Councillor IlurcHmsow thought the quality of the gas was capable of hnprovement. Councillor BEAMAN mentioned that twelve months ago, a resolution was passed by the Council for the purchase , of an apparatus for the proper and independent testing of the gas, but they had not got it yet. Councillor FAIRCLOUOH in replying said with respect to Councillor Hutchinson's complaint about the quality of the gas, he could say that he disagreed with him. The 'gas at the Parish Church was as good as at any place in Lancashire. Councillor HUTCHINSON : Oh but you go there. Couneillor FAIRCLOUGn : What applies to one place applies to the other. If the fittings at Councillor Hutch- inson's church are out of repair he ought to send down to the works. (Laughter.) Respecting the purchase of the instrument mentioned by Councillor Beaman he could only say that the matter had been adjourned sine die. The min utes were adopted. RIVERS COMMITTEE.—THE CORPORATION AND TMS CANAL COMPANY. Councillor R. T. Fainctouon in moving the confirma- tion of the minutes of the Rivers Committee, read clause 88, section 14 of the Ship Canal Act,lBBs,to show that the Canal Company were required, before the canal was opened for traffic, at their own cost ti dredge the bed and banks of the river at a certain depth. The Borough Surveyor had been watching the progress of events, and he did so for the reason that there was a difference a short time ago between the Canal Company and the London and North-Western Railway Company, and Sir John Harwood said the Railway Company were throwing obstacles in the way of the -Canal Cotnpasy. The committee did not wish to have the same thing said of them as was said of the Railway Company. They did not wish it to be said that the canal was being kept back by the Warrington Corporation. Therefore they sent a copy of their minutes t them every month. Councillor ROBERTS said they had every desire to work in the greater possible harmony with'the Ship Canal, because they knew the difficulties the company had had to encounter, and they did not want to add one single item that should in any sense be a burden to them. At the same time, as representing the interests of the town, they did not want to see anything overlooked. The minutes were passed. t</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>FXAMINER, SAT URD AT, OCTOBER 7, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE FXAMINER, SAT URD AT, OCTOBER 7, 1893.</p>
        <p>FIRE ENGINE COMMITTEE. NACLE Councillor HUTCHINSON, in noting the adoption of the ilk )11* * • minutes, mentioned that an 'alarm of fire was frequently .. given, and the fire bell rung, and yet the brigade were not able to ascertain where the fire was situated. The Watch Committee had been written to, and Mr. Hunt, the chief constable, had been requested to see that the police used more vigilance, so that persons who rang the bell without cause might be seized and punished. Councillor Dr. CANNELL seconded. Councillor SHAW GREEN asked when the Council might expect the new fire engine that had been ordered. Some months ago they were told that it only wanted painting and its head wetting. (Laughter.) Councillor HUTCHINSON explained that when the fire engine was ordered,it was promised for the first week in August. The committee had been from time to time in communication with Messrs. Merryweather, and the last reply was that they should have it in the middle of October. It was finished,and would be on exhibition at Hyde Park next Saturday. The minutes were then confirmed. - - • t, WATER COMMITTEE. The minutes of this committee were confirmed,on the motion of Alderman Fa..atecoma, seconded by Councillor HEPHERD. Councillor Snew rose to speak, but was ruled out of order by the Mayor.—Councillor Saw : If you make any mistake it is your own fault not mine, I say—ethat fhe TOWN CLERK: It is out of order. k Councillor SHAW : Are the Water Committee's minsu tes before the Council? A VOICE : No; they are passed. The MAYOR : You are out of order. . e Councillor Slum: Will you let me mention the subject which The MAYOR: No. GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee it was resolved—That the common seal of the borough be affixed to the following documents :—Lease of the Nag's Head, to Mr. Lawrence Flood ; lease of the Millstone Inn, to Mr. J. Davies ; mortgages numbered 27 and 28 for £2OO on baths extension, and mortgages numbered 224 to 235 inclusive for £1,200 on water undertaking. The minutes were passed,on the motion of the MAYOR, seconded by Alderman FRANCOMB. A QUESTION OF TENDERS. Councillor SHAW, atter reraarking that" he supposed he should have a chance this time " moved " That the tenders for the painting of the market hall and shed be produced for examination, an that an explanation be given as to why the lowest tender was not accepted." He found that Mr. Dell's tender, which was the one accepted by the Corporation, was for £234 155., and there was another tender before the committee from Mr. Waring, who had done the work nine years ago for £165, which was the amount of his tender on the present occasion, leaving a margin of £69 15s. He only brought this matter forward from a ratepayer's point of view. Considering the bad times he thought the committee might have displayed better care and judgment than accept a tender so much higher than the other. If as it might be said, Mr. Waring did not do his woe( nine years ago as it should have been done, why did the com- mittee pay the bill, and on the other hand, if the work was done satisfactorily, why should they not accept his tender ? He should like an explanation. Councillor ROBERTS said he would second the motion, not because he thought the committee had not done what was correct. No committee was required to accept the lowest tender. The Markets Committee, like other ci..mmittees, used its discretion. No doubt the chairmin of the committee would be able to give a perfectly satisfactory explanation as to why the tender had been accepted. He seconded the motion so that there should be an opportunity of explan- ation. At the same time, if he had thought there had been any favouritism he would have been amongst the first to condemn the action of the committee unspar- ingly. During the time he had been a member of the Council he was bound to say that he had never seen any. thing which would justify the casting of a slur on toe action of any committee with regard to the letting of contracts. (Hear, hear.) Alderman BLACKHURST said this was the most extra- ordinary motion be had over seen on an agenda paper. If a member of the Council wished to see a tender he could do so on application at the office of the Borough Surveyor, (Hear, hear) It would be dangerous to ex- plain why the committee did not accept the lowest ten- der. The committee had a reason, and a sufficient reason, for what they did. Beyond that he did not think he was justified in saying anything. Councillor ROBERTS : After that explanation I shall withdraw from the position of seconding the motion. I can quite sympathize with Alderman .Blackhurst in not desiring to go into details. Councillor SHAW : After hearing the remarks of Alderman Blackhurst, lam very well satisfied. (Hear, hear.) The Council then resolved itself into a committee "for the selection of Mayor for the ensuing year, and other business," and the reporters withdrew.</p>
        <p>any— The</p>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD. --- Mr. Pritchard presided over the monthly meeting held on Tuesday evening. Present the Rev. Canon Wood, Dr. McDougall, Messrs. S. Taylor, T. Hand- ley, E. 'liaison, and G. Moss (clerk.)</p>
        <p>, SCHOOL ATTENDANCE. The report of the School Attendance Committee showed that the Committee had held two meetings during the month and had interviewed 26 parents. The attend- ance returns for September showed a falling off in the number on the rolls of 34, and an average attendance of 17, compared with the same month last year. The per- centage was 83 against 82.8. The annual report of the Committee for the year ending September 29th, was read. It showed an increase in the number on the rolls, ana in the average attendance as compared with the preceding year. There was a slight improvement in the percentage.—The minutes were approved on the motion of Canon Wood, seconded by Mr. Handley.</p>
        <p>SUCCESS OF THE SCHOOLS. The School Management Committee reported that the annual reports of H.M. Inspector of Schools had been received. The reports showed that the whole cf the schools had obtained the highest merit grants. Mill Brow Infants' School again earned 17s. per head, the highest figure obtainable in an infant school. Greenway-road Boys' SCh3ol earned 19s. 6d. per head and 21s. for excel- lence in drawing; and the Girls' School obtained 20s. 6d. per hehd. The reports were considered highly satisfac- tory. The Commtttee recommended that the following teachers be appointed to the evening schools: Miss Kil- burn, Miss Ellison, Miss Acton and Mr. E W. Rosser.— Dr. McDougall moved the adoption of the minutes and remarked that it must he swisfactory to the Board and to the ratepayers that the Schools had attained such a high state of eflicienc). They had during the year ebtailuNt the highest possible grant.—Mr. Handley seconded, and said the results must be highly gratifying to everyone.—The Chairman remarked that they had every reason to be satisfied considering what the b*-hoofs had to contend with when established ; seeing that is. any all the strays were driven there. The results must have been attained by determination and diligunce on the part of the masters and mistresses. Thanks were due to the teachers for the energetic way in which they had per- formed their duties.—The minutes were passed. __APPLICATIONS FOR INCREASES OF SALARY.</p>
        <p>Applications for increases of salary were read from Miss Kilburn, head teacher of Greenway-road School, and Miss Robb, head teacher of Mill Brow Infant School. The communications were referred to the School Man- agement Committee.</p>
        <p>A NEW PRECEPT. The Clerk reported that the expenditure for the corn- ing year would be about the same amount as usual, and it was resolved to issue a precept upon the overseers for £5OO for the first eight months of the year.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL SANITARY AUTHORITY. The fortnightly meeting was held at Dutton Workhouse 3n Tuesday. Mr. Mclntyre presided and there were present Colonel Fox (ex-officio), the Rev. J. W. N. Tanner, Messrs. E. Aaron, H. E. Heywood, J. Weir, C. E. Davenport, J. Smith, J. M. Williams, C. T. Whitley, VV, Priestner, W. Whitlow, J. L. Jones, S. Woodward, J. Parkinson, J. Ashton (Clerk), G. Ashton (deputy clerk). THE SEWERAGE OF APPLETON. The contract with Messrs. Prescott and Robert Edward Jones, of Patricroft, for the cowering of a portion of Appleton for the sum of £l,OOl 7s. was ordered to be signed and sealed. MR. PRITCHARD APPEALS TO THE L.G.B. The Local Government Board forwarded the following communication from Mr. Joseph Pritchard, of Halton Brook,.with a request to be furnished with the observa- tions of the Authority thereon :—" This is an open sewer flowing into a stream called Halton Brook, which stream runs through many fields used for cattle grazing. Originally the drain carried the sewage from six houses and two small cottages; in those days it was bad enough. Within the last two years one dozen small houses have been built which the local Sanitary Authority have sanctioned being connected with the old drain, which I may here mention flows under my garden before it joins the sewer and is carried into the brook. This year four more houses have been ereated and are about to be tenanted, and the connection has been made with the old drains so that what was originally intended to convey the sewage of two cottages has been made to take the drainage of sixteen before it joins our sewer. In Novem- ber, 1891, SOMQ of my neighbours and myself called the attention of the Sanitary Authority to the filthy and dan- gerous condition of the sewer. A committee was formed and the sewer inspected, a report was given to the Auth- ority, some resolutions passed, the only thing uone was to sent a man with a shovel and have it cleaned. Some months afterwards we again addressed a letter to the Sanitary Authority, this time threatening that if nothing was none we should communicate with the Local Government Board. Nothing has been done for months, the whole of the sewage flowed over a field, the solid matter remaining as k source of danger to the health of the neighbourhood, until we persuaded the man who farms the land to cut a trench so as to carry the sewage into the brook. Will you kindly take the matter up and compel the Sanitary Authority to take some steps to remedy what at present exists as a hot bed :.or propogating disease. With small- pox, typhoid fever and other diseases, existing in the near neighbourhood, one may bepardoned for displaying a little anxiety especially when there are children—hence my appeal to you."—The Clerk said he had replied to the Local Government Board stating that negotiations were proceeding between the Authority and the owner of the land to put in a pipe sewer and directly the agreement was completed the work would be put in haud.—The Board considered this satifactory. HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT. The Medical Officer (Dr. Adams) reported that in com- pany with the Inspector he had made a detailed exami- nation of the property at Weston Point, where typhoid fever had been prevalent, and beyond over-full ashpits and privies he could find nothing to throw any further light on the outbreak. A case of typhoid fever had oc- curred at Halton,the patient having come home ill from Warrington. No sanitary action was called for. Another case of scarlet fever had occurred at Kingsley. A fatal case of typhoid fever had occurred at Helsby. There appeared to be possible sources of contamination,and he suggested that the water should be analysed. INSANITARY PROPERTY AT HALTON. A letter was read from Dr. Ormsby complaining that the insanitary condition of Holly Bank had not been re- medied. The matter was referred to the Nuisance In- spector. THE LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD AND THE UNEMPLOYED. A circular letter from the Local Government Board was read, drawing attention to the great scarcity of employment existing at the present time,and which was likely to increase as the winter advanced,and suggesting that Boards of Guardians and sanitary authorities should undertake schemes of public improvement in order to provide work for those artisans and labourers who de- sired to escape the stigma of pauperism.—The Chairman said there was no likelihood of anything of that kind be- ing required in the Hnion,and they trusted the necessity never would arise.—Mr. Williams said the Authority had matured schemes months and months ago,and they were simply and solely waiting for action by the Local Government Board, which so far had greztly impeded their progress. This was especially so in regard to the supplying of Frodsham with water. In that township they had been greatly parched for water during the summer,and they had at the present time an epidemie of diphtheria and sore throat, and had had even worse troubles. For ten or twelve weeks, the Authority had waited for a reply from the Local Government Board to a communication,and even now they were going through a process of arbitration which was utterly useless and costly. Therefore it was not the Authority which wanted moving but the Local Government B-ard. He moved that a reply to that effect he sent to the Local Govern- ment Board.—Mr.Aaron seconded the motion with great pleasure. The Authority had asked the Local Govern- ment Board to send a man down to consult with them as to where they should build an infectious hospital, but they had declined to do so, and no doubt when the Au- thority had selected a site the Local Government ea oard would refuse to sanction it. They would pass Acts of Parliament for parks,howling greens,dancing rooms,and other things at the cost of the ratepayers, and vet they woeld not send a man to show them a site for a hospital, and when the Authority have chosen a site the Local Government Board would not have it.—Mr. Heywood said he would support the resolution if reference were made to the hospital.—Mr. Williams agreed, and the mo- tion was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>DELEGATES.</head>
        <p>DELEGATES.</p>
        <p>The Chairm' an the Rev. Canon Wood, and the Clerk were appointed delegates to the Associatlon of School Wards; and Dr. McDougall, Mr. T. Handley, and the Clerk were appointed to represent the Board on the Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes at the annual confe- rence to be held at Northwich on the 17th October.</p>
