      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>CHARGE AGAINST AN ASHTON GREENGROCER.</head>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST AN ASHTON GREEN- GROCER.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED HORSE STEALING.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Thursday morning John Wood, greengrocer and coal dealer, Asliton-in-Makerfleld was charged on a warrant with feloniously stealing it horse, value £25, on the 2nd inst. Mr. A. Browne, represented the prosecutor, (Mr. Joseph Caldwell, greengrocer, Winwick-road, Warrington), and Mr. Forshaw defended. no:n Mr. Browne's statement it appeared that the pros- ecutor had a chestnut horse of which he had thought of disposing of, and on Saturday, the 30th September, the defendast came to him to buy it. There was a •6 swop." , The defendant changed his horse for Mr. Caldwell's, and gave him 53. to boot. No conditions were named when the bargain was struck. Mr. Caldwell put the grey horse in the ley belonging to Mr. 'Thomas Wright, Folly- lane, on Saturday, but on Monday morning Mr. Wright I imformed Mr. Caldwell that his horse had been taken. He (Mr. Browne) was informed of the matter, and he advised Mr. Wright, to go to Ashton to see Mr. Wood. Mr. Wright went,and the defendant admitted taking the grey horse out of the field at seven o'clock on Monday morning, but he said the horse belonged to him. A warrant was applied for and granted,—Mr. Joseph Caldvrell, greengrocer gave evidence as to the bargain,and putting the horse in Mr. Wright's ley. He received the grey horse back again from Sergeant Monaghan the previous night. He had never had the chestnut horse back, nor been asked for the five shillings to be returned. The defendant did not tell him he was going to return the horse.—Mr. Thomas Wright said he received the grey horse in his Icy on Saturday,but found it had gone on Monday morn- ing. He did not know there had been an exchange when he was advised by Mr. Browne 'to see the defendant. When he asked the defendant if he had got the horse he replied " Yes, I have got it in the stable." Ile (witness) told Him that he was responsible for the horse and Cald- well had asked him to come over for it. Defendant said he would not deliver the horse up, as there had been an exchange, and that the horse belonged to him. The defendant said he had taken Mr. Caldwell's horse back again when he took the grey horse from his (witness's) , field on Monday morning. D.S. Monaghan gave evidence as to the arrest of the prisoner, and bringing the grey horse to Warrington. Mr. Wright re-called said the defendarit had put the chestnut horse, which had belonged to Mr. Caldwell, in a field opposite to his, as it was there now. At this stage Alderman Sutton said the bench didn't see that there was any intention on the part of the prisoner to steal the horse. The difference between the two horses was very small, as Mr. Caldwell was satisfied with five shillings when the exchange took place. It seemed to him that, it was more a case for a civil action in the County Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>NEW ZEALAND BUTTER FOR MANCHESTER. Mr. Charles R. Valentine, Chief Dairy Commissioner to New Zealand, delivered an address on Tuesday before the members of the Manchester Chamber of Commerce, on "The production and marketing of colonial dairy produce." Re stated that New Zealand was able to pro- duce and send the finest qualities of butter, and that she desired Manchester, as one of the most important distributing centres in the world, to help her to place her produce on the market. The President of the Cham- ber (Mr. L. Ogden) said ha looked forward to an early arrival in the port of Manchester of ships direct from New Zealand and other colonial ports. Re thought that at an early period next year, probably the first week of the year, the Ship Ca nal would be opened for traffic.</p>
        <p>King Lobengula lives in a house made of mud and wocd. le sleeps on a dirty mattress, and is watched over by his favourite beer-girl. The king is a great sufferer front gout and obesity,</p>
        <p>The lioyal Laundresses say Critchley's " STARCH GLOB is unequalled for impartintr a beautiful finish to starched linen, &amp;C., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr. —, Corn- wall. says :—" My wife will not finish our linen without your " Starch Gloss.'" From Mrs.—, Bradford "Dear sit . lam pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-called Starch Glazes and Polis'bes, but find none equal to yours." It should be used in every house. Sold at id., 3d., and 6d., by most starch sellers. If not in stock will procure it. T. CRITCBLICY. BlAckburn. Poaprietor.—Ad.</p>
        <p>TOY:TiI:AW'',II:3;4TZ</p>
        <p>THE PURCHASE OF RUNCORN WATERWORKS. THE FINAL STEP.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Runcorn Improvement Commissioners was held on Saturday morning in the Board-room, when there were present—Messrs. F. Wis wall (in the chair), R. Abel, W. Collier, T. Lea, D. Bisbrown, W. Hankinson, W. Leach, E. Imi- son, E. Wynne Humphreys, 11. J. Grice, J. Pritchard, W. Handley, and P. Speakman, J.P., C.C., Mr. F. A. Lake (law clerk), and Mr. E. Marshall (secretary), Mr. Lake stated that the resolutions for submission were that the seal of the Board be affixed to a convey- ance of the undertaking of the Ruiz mil, Weston, and Halton Waterworks Company to the Runcorn Improve- ment Commissioners, and to a temporary charge, under- taking to execute mortgages to secure the loan of £69,000, to be given to the Manchester Trust, Limited, and that the Secretary be authorized to sign on behalf of the Commissioners the provisional certificates for the issue of mortgages to secure the loan mentioned. In answer to Mr. Collier, Mr. Lake stated that the ex- penses connected with the loan would not be increased by the amount being divided into small mortgages. The only thing it would entail would be a little extra labour to the officials in making out the dividend warrants. The Trust Company would have to pay the mortgage stamps, &amp;c. Of course those•who took up the mortgages would receive certificates showing that they had an interest in the property of the Runcorn Commissioners. Mr. Humphreys remarked that when the Board had once paid stamp duty on the mortgageits liability would cease. The number of documents would make no difference. In answer to further questions, Mr. Lake said the stock could be sold in the open market like any other. Mr. Pritchard, in moving the adoption of the resolu- tions, referred to the criticisms that had been made by the members of the Board, as well as by outsiders. He stated that the Commissioners were not in any way liable to any further expense than if the mortgage had I been made in one amount. If the money were obtained in small sums, then all the officials would be required to do was to make out a few dividend warrants. The whole subject had beerea matter of deep concern to the com- mittee, who had bestowed much time and labour in considering the best mode to obtain the figure required. There were three ways of dealing with it. One was by annuities, another by the issue of stock, and a third by mortgage. Those various ways had to be considered by the committee, and in order to secure their object with greater success, they sought the advice of accountants and financial experts, not only of those in the district, but one or two London gentlemen. He added that not one of those consulted were of opinion that the Board could get the am•tunt at the rate of interest they had succeeded in doing. Further an eminent official had re- marked with reference to the favourable terms they had secured, that he quite expected they would have had to pay a higher rate of interest. Some critics mid that the Commissioners might have undertaken to issue the loan Ithemselves. That point had been carefully considered by the committee, and they were advised that on account of the size of the town, and in a measure its being unknown to the financial world, that it would be very unwise for them to attempt it, because in all probability they would fail to secure a requisite sum. It would have been ex- ceedingly awkward if that day they had only been able to tell the Board that they had not secured the full amount wanted, instead of having the whole figure at their command. Bad they failed they would have been at the mercy of the mortgagee and financial agents,who would probably have dictated their own terms. With respect to the period for which they had borrowed the money, and to which objection had been taken by some, he thought on careful consideration it would be seen to be obvious that the 30 years' system was advantageous to the ratepayers, inasmuch is they had to set aside one- sixtieth part annually for a sinking fund, so that in 30 years one half of the mortgage would have been accumu- lated, instead of making the ratepayers liable for the fall amount for 60 years. Mr. Humphreys seconded the proposition. Mr. Collier said that this argument for borrowing the ' sum for 50 years had been that then money might be cheaper. But at the end of that time they would not have the same security to offer for loans. The works would be worse after the wear and tear. Mr. Humphreys assumed that in the ordinary course ot events they would have doubled the security, for they would have provided at the end of 30 years a sinking fund towards the repayment of the money, and they would have to borrow one half of the amount on the same undertaking. Mr. Uric° did not wish to criticise the action of the committee, but at the same time he must say that they had had very little information put before the members. Be perfectly favourable to the purchasing of the water- works, and was satisfied with the way the money had been raised. The outside public, however, when they were informed as to how the amount would be obtained, were of opinion that the whole sum would have been ad- vanced in one figure, and they would experience little trouble with it. Now they found that the Manchester Trust had simply acted as negotiators, and the Board were acting as bondsmen to the public. The officials would have trouble in making the mortgages and cheques. The Commissioners were not aware of this, and if they had had the information placed before them, they would have been better satisfied than they were. He had asked how the negotiations were going on, but could not get an answer to his question. Mr. Pritchard, in reply stated that immediately after the arrangement for obtaining the money had been made with the Manchester Trust, Limited, the bank rate increased, and money became dearer, and he was informed by one of the firm that if the understanding had not been entered into, they would not have offered the sum at so reasonable a rate, and even then if the Commissioners wished to be released from their desire should be granted. As honourable gentlemen they had kept their part of the eneagement, and were there that day to hand over the £69,000, so that the transfer of the waterworks undertaking might be completed. The advantages associated with the loan were decidedly favourable to the Commissioners. The motion was then put to the meeting, and all voted for it with the exception of Mr, Collier, who opposed It. • Mr. Handley asked if it was not necessary in such a matter as the one in question that two-thirds of the Board should be favourable to it. He drew attention to ,the fact that only eleven members had voted for the proposition. The Law Clerk did not consider it incumbent for two- thirds of the Board to make the resolutions legally bind- ing. Mr. Handley wished it to be understood that he had drawn attention t the matter. Mr. Humphreys then proposed that a cheque for £67,500 be passed in favour of the Runcorn, Weston, and Halton ss aterworks Company, for the purchase of the undertaking ; one for £862 10s. for the Manchester Trust Limited, for the payment of the commission due to them ; one for £197 17s. 3d. to Messrs. Addleshaw and Warburton, in payment of the law charges and out of pocket expenses ; and one for £337 10s. for Mr. Charles E. Baker, for the stamp du, y payable on the convey- lance ; the total being £68,897 17s. 3d. Mr. Imisen seconded the motion, which was carried, only Mr. Collier voting against it. The Chairman congratulated the town upon the very satisfactory termination of the purchase of the water- works. Immediately after the meeting the interested parties met at Parr's Bank to complete rna,ttere. There were present—Mr.Guthrie, managing director of the Manches- ter Trust ; Mr. Brelsford, secretary of the Trust ; Mr. Warburton. of the firm of Addleshaw and Warburton, solicitors, Manchester ; Messrs. Pritchard, P. Speakman E. Wynne-Humphreys, F. A. Lake, and E. Marshall, representing the Improvement Commissioners ; Messrs. Brundrit, Hays, Simpson, and Ashton, for the Runcorn, Weston, and Halton Waterworks Company. The docu- ments duly sealed and the deeds were handed over, and the money paid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>"COME BACK FROXITHg VERYIAWS</head>
        <p>"COME BACK FROXITHg VERYIAWS</p>
        <p>OF DEATH."</p>
        <p>From the "Irish Weekly Independent," Aug. 26, 1893. For an up-to-date genuine miracle (says a" Weekly Independent" reporter) commend Me the case of Mr. John B. Dorney, of 48, Middle Abbey Street, in this city. Dorney has come back from the very jaws of death ! as his story will convince the reader. Hearing of the recovery of the man whose demise some of the principal doctors proclaimed a few months ago to be within measurable distance, I called the other morning at 46, Middle Abbey Street, where Mr. Dorney carries on the business of a merchant tailor. He is a smart little fellow of about 33 years, and was at the time of my call working away in the best of health and spirits. When I announced that the object of my visit was to interview him, he left down his work and readily consented to answer any questions I might put to him. For you see," said he, " lam perfectly well now, but was a living corpse for many months, and having been saved by a few pills, I consider it but right to let sufferers know what remedy will cure them of disease." "Quite right, Mr. Dorney. I heard of your cure over town from student friends and others, and I think your case would be well worth chronicling. You suffer t d from rheumatics, dropsical swelling, and paralysis, did you net 2" " Mose were my symptoms." "First tell me how you became affected, and how far back your attack dates." " It started with Influenza about four years ago in the winter months. I was very bad and hardly able to work during, the time, Three months later Rheuma- tism set in, and attacked ney limbs and joints. That was in the month of March. For twelve months I suffered the most dreadful pain, and no remedy seemed to be fit to cure me. Sometimes when a lull would come</p>
        <p>I could do a little work, but for most of the time I was compelled to remain idle. But that attack was not the worst. Dropsy came along and swelled me up like a football. I could not walk. I could not stoop. I had to be dressed and undressed."</p>
        <p>" That was bad. How did your flesh look ? " " It was of the colour and apparently the consistency of lard. You might almost have lifted it in rolls, and there was no sensation in it."</p>
        <p>" He was like a dumpling," remarked Mrs. Dorney, "and we had to do everything for him. You may imagine bow had he was when I tell you that I had almost ordered mourning dresses for his death. He was given over by everyone." "Yes," continued Mr. Dorney, " weak action of the heart set in, and a large lump grew in my stomach, which rejected almost everything I ate, I was under the care of several doctors, who said they could do nothing for me. They gave me up as a bad job. In January, 1892, I went to St. Vincent's Hospital, where I remained for five weeks; but, as nothing could be done for me, I came out again, wishing to die at home, and I sat at home absolutely helpless, waiting for death."</p>
        <p>Here Mrs. Doraey observed that each morning she awakened expecting to find her husband dead in bed. "But he has not died, you see," I remarked. "He is as good as twenty dead men. How did you come to be cured ?"</p>
        <p>"I had given up the patent medicines long since in despair when I was recommended Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. I refused to get them at first. But after a while I consented and procured a box. That was at Whitsuntide. They acted like magic, a 4 they say. When I bad taken three or four I began to improve, and before I had finished the box I was practically all right." • "And when did you become so well as you are now ?"</p>
        <p>"Six weeks ago. When I was bad I could not boar anyone to touch the under parts of my legs, but now I could let you do anything with them. lam now as strong as ever I was-" I "You certainly seem strong enough. Tell me your I sensations during the progress of your recovery." " I felt ' pins and needles' all over my body. The lump in my stomach gradually disappeared, and the dropsical swelling became reduced and disappeared also. I am now hard and wiry, and can walk long distances and stand a good deal of fatigue. I forgot to tell you that the doctors told me I had Kidney Disease with my other ailments." " I noticed the improvement," said Mrs. Dorney. " His face and ears had been of a greeny-yellow colour, and when he began to take the pills the natural colour came back to him. I have turned the pill box into a needle box, as a souvenir of my husband's return to life." As Mr. Dorney had some bnsiness to look after I took my leave. When I left the house I spoke to several neighbours, who fully substantiated Mr. Dorney's statements as regarded the duration and magnitude of the disease. After the foregoing there can be no reasonable doubt that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People are a posi- tive and unfailing cure for all diseases arising from an impoverished state of the Islood, such as Anremia, Pale and Sallow Complexion, Green Sickness, General Muscu- lar Weakness, Depression of Spirits, Loss of Appetite, Palpitation of the Heart, Shortness of Breath, Pain in the Back,Nervous Headache, Dizziness, Loss of Memory, Early Decay, all forms of female Weakness, Hysteria, Paralysis, Locomoter Ataxy, Rheumatism and Sciatica ; also of all diseases arising from Vitiated Humours in the Blood, which cause Scrofula, Rickets, Hip-joint Diseases, Chronic Erysipelas, Consumption of the Bowels and Lungs, and that they invigorate the Blood and System when broken down by overwork, worry, diseases, ex- cesses and indiscretion of living. These Pills are not a Purgative Medicine. They contain nothing that could injure the most delicate system. They act directly on the blood, supplying to the latter its life-giving qualities, by assisting it to absorb oxygen, that.great supporter of all organic life.ln this way, the blood, becoming " built up," and being supplied with its lacking constituents,be- comes rich and red,nourishes the various organs, stimu- lating them to activity in the performance of their functions, and thus eliminating diseases from the</p>
        <p>• system. These Pills are manufactured by the Dr. Williams' Medicine Comany, of 46. Holborn Viaduct, London. England (and of Brockville, Ontario, Canada, and Schenectady, New York), and are sold only in boxes bearing the firm's trade mark and wrapper at 2s. 9d. a box, or six boxes for 13a. 9d. Pamphlet free by post on application. Bear in mind that Dr. Williams' rink Pills for Pale People are never sold in bulk, or by the dozen or hundred, and any dealer who offers substitutes in this form is trying to defraud you, and should be avoided.</p>
        <p>Dr. Williams Pink Pills may be had of all chemists, or direct by post from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company from the above address. The price at which these pills are sold makes a course of treatment comparatively inexpensive as compared with othcr temedies or medical treatment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>ST. HELENS FIRE BRIGADE.</head>
        <p>THE ST. HELENS FIRE BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>STRANGE ACTION OF A TOWN COUNCILLOR. The fire brigade sub-committee of the St. Helens Corporation have had under consideration Superin- tendent Rutherfoot's report of several fires which occurred during the month in St. Helens. They found that generally speaking the MEW) of turning out would seem to have been good, and the be- haviour of the brigade satisfactory ; that in taking the engine to the fire at Kurtz' s chemical works the horse became unmanageable and beyond the control of the driver ; that at the fire at Messrs. Pilkinoton's the management of the fire and the behaviour of the mem- bers af the brigade (with one exception) seemed to have been satisfactory, but that they were much hampered by the action of the crowd by whom several appliances were injured ; that on one occasion a member of the Council desired to personally drive the engine to the fire, and mounted the box for that purpose. The Committee recommend that a younger driver be appointri, and that it is not desirable that any member of the Town Council should interfere in the discharge of their duties by officials and servants of the Corporation. The Watch Committee concur in the transfer of the management of the fire brigade to the Watch Com- mittee.</p>
