      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE IN RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE IN RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Runcorn School Board on Tuesday, Mr. Moss presented the report of the school Attendance Committee, for the year eudiAg September 29th, 1893. The report stated that" The following Statistics show the work done under the direction of this Committee during the past year, also the state of the attendance at the Public Elementary Schools, as compared with tha preceding year :—Warning notices served by visi- tors, 3,948 warning notices served by visitors preced- ing year, 3,577 ; increase, 371. Parente before Commit- tee, 480; parents before Committee preceding year, 691; decrease, 211. Prosecutions before magistrates, 128; prosecutions before magistrates preceding year, 176 ; decrease, 48. Average number on rolls, 3,696 ; average number on rolls preceding year, 3,611 ' • increase, 85. Average attendance,3,o3B ; average attendance preceding year,2,959 ; increase,Bo. Percentage of attendance, 82.2; percentage of attenoance preceding year,Bl 9; increase '3. Owing to the epidemic which prevailed during part or the year the Committee considered it desirable to sus- pend for a time the meetings to which parents are I summoned, and at which prosecutions are instituted, hence the reduced figures under these heads, but during the Fame period the visitors, being relieved from much office work,were enabled to visit parent i more frequently as is shown by the increased number of waroing notices served. It is satisfactory to find that the regularity of attendance has not suffered, but is a shade better than it was last year. Allowing for Canal boat children the percentage for Runcorn is 83, which compares favour- ably with other town. The percentage for boys is 86 6 girls 79, and infanta 80'3. It might have been expected that with the completion of the Manchester Ship Canal, and the consequent departure of an army of workman, the numbers of children on the books of the school.; would have shown considerable diminution. Instead of that the annual increase continues, and there is a want BLECTRICTY WILL CURB YOU. of school accommodation for girls, as the Board is already aware. No children have been sent to Industrial AU sufferers should know that Illectrieity Is now recognised schools during the past year." as a valuable curative agent in all forms of disease. llmaxv.ss's BLECTItoP.THIC BELT has proved to thousands an unfailing remedy In Indigent on, Constipation. It eumfaism, Gout, Sciatica, Liver and Kidnev Complaints, Varimicele, " ToBACCONISTS aommeneing b " I • .raLedginile (neat free) Ladies' Ailments. nd Nervous Diseases. Send for consulta- TGACCuNISTS' OUTFITTING Co.-186, Huston 'cad, London I ti°n f°'M and a B s pamphlets post free. Write Mr. C: B 1866.—.Adrot. NESS, 25, Oxford-street, London, W. ~14ve. • . , • 4 ,</p>
        <p>/L.</p>
      </div>
