      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Mr. Mclntyre presided and there were present Colonel Fox (ex-officio), Rev. J. W. N. Tanner, Messrs. S. Taylor, T. Handley, D. H. Jones, T. Lea, S. Yarwood, E. Aaron, H. E. Heywood/ J. M. Williams, J. Weir, C. E. Davenport, C. T. Whitley, Priestner, C. Mounfield, William Priestner, W. W hitlow, J. L.-Jones, S. Woodward, J. Parkinson/ T. Lewis, E. Bratherton, E. Jackson, A. R. Norman; J. Ashton (clerk), and G. Ashton (deputy clerk).</p>
        <p>APPLICATION FOR INCREASE OF SALARY. A letter was read from Mr. Thomas Cooke relieving officer, Runcorn, asking for an increase of his salary. He said he had served them to the best of his ability since his appointment, 15 months ago, and that whilst having been ever watchful of the wants and comforts of the poor under his charge, he had alw4ys ben mindful of those whose interests he served. In addition to the liconsiderable saving during the first quarter he held the office, he hai with care and watchfulnesssaved a further sum of £.125 during the year ending Michaelmas.—The application was referred to a committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>SAMPLES.</head>
        <p>SAMPLES.</p>
        <p>, Mr. Woodward moved that the following Committee be appointed to deal with the question of samples to be placed before the contractors the next half year : Messrs. Williams, Heywood, Jones (Runcorn). Stokes, Yarwood. Lea, Woodward, Jackson and the Chairman. Carried.</p>
        <p>THE SCHOOL SEE QUESTION. Mr. J. Pritchard, hon. secretary to the managers of Doty Trinity day schools, Runcorn, wrote under date September 30th, stating : " Your letter of the 21st inst. has been considered by my managers, and I eat instructed to say that in my letter of the 18th June last, ou were informed of the arrangement existing in these schools with regard to free places,and which I now repeat namely,that the Infants' Department, and standard 1, in the boys and girls department are free. In so far as the 49 children referred to in your letter comply with these conditions, the managers will be glad to render your Board all the arsiatance in their power."—Mr. Williams suggested that children in the standards named might attend Holy Trinity.—Mr. Norman : That is only applic- able to those parents who are willing to send their chil- dren to Trinity School. If they insist in sending their children to another school—there is the difficulty.—Mr. Williams: I understand they are all Church or Wesleyan. —Mr. D 11. Jones : All the parents that come before us claim to send their children to their own school whether it be Church, Wesleyan or Catholic.—Mr. Lea: The Catholics are free.—The subject then dropped. Mr. Moss, clerk of Runcorn School Board, wrote as follows: I understand that our School Board visitor has been asked to attend at Dutton to morrow about school fees. I have told him to go, but it will be necessary to have the Board's permission if ha is required in future. Please advise me in time."—An amounted discus.ion took place with regard to compallng the inquiry officer to go the Workhouse, instead of allowing the Runc.)rn Guardians to consider the applications made for fees and to decide what fees should be allowed. The Run- corn Guardians held that this alteration of method showed a want of confidence in them. It was pointed out that the Runcorn township had to pay the fees.— Mr. Williams ultimately gave notice that at the next meeting he would move that the resolution passed on June 27th by which it was resolved that the inquiry officer for the Runcorn district be required to attend by the Runcorn Guardians at their ordinary rneetings,w hen applications were made for school fees, and lay before them the school fees application and report book and take the orders of the Guardian.; thereon be rescinded. On the motion of Mr. Williams it was resolved that the recommendation of the Runcorn Committee to pay fees amounting to EI 12s. 3d. be approved.</p>
        <p>THE APPOINTMENT OF A NURSE. AMUSING INCIDENTS.</p>
        <p>William Lea stated that there had been 13 applica- tions in answer to the advertisements for a married couple with a view of the wife being appointed nurse and the husband to assist in nursing and generally as might be required ; and for single women or widows without children depending upon them. A Committee had gone carefully through the applications and testi- monials and had reduced them to four—two married and two single. Miss Clara E. George, of Stoke-on-Trent was first called into the room, and at the outset imformod the Guardians that after looking round the placeshe was somewhat disappointed where she was now, she had an assistant, and there were also servants whereas at Dutton it was expected that the nurse should perform the whole of the work herself. She considered the advertisement misleading, it was not a nurse that they wanted, but a maid of all work. She therefore with- drew her application. Miss Mary A. Jones, of West Bromwich, was the other lady selected by the Committee and she after answering a number of questions expressed the opinion that she would not care much for the situation. The Workhouse was to far in the country and the neighbourhood some- what quiet. There was no one to see. A member ; We are here.—Miss Jones yes,but only once a fortnight. (Laughter.) This applicant also with- drew.</p>
        <p>—The application of Mr. George F. and Emily Batshelor of Exeter, a married couple was then considered the other married applieaot4 selected being unable to attend. Mr. Heywood moved and Mr. Wood ward seconded that the Committee be requested to again go through the testimonials of the other applicait..—Mr. Tay!, r pro- posed and Mr. Aaron seconded that Mr.and Mrs. Batchelcr be appointed to the position and this amendment was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>UNEMPLOYED AT ST. :HELENS.</head>
        <p>THE UNEMPLOYED AT ST. :HELENS.</p>
        <p>DEPUTATION TO THE BURIAL BOARD. , At the monthly meeting of the St. Helens Burial Board ,on Thursday, at wh,ch there were present Alderman Harrison (chairman), Councillors Martin and Mearns, Messrs. John Pennington, N.Lacey,and Nter M'Kinley, a deputation of the unemployed, consisting of five colliers, were admitted, and stated their object in coming. One said it was hard work knocking about the street and getting no support except one meal a day or one riseal a week out of subscriptions. Some of them had children. —Another said many of them had no place to sleep in, ' and what were they going to do ?—Another said he had a big family of six girls, and they were all at home. Be only got about a ticket a week, and would like to be employed. He had been in the mine thirty years, and understood the work of blasting and excavating rock. —Mr. Lacey :DI you speak for yourselves wily, or for others P—We Speak for many more who are unempleyed. —The Chairman : You want employment, hut thia Board ceases to exist in three or four weeks. We shall I be no longer a board, and the cemetery will be under the Corporation.—Mr. Wginley urged that work should be found during the three or four weeks. It was a matter of urgency, and would help some of them to tide over the hardest time.— The deputation having withdrawn, Mr. M'Kinley pro- posed that 200 or 300 men should he engaged to take out the rock from the cemetery and make the land easier for the gravediggers.—Mr. Me sync : That would cost £6oo.—Mr. MlCinley Better spend the money of the ratepayers in that way that way than in the emoloy- merit of police and military. The men are getting sullen, rod if they get into that state I don't know what they might do. An additional fifty or sixty men might be put on t,-) clear up the roads, so that tho cemetery could be handed over to the Corporation in thorough order. My opinion is that something will have to be done to find, food for the people or they will find it for them selves.— Mr. Mearns: Not unless they are incited.—Mr. M'Kinley: They are getting sullen.—Mr. Mearns : There are other people who feel the hard times. What about the poor , struggling shopkeepers ? If the Work is done rates will be put on them for years to corne. I should be glad to do something for the employed. but I take it that our duties close at once. There was no seconder of the pre- tolution, which fell through. It afterwards transpired that a cottage tit the ceme- tery needed repairing, and some odds and ends wanted doing, but Mr. Grant, the registrar said practical nu n ' would have to be engaged. This led Mr. DrK,atey to again press for work for the unemployed, and a lively discussion ensued. —Mr. Pennington said thereNvere shop keepers who were under expenses of £7 or £8 a week, and some days they did not take a sovoreign. The mat- ter then dropped. A meeting to wind up the hmiines3 of he board will be held on the 31st inst., after which the Burial foam will be absorbed by the Corporation.</p>
      </div>
