      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>lEontp.</head>
        <p>lEontp.</p>
        <p>T BB “EX.AMINER " STEAM PRINTING WORIiS</p>
        <p>EMMY STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>I IT.</p>
        <p>Zirprieg, aontettes, Ztr. _ _</p>
        <p>THE decision of the Miners The Coal Strike. Federation, come to at the end</p>
        <p>of last week, to allow work to be resumed wherever the employers offered the old rate of wages, has, so far, made little difference to those who may be termed outsiders. Work has been started at a fair number of pits, but in nearly every case these are the smaller concerns and their output of coal seems to be well-nigh absorbed in their respective localities. Owing to the exhaus- tion of those huge "stacks" of the "black diamond" which were piled up by smart and pro- vident coal-owners who knew what was coming, there is now even a greater scarcity of coal than there was two or three weeks ago, and prices have reached a truly famine height in many districts. One of the consequences is, of course, that the number of those who are thrown out of work by the lack of fuel is being daily augmented, and that the distress of those who kayo been unemployed for weeks is deepening. It is not surprising, there- fore, that, under these circumstances, the cry for a settlement of the dispute gets more and more ur- gent. In spite of this, however, it cannot be said that there is any prospect of an immediate settle- ment. At their meeting on Tuesday, the coal- owners re-affirmed their determination that there shall be a reduction of wages. The miners, on the ether hand,seem to be equallydetormined that their wages shall not be reduced. Such is the deadlock which is ruining trade over a large part of England. As time goes on, the public seem more and more disposed to range themselves on the side of the locked-out workmen. The dispute as to the real earnings of the miners has been pretty well threshed out, and, whatever may be said as to the possible earnings under specially favourable cir- cumstances, there is only too much evidence to show that the average wages of miners, even while paid at the rate that has prevailed during the last</p>
        <p>AW</p>
        <p>few years, have been the reverse of excessive. The employers on their side have had a good deal to say about the fall in the price of coal,but that is an argument which has lost a good deal of its force recently. The demand for coal has lately been far in excess of the supply,even though double, and more than double, the normal prices are readily paid. Starvation is, of course, the weapon which the masters rely upon for forcing the men into accep- tance of the proferred terms. But in the present instance the edge of that horrid instrument is being very considerably blunted by the active generosity of the public. Funds for the relief of the wives and children of the miners have been opened in many directions, and the daily contri- butions to these, taking them in the total, amount to a large sum. Some of the masters, we are told, complain that they have to fight not only their own workmen but the public as well. In that very fact they should find the most crushing condemna- tion of the position they have taken up.</p>
        <p>To all who are interested in the Ship T 91P i Canal—and practically everybody in Canall. this part of the country is interested one way or another in the great waterway—the announcement made by Sip Joirx HARWOOD at a meeting of the Manchester City Council on Wed- nesday, will come as a welcome piece of informa- tion. Sir Joun- HARWOOD took that opportunity to state publicly that the Directors of the Com- pany had come to the unanimous conclusion that the Canal would be ready for opening through its whole length from Eastharn to Manchester on next New Year's Day. That is to say,that within a period of about three months large ocean-going vessels may be seen passing along the Canal past Runcorn and Warrington,freighted with merchan- dise for Manchester warehouses. The announce... ment made by Sir JOHN- HAR WOOD is not qualified or conditioned in any way. It is well known that not a few predictions as to thecourso of events on the Canal have been falsified in one way or an- other, and,of course,it may be so in regard to this latest attempt to forestall the future. But on the other hand, it can hardly be doubted that the' Directors, warned by previous miscarriages and aware as they are of the extreme incon- venience which might be caused by a premature announcement, have allowed an ample margin for ' such unexpected delays as may occur. The opinions which are held as to the advanced state of the Canal are, as far as this locality is concerned, sup- ported by a report which we give this week of the work in the neighbourhood of Warrington. Owing to the miles of railway embankments that were to be erected, and the numerous bridges that were to be built, the section of the Canal in this district was one of the most com- plicated and expensive throughout the whole I length-. Yet we now find that this extensive programme of engineering works is rapidly approaching completion, so far at all events as it is necessary to complete it before vessels ca lt ven- ture to pass along. What remains to be done, will, to all appearances, be easily compassed by the end of the year. While upon the subject it may fairly be asked, what about the Warrington docks ? When the canal in its present form was I projected, and for a considerable time afterwards; ' glowing accounts were given of these docks and of the benefits they would confer upon the town. 'Their dimensions were calculated to a foot, and,. we all knew the number of sea-going vessels they would accommodate. That was about the time the first sod of the canal was cut ; in three months time the Canal will be open, and, yet, where are the docks ? Is Warrington again to be " left" The prospect, so far as it has been revealed, ismot one upon which those who are supposed to have the destinies of the town more immediately in their hands can be very heartily congratulated.</p>
        <p>WARR/NM:ON has been quite proli- Church "Affair." fic of late of incidents which, in their way, should be of interest to political and social reformers. A few months ago the relations that obtained between one 0 the large local firms of brewers and their tenants, as revealed in a court of law, evoked some severe language from one of Her Majesty's judges. To say the least that case did not strengthen the cause of the brewers when they take up the favourite cry of "confiscation." There is now what is known as "The Church Affair at Orford," which, in its way, is equally instructive. As every one knows,. when anything is said about tha disestablishment of the Church, or the slightest suggestion is made of touching the fat endowments which are enjoyed by the more fortunate portion of the clergy,. there is at once an outcry of " robbery " and 'spoliation,' and some very uncomplimentary things are said of the would-be meddlers with no interests of the clergy. Yet this affair at Orford shows once again how little is the regard that is paid to the welfare of the humbler clergy by the. governing powers within the Church. From what has been made public we gather that the Rev. Mr. PHILLIPS was appointed to the chaplaincy of Or- ford about nine years ago, and from that time to, within tha last two or three weeks he has laboured I assiduously in the district. That he has gained the approbation and the esteem of what may be called the "rank and file" of the parishioners is proved by the testimonials of which he has been the recipient. It is just possible, of course, that he may not have met with equal favour in the eyes of one or two local " magnates," but then,are we not constantly told that the Established Church is pre-eminently the Church of the poor? Apart from some such explanation as this, there appears to be no reason for throwing a hard- working and deserving clergyman out of his living for the mere sake of a "re-arrangement," which, we believe, is not desired by the bulk of the resi- dents in Orford, and which for any practical pur- pose might just as well have been made some years hence as at the present time. Churchmen can hardly complain, if, with such examples as these before them, ordinary people fail to see the enormity of interfering with the privileges of the bishops and the upper clergy.</p>
        <p>SUICIDE C'F A Sr. HELENS HOTEL-Kxuar .- RESULT OF A " NOTICE TO QUIT."'</p>
        <p>1 On Wednesday morning, William Peet, landlord of the Union Hotel, St. Helens, com:nitted suicide Iby hanging himself in a stable behind the house. His name was in the black list at the licensing ses- sions for being drunk, but the magistrates renewed the license. Messrs. Oreenall, Whitley, and. Co., the owners of the house, had, however, given him notice to leave. Deceased bad been addicted to drink, and WAS of a very excitable temperament. The notice to leave the premises appears to have , preyed on his mind, and he has since been very low spirited. He went to bed between six and seven o'clock on Tuesday night, and rose at five o'clock on Wednesday morning. His wife heard him walking about the house, and she got up at half-past six. He was not in the house then, but thinking he had gone out for a walk she took no notice of it. At ten minutes past Seven she had oc- casion to go into the coach house, and thers found. her husband hanging from a beam,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>PROGRESS NEAR WARRINGTON.. As our readers are no doubt well aware, it Woe fidently expected that the Ship Canal Will r opened at the commencement of next year. ° achieve this much desired consummation, the eote.,o pany are pushing on operations with the expedition, and and a visit was paid this week to the. section near Warrington by one of our reporters ute: notice what progress had been made lately work. Commencing at Morrisbrook, was noticed that at this point, the Canal could u' seen as it would be when it is opened, as it is hele filled with water until near the Latchford loclisti Needless to say, very few men are now emplcYek about this part of the canal. At the time the Welei here was in full swing, there was quite a villagee Morrisbrook,but now all that is changed. Alizes&amp;thL,"4 whole of the inhabitants have left the place, a the huts are almost entirely deserted. During u present week,some men were engaged pulling Wig down, and very shortly. the village of huts be a thing of the past. The Ship Canal Mission wh'ed used to be so well attended is also in a dilapidatehe condition. Almost every window is broken, tmt grass around has grown to a good height. It alo_L-0 seems an act of saerilegeto allow such a fine scren`,."o : structure to go to multi the way that this rnis,s-/31 is doing. In travelling towards Warrington Thelwall along the Canal,the next feature of inl,P,°,ld, ance is the locks which are practically eonitnet,""ad The lock gates are finished, and if the water To also been there, a visitor could almost have fane:ilea that he was in Liverpool. The sluices which parallel to the lock gates are however not flnis""ta as all waste soil which is excavated by the steitle navvies have to be taken this way up a steep '° and it requires the assistance of two and solnet'7lo three engines to push and pull the loads embankment. Near the locks is the hydraulic Pr'os sure house now completely finished, and furth,erme is the London and North Western railway the great height of which is very apparent at', owing to the temporary bridge over which wall-lane formerly crossed being taken up, an;liat'as canal cut to its full depth. There is still 710 number of men at work at this part, engage",se putting on the finishing touches and facing 40 sides of the cutting,but there is a less number b'rn, there were about two months ago when the.," of porary bridge was pulled up. Thelwall-lane pp course a " blind " lane now, and a notice is Itaas at the end, at which the bus stops that perT'a-vg intending to go to Thelwall must go linutsford-road, where they cross the ca';io by means of a swing bridge which is of a structure, and and which gives a good view of the c- ing both ways. Passing a section which is practically cocall 0 '4 we next come to the cantilever bridge,now oPe,T;rali passenger and vehicular traffic. It is a stiff C7or to get to the top of this bridge although on a, ca's day the view quite repays the trouble. it glva-a be good view of the cutting, and in the distance c"-- seen that portion which is filled with watet. osi will certainly be a point of vantage when the c,,adef • is complete, to watch the traffic which will go oft it. This bridge is the far end of Wash-lane after crossing the bridge, the road divides 'int° Lai one going to Lymm and the other to StoCt'ibe Heath, etc. A road also goes under it, ale,ng, tliS banks of the canal. Immediately after pass'es'sicY bridge it will be noticed that the pile driving W-tbs has been going on for some time to strengtherinor sides of the cutting, is now finished. The oty portion of any importance is what was once Via-jthe Step Bridge. This old bridge is now a thing °„'iace• ' past, and a ponderous swing bridge, takes its V 001 The bridge is completely demolished and two St soil navvies are hard at work, cutting through the The to make it the required depth for the canal foe barge lock is also finished with the excepticni ,C,)f tbs lock gates, and the junction with the branch '-" Old Quay Canal. a ger Another completed portion follows until W-rati to Tom Paine's Bridge, or rather the ten:Vole bridge which does service for that bridge. hingo hydraulic house here is finished, although t'aorse do not appear to have made much progress inr,'„, 0 time. It was near this bridge that on Mone7ol poor fellow was buried alive owing to aBO4 slip. The soil is of a very loose tse sandy nature just about here. Looking aCcessbich river, the trenches which are being cut and W will form the Warrington docks in the futurere t„sde be seen. There has been very little progress r.OO here during the past two mon ths,and if work ,det- pushed on with more energy, it will be a CO' so, able time before the Warrington docks are 01300" complished undertaking. A little further is the„d river diversion in which the dredger is hss,7' jag work. The bridge over the river and theABtroia bridge over the canal,over which the new roe° (1119 River-road to Moore will cross, are now practic finished, as also is the cutting all the way fr0,133,15t" lane near the Stag Inn to Acton Grange. At tcitigi ter place the work of excavation is in full e';d 00 and no fewer than four steam navvies are ha; the work tearing up the earth. The character Olargo ground here is very rocky, and necessitates I,?' ste4 amount of blasting. The scene is a very anlorr one, and the foot passengers have to keep ,5 ofer look out for locomotives which may be comingt dis the many sets of rails. When this portion cies tiV work is complete, one of the greatest obStaeo/A present remaining will have been rein° the Work is going on here day and night owiog.jgbl excellent illumination afforded by the electllCiaatrr, which has been placed here by Mr. Morton, e-caria' clan, Sankey-at. Prom here to Runcorn the is almost complete,and calls for no special con3;000 ' The nearest way from this portion of Olet'C!, which is under the L. and N.-W. railway bri OOP Warrington is by the way of the Old QuaY and across Walton Arches.</p>
        <p>BURIED ALIVE ON THE SHIP CANA,II,,.ca Mr. H. C. Yates held an inquest at the S".` (19.,?"- Hospital, Latchford, on Wednesday, on the John Sharp employed en the Ship Canal who vvre alive in some sand in the Ship Canal on Mon"„a',,ol9o-d following his employment. Mrs. Sharp said her" ,boe" ,-her was a labourer on the Ship Canal, and lived wi t' Mersey-street, Warrington. He went to NvOrif.nirig.s; thirty-five minutes past five on Monday me''-rairw" George Gale said that he was working at Toni t gor Bridge ~Walton, with thirty men under hirridaevasd day morning. They were under-pinning a terll;ecljP bridge,and deceased and another man were eflg3o,s- -trench six feet deep. The sand came down oe -",efesv,,,4l've buried Sharp. He (witness) ordered his men re.,e7,0 fet'd and the body was found quite dead. The sand 7,7sged„ja" fine.—George Davis, who was working with dee-Det"-,1 the tench, said the sand came down upon thera, d tli0; escaped. The deceased was nearer the fall of safjoer " he. He could not help Sharp. The sand cattle; ofpt top—Mr. William Fletcher said he was in char ~up section. The two men must have knocked away, which would cause the railing to give *ci. let the sand in.— A verdict of "Accidental cleat" returned.</p>
