        <p>, ST. MICHAEL'S CHURCH, RUNCORN. st;r4e harvest thanksgiving services were continued on S aY, when the preachers were the Rev. G. D. Wray, ' t Raitnn, and the Rqv. A. W. Oliver, M.A., of Bootle. • . ...a collections were made in aid of the curates' fund. Its, . WIDNES COUNTY COURT. Th3:llrige Collier, held a sitting of this court, yesterday. d t`re Were a considerable number of cases in which the (heti:tants swore that they were out of work or only ILII4IIY employed, and the Judge in each of these cases e•cliried to make the order. 43 It ./ILl'a PRITCHARD, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, High- ' 4nnora.—Adut. Oia y THE NEW POOR RATE FOR WESTON. tai„.., senday Mr. Collier, the assi,tant overseer, ob- ti "mil the signature of Messrs. J. Brundrit and James aadle ta , „Y to a new poor rate of Is 2d in the £, estimated ~ a"s. £707 153.20. There were so arrears on the last "-Year's rates. ST. AMBROSE CHURCH, WIDNES. i kill. e harvest festival was held on Sunday, when the Shaw preached in the morning, and the Rev. S. i Liverpool, in the evening. The Rev. F. Night- ao;;Ei livered an address to the scholars in the after- ' 'ihe collections amounted to £l7 153. The i 1 ST. Pent's CHURCH, WIDNES. ' kid uarvest thanksgiving services were continued on the ~,Y, when the Vicar, Rey. W. Hodgson preached in St. p, „ Ina, and the Rev. R. M. Linton, M.A., vicar of ties -In 8 l Birketthi ad, in the morning. In the afternoon . sett eiii, o , W. Stanton Jones delivered an address to the The collections realised £2l 7s. i Pete NEGLECT OF FAMILY AT RUNCORN. Betn,,,r.Thight, a young man was brought before Mr. Satinnt and Mr. Speakman at Runcorn Police Court on aedluaY, charged with neglecting to maintain his wife : tjzi 'our children.—Mr. Cooke the relieving officer, said e rn , priso,„ '4lB family had cost the Union £l7 153. The ci "er was sentenced to a month's hard labour. Ps4tg PRITCHARD's, 61. High-street. Runcorn, for the best ' 'UIsEA WATCH a worklugmau can have.—Adyt. WIDNES