        <p>At the ii!",c,II,,,TINcoRN COURSER AT LYTHAM. Which —0°837 Club (Lr ham) Coursing Meeting raennstic.:ll:4ckee:on wednesday, Mr. Joh.n BruiLdrit.'.6 Y oulid Brilliant Star, won two courses In the Nona defeating Mr. W. Paterson's. third toun'd and. Mr. Launde's Lost Chord 11. In the /tn. • Brilliant Star had to strike his colours to Mr. J. ?es Round head." In the South Lancashire kingtonr• Brundrit's " Bessie West" (Heated Mr. L. " Pulcherrima " in th,,, first round and went the " uer•Te Mr. Paterson's Prince Alberrneli "in second round.</p>
        <p>The WIDNES CHESS CLUE. MktelCanu‘,,al . tn. eeting wag held on Tuesday at Bedford lee —Ts, air G Warren in the chair. The hon. sec. sheet an an increase of membership, and the balance maAved a credit balance of £2. The officers were illa_ected as follows :—President, Alderman Gossage, ELT ; chairman, Mr. G. Warner ; hon. treasurer, Mr. , Sir. • Shores ; hon. sec. Mr. .1. Whitfield ; assistant sec. tan..A nunting. A capital list of fixtures have been ar- -140 for the coming season, including matches with InatittPeel third, North End second, Birkenhead Balfour tatgeudt.e) &amp;e. A club tournament is also being „ -terrili,4l ar- wafl of a CHASTE and ELEGANT WEDDING or 4.z PRESENT go to PRITCHARD'S, 51, High-st.--Advt. A BEGGING IMPOSTER AT RUNCORN. At, 13 ‘3413 Bell, a collier trom Wigan, was brought before Courtrandret and Mr. Speakman at Runcorn Police Cliur Saturday to answer a charge of begging in the ,cll-street. On the previous night a constable saw tlonvtisener and another man,both in a state of intoxica- .,theire;Ping into shops and begging for the colliers. and `c)Ot The prisoner carried a tin box with a floeaattached. On the constable approaching, one man !cl.hT„Pserl and the other admitted that he was begging lllto „-"Self and not for the strike fund and he was taken dsys•-1/3tcalY. The Bench sentenced the prisoner to 14 hard labour.</p>
        <p>Or; T OVERTAKEN BY THE LAW AT LAST. toles,.,daY, at Widnes Police Court, before Mr. birn' Thomas McLaughlin, of no fixed abode, • stievo -tight up under a warrant. charged with causing not bodily harm to S•imuel Hurst, of the Commer- : bight ;:elt Appleton, on November 19• h, 1892. On the Ltith a" que i stion, prisoner was in the hotel n company Inert named Bradley, when a row took place. -°l:lrevst fleeted both men, and when outside they e nil down, and it is alleged prisoner kicked him ' ollt .teh`t arm, breaking it. whilst Bradley kicked him 'tat" •-",e lower Dart of the body. Bradley. was Abge,meeol to six months' imprisonment, but prisoner —4ed. Prisoner was committed to the County rter &amp;Talons for trial. h TRANSFER OF LICENSES AT RUNCORN. klyo "oncorn Petty Sessions on Monday Messrs. H. Thomas and Thomas Rigby granted the IVraetistBf9eldrs4fAtile following licenses :—The Weaver Hotel, t091°.4 Point, from William Edward Orme to Peter Georec,. the George and Dragon, Egerton-street, from ' ItegX Nall to Georg, Shore ; the Shamrock Tavern, 811e,.:,•-qreet, from William Joinson to Samuel Henry ; the Crown Vaults. Heath-road, from John ' load etrt to George Hughes ; the Bird in Hand, Halton- , 'vrirri 4.°1:11 Charles T. Bridgman to William Baker; the ' Rich ea Tavern, Regent-street, from James Ward to Irernaurd Marshall ; th". Flettwood Arms, Church-street, ' kin '',artha Oatps to Alfred Chaplin ; the Millstone Inn, "Street, from William Fuller to William Skillicorn. ; sT;tto RUNCORN GENERAL DISTRICT RATE. .tig of an'urelaY, at Runcorn Town Hall, a special meet- the Commissioners was held,under the presidency 44414. Wiswall. With reference to writing off the • .geTlß:',lable arrears of the first instalment of the • aslie;ai District Rate Mr. Humphreys moved that the 4..24.01:11ent of the Hi'ghfield Tannery be reduced from d b.° 80, in consequence of the fire. The reduction ktitro,een allowed by the Assessment Committee.—Mr. • kr. "DrY seconded the proposition, which was carried.— irr°ll3ehreys stated with regard to wiping off the orhi"c(h)verable arrears that they amounted to £77 ss. ld., lea," was a considerable increase over the last half- th'eNi *•.;:.,hn they were £2B 9s. 7d. The rate being 2s. 10d. instead of 2s. 4d., there was a corresponding 41:4, eaae, for if the rate had been at the lower figure the aiderlat! would have been £6l. That, however, was con- Of e,.„taulY in excess, a crrcurnitance due to the number 100 -ttitY houses. The items numbered 154, and at least thr were for empty houses. The committee had gone Stit?gh the list, and it was feund that in the cases Mr. the,c)h.,n had not obtained the poor rate. He moved that wiPod off.—Mr. Bisbrown seconded, and it was I•tllleagnel—rai—'Nfr. Humphreys further moved that the District Rate be amended in the following Increased by the new assessments £7lB 55., thA 5 55. lOd. in which amount was the £l6O for lai'llghfield Tannery. The other items were small, the bnikest being £5. The. net increase of the rateable value half-year was £542 19'. 101.. making the present in'e%ble value of Runcorn £58,674 Bs.2d. The principal was due to new proverty.—Mr. Handley "fled tiiis prvcsition, which was approved.</p>
