      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <p>•</p>
        <p>DISOWNED BY HIS PARENTS.-A YOUNG THIEF AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN. At the Runcorn Police Court, on Thursday morning, before Messrs. J. Brundit and P. Speakman, Arthur Elson, aged 17 years, of Moulton, near Northwich, was charged with stealing a pair of men's boots and a silk handkerchief, the property of Frederick Myers, lock tenter, Weston Point, on Sunday.—P.C. Gregory, of Runcorn, stated that on charging the prisoner with the theft he said he was very hungry and he had to do it. He had been recently working at the salt works at Wes- ton Point.--Superintendent Atherton said the prisoner's parents had disowned him.—Be was sent to Kuutsford for 14 days' hard labour. PRIMITIVE METHODIST SUNDAY SCHOOL, RUNCORN. The service in connection with the opening of this new school were continued on Sunday, when the Rev. W. Albert, the pastor, preached morning and evening to large congregations. On Monday evening, a good audience assembled in the schoolroom to hear the rev. gentleman lecture on the labour pr„blem and the land question. The lecturer dwelt upon the evil caused by the formation of syndicates and advocated co-operation in labour as the most honest and beneficial method of work. He advocated state old age pensions, and sug- gested that a heavier tax should be placed upon beer and spirits, and the money devoted to purchasing land that was in the market, the land to be utilized for OA benefit of the people.—Mr. Reynolds occupied the chair. PRESENTATION TO THE REV. G. H. ICINeszoN, AT • WESTON. On Friday evening a meeting in the Weston SchooLfor the purpose of handing to the Rev. G. H. Kingston, who is leaving the district, a testimonial of the respect and esteem which he is held by the parishioners. The Vicar, the Rev. Canon A. Maitland Wood, presided over a well attended gathering. After a few remarks by the Chair- man, the present ition of a pocket communion service was made by Mr. Moores. He spoke in eulogistic terms lof the way in which Mr. Kingston had performed his 'duties during his stay in the parish, extending over four years. He had won the esteem and affection of the peo- -1 ple.—Mr. Sill also contributed his mead of praise ta the worth of their late curate.—Mr. Kingston thanked the donors for their kindness in thus showing a practical proof of their regard for him, and an acknowledgment of his services. THE FAILURE OF A WIDNES PROVISION DEALER. 1 At the Liverpool Bankruptcy Court, on Thursday, before Mr. Registrar Cooper, John Fitzpatrick, a Wid- nes provision dealer, was called for public examination, but did not appear.—Mr. Gittens, oflicial receiver, said that the receiving order was made in April last, but ow- ing to the illness of the bankrupt, and at his request, the public examination was adjourned. It had been adjourned several times since then owing to the contin- ued absence of the bankrupt, and for the last three months nothing had been heard of him at the bank- ruptcy offices, nor was his address known. For those reasons he had not been served with notice to attend the Court for examination that day.—The Registrar re- marked that the man was not likely ta be present un- less he had been served with notice, and further ad- journed the examination, WIDNES YOUTHS PAY DEAR FOR A FROLIC. At the Widnes Petty Sessions on Thursday before Mr. J. Brock and Lieutenant-Colonel Gasket!, William O'Keefe, Frederick Woolley, formerly of Simms Cross, but now of Ditton, John Kershaw, Hutchinson-street, and George Martin, Cnapel-street, were summoned for committing wilful damage to the extent of lls, to lamps belonging to the Widnes Corporation. Mr. G. Allen,who prosecuted, said eleven panes of glass were broken on the night of the 17th Auv,ust. He believed the lamps were broken in an evening's frolic. A great number have recently been broken, no fewer than 150 since 'August last, but the &amp;Unguents have hitherto escaped detection. Police-sergeant Carter said defendants had all admitted being concerned in breaking panes.—The Chairman (Mr. Brock) said defendants were all respect- ably connected, and had had a good education. No doubt they thought it was a lark, but if it was a lark for the defendants it was not for the ratepayers and for those living in the locality. It was a disgraceful way to start life, and he regretted he could not send them all to ' gaol, but if they came again before the court they would be severely punished if he was on the bench. Fined £1 4s. 9d. or sixteen days.</p>
        <p>' RUNCORN WESLEYAN QUARTERLY MEETING. The Wesleyan circuit quarterly meeting was held in Brunswick Wesleyan vestry, Runcorn. The Rev. T. Wilkes occupied the chair, and he was supported by his colleagues and a large number of members. It was re- ported that a reply of the Conference to the memorial presented respecting the publication of gambling news in the press was to the effect that under the heading of temperance affairs a resolution in harmony with the spirit of the memorial had been inserted in the minutes. The stewards' accounts showed an income - for the quarter of £196 12s. 11d., and an expenditure of £lB2 16s. Bd., leaving on the quarter a balance for the circuit of £l3 16s. 3d., which, after deducting a deficit from last quarter of £l2 4s. 7d., left a net balance of £1 11s. Bd. The members reported were 675, with 28 on trial. The Missionary Committee was re-appointed, The Rev. W. Arrowsmith and Mr. Edward Taylor were re-elected secretaries, and Mr. J. It. Frith treasurer. The 'recommendations of the sub-committee as to distinctive aggressive work being undertaken were endorsed, and each society was recommended to establish a tract society, if not previously in operation, and where prac- ticable, to establish a Sunday afternoon Bible class for men. Mr. A. R. Norman was thanked for his services as treasurer to the worn-out ministers and widows' fund. Be was reappointed. This was the whole of the business. RUNCORN DISTRICT NURSE FUND. The annual meeting was held at the Town Ilan, on Monday afternoon,under the presidency of the Vicar,the Rev. Canon Wood. In their report the committee stated that Nurse Webb had been re-called to Worcester,and in April Nurse Potter came from the South London Nursing Institution, and there was every reason to hope that her labour would strengthen the public opinion in favour of the work. The number of visits paid by the nurse was 4,316, as compared with 3,946 in the previous year, in- crease 370. There had been 149 medical and 82 surgical cases. The committee urged the supporters in no way to relax their efforts in pr: muting the collection of sub- scriptions. The statement of accounts showed a balance due to the treasurer of £8 lls.2d.,but the total indebted- ness of the committee was £25. The beef tea fund re- ceipts mere £l2 is. The food had been given to patients. The report and balance sheet were adopted, on the mo- tion of the Chairman,seconded by Mr. Lake. Mrs. Imi- son, Mrs. Fred Norman, and Miss Taylor were re-elected members of the committee. The other members of the committee are Mrs.Maitland Wood, Mrs.Lightburn, Mrs. Meadowcroft, Mrs. Joule, Mrs. Barker, Mrs. Speakman, Mrs. Fisher,Mrs. Preston,and Miss Andrews. Mr. S.Tay- lor was re-elected treasurer. The representatives of the friendly societies present expressed a wish that other or- ganisations wruld subscribe to the fund, which was de- serving of their support. It was stated that every club subscribing a guinea had the right to a representative. It was suggested that every collector of that sum should also have a vote. It was announced that the Football Club had promised a match. On Wednesday afternoon a football match was played on the ground of the ROE- corn Club in aid of the Nurse Fund. The sides were chosen from the publicans and tradesmen respectively, and a closely contested match resulted in a draw of one try each. There was a large crowd of spectators, and a good sum would be realised. SOCIETY OF CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR. A new feature was introduced into the meetings of the Young People's Society of Christian Endea- vour, held in connection with the Baptist Church, Golborne-St., on Tuesday evening, when the first quarterly "social was held. It was thought that the coal crisis, which seriously affects several of the members, would interfere with the event, but the misgiving was groundless as the gathering proved highly successful. In the absence of the President, Mr. John S. Green presided, and at intervals the following members contributed to the pleasure of the evening with recitations and vocal or instru- mental music:—Mrs. E. Lloyd Jones, Miss Jackson, Miss Pennington, Messrs. Wm. Woods, H. Buckley, D. Patrick, and A. McKenzie. A cordial vote of thanks was accorded to the Social Committee for the successful manner in which they had embraced their first opportunity of service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>„ , • , , THE EXA'MINEP, SATITIMAY. OCTOBER, 7. 1893.</head>
        <p>„ , • , , THE EXA'MINEP, SATITIMAY. OCTOBER, 7. 1893.</p>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>sljs'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</head>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>The School question in Runcorn was not ad- vanced one step by the application of the Guardians to the managers of Holy Trinity school. Their re- ply is practically, that they will be glad to receive scholars in those standards which are free. In the others fees must be paid .and on Tuesday last the Guardians passed an account of ss. for school fees at that school. The inquiry officer put in an ap- pearance at the Board meeting, but it was plain that his presence was unnecessary and Mr. Williams has given notice tha the resolution requiring his attendance be rescinded. Thus ends for the pre- sent this vexed question.</p>
        <p>Except at some public meeting at a tine of great political excitement, Runcorn Public Hall has never been so crowded as it was OH Thursday night on the occasion of the first Gilchrist lecture. It is estimated that there would be about 1,600 persons present. The lecturer was Sir Robert Ball, who discoursed of the wonders revealed by the telescope in a most eloquent and entertaining manner. By turns he had his perspiring audience convulsed with laughter at some humorous sally, then lost in amazement at the stupendous infinitude of the heavens and again full of admiration at the mar- vellous facts revealed by the astronomer by means of his lenses and his knowledge of mathematics. In every respect the commencement of the course was highly successful, and as the other lectures are equally interesting and instructive, there is no rea- son the hall should not be packed on each occasion. Considering so much interest displayed in scien- tific research it is surprising that the scheme of the Technical Institute still languishes for the compar- atively small sum of £1,200.</p>
        <p>One of the most important and momentous trans- actions that has ever been carried out by the Run- corn Board of Commissioners since it came into existence was brought to a conclusion on Saturday. On that day the Commissioners took over the un- dertaking of the Runcorn Waterworks Co, and in return handed a draft for £67.500 to the represen- tatives of the company. While some ratepayers may have objected to the price, there has been a consensus of opinion that the water supply ought to be under the control of the local authority. It is to be hoped that the result will justify the pur- chase.</p>
        <p>Widnes Corporation. and consequently the rate- payers have suffered greatly through damage done to street lamps in the outskirts of the town,hut the delinquents could never be discovered. A few weeks ago there was a more than ordinary destructive smash, and fortunately a constable was in the neighbourhood and able to identify the culprits, who were youths very respectably connected. Strenuous efforts, I believe, were made to get the matter compromised privately, but the Corporation Committee declined to make fish of one and fowl of another, and the affair was brought before the magistrates on Thursday. Perhaps there will now be some cessation of the lamp smashing " lark " as the boys no doubt consider it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>DISTRESS IN WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THE DISTRESS IN WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The Relief Committees met in the Town Hall on ' Wednesday afternoon. The Catering Committee,' under the presidency of Councillor Christie, passed accounts amounting to £ll9 78. It was decided to distribute 1,088 hotpots on Sunday next in the various wards. A suggestion was made that half a lb. of tea and 31bs. ot sugar should be given to each family on Sunday next, but on the Chairman pointing out tht t the cost would be £.60, the matter was allowed to drop. A further suggestion was made that coal should be distributed, but it was pointed out that the Commit- tee would not be able to obtain any. At a meeting of the General Committee, held afterwards Mr. H. Wade Deacon presiding, the proceedings of the Catering Com- mittee were approved.—Councillor F. Neil, Chairman of the Finance Committee said he had to report an increased expenditure and a decreased income. The expenditure last week was £3O more and the income £6O less than last week.—lt was pointed out by several members that the increased expenditure was due to an increase in the butchers and bakers bills owing to more hotpots being distributed each week.—Councillor Neil said he saw no chance of the expenditure decreasing and he suggested that another appeal for subscriptions should be made.— Councillor Christie, thought that if thE Committee became short of money the United Alkali Company would in- crease their subscription of £3O per week. After some further discussion, the subject of making another appeal was adjourned until next meeting.</p>
        <p>GILCHRIST LECTURES AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>SIR ROBERT BALL ON OTHER WORLDS..</p>
        <p>_ The first of a series of lectures under the Gil- christ Trust was delivered in Runcorn Public Hall on Thursday evening. The building was crowded to excess. The lecturer was Sir Robert Ball LL.D., • F.R.S., Lowndean, Professor of Astronomy in the University of Cambridge, and the title of his sub- ject was ' Other Worlds," The lecture was illus- trated by an oxyhydrogen lantern in charge of Mr. James W. Carbutt, of Armley, Leeds. The Vicar, the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, presided. and in opening the proceedings, he gave a brief and in- teresting sketch of the life of Dr. Gilchrist, the founder of the Gilchrist Trust. He was, he said, an extremely remarkable man. He was doctor, soldier, traveller, and author, and one familiar with the commercial world. Having such extremely wide experiences, being a man of great sympathy and philanthropy and a Scotchman, he naturally became at the same time an educationalist. He was one of the first founders of the Mechanics' Insti- tutes in the country, and a great supporter of the Gym- nastic Society, because he believed in educating the body as well as the mind ; he was one of the founders of the University of London, and in the earlier part of this century he was one who supported the great scheme for the education of all children in elementary schools, Perhaps the greatest proof that he could leave of his sincere love for education was that he left the larger portion of his property to form a fund, and the words of the will were these "For the benefit and advancement and propogation of education in every part of the world as far as circumstances will permit." The Gil- christ Trustee spent money partly in giving grants to in- stitutions, in founding scholarships in different parts of the world and partly in establishing tnese Gilchrist lec- tures. The trustees were not by any means of opinion that any great educational advantage would be derived directly from giving six lectures, and they hoped that these schools would always ,e made use of to lead up to something permanent. In Runcorn the permanent ob- ject was the Technical Institute. (Applause.) The Committee trusted that these lectures would stir suffi- cient interest amongst us to make the project of a Technical Institute a success. The Committee had reason to think the town was interested in such a matter because there were already classes going on in Art and Science and in other subjects, and there were now 110 male and 46 female students in attendance, making a total of 156. (Applause.) This was proof, therefore, that the town was not indifferent to technical education. For the last twe years the town had rated itself a penny in the £ in order that these objects might be promoted. It might be asked what advantage would there be in a Technical Institute over the technical classes which are already so successfully conducted. Simply this that the Institute would lift the matter from the footing of a school to the footing of a college. '1 he Institute would give performances and solidity to the work and above all it would make it more practical. ha the classes at present it was only the theory of chemistry which was being taught, but in the Tecenical Institute there would be a laboratory and practice would be taught. k Applause.) For this reason everybody who had the welfare of Runcorn at heart, was desirous of seeing the Institute established. A great deal had been already done through Mr. Wisw all's work and the work of his committee. It was estimated that the Technical Institute would cost £4,000; towards that the Science and Art Department had made a grant of £l,OOO ; the County Council £4OO ; and there were donations to the amount of £6OO, and savings had been made which were estimated up to the end of next year at £450. The sum of £3lO had been already spent in apparatus and the Committee were only £1,200 short. They certainly would not allow their effort and their money to be wasted, the rich men of the neighbourhood would not allow it to be wasted for the sake of £1,200. If the money had to be borrowed it would interfere with the income. It was always said that the difficulty would be to support the Institute when they had got it, and the income to carry it on would be about £430 a year ; if it were opened free from debt. He believed that it would be the public desire that the money should be found,and if these lectures gave a strong impulse to the scheme and the money was found, he believed it would satisfy the lecturers, it would saGsfy the trustees, and if the ori- ginal founder, Dr. Gilchrist, should know it would satisfy him. (Applause.) Sir Robert Ball, who was loudly applauded on rising, then gave his lecture which was followed with intense interest by the audience. At its conclusion he was accorded a vote of thanks on the motion of the chair- man.</p>
        <p>EMIGRATION.—Iatending emigrants to New York, Boston Philadelphia, San Francisco, Canaria, China, Japan, Aim trails, or New Zealand, should take ta „kets (lowest rates) at the " Examiner" Office, bv Guinn American. Australian Oriental, and other lines.—John Walker, agent,"lliamin.e.l Mee, Botweey-et.. Warrington.—dited.</p>
