      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>SELECTING THE CANDIDATES.</p>
        <p>Both parties have been busy during the week in selecting candidates, ad in making other prepara- tions for the coming contests. There is a wide- spread feeling in the town, shared by a large num- ber of Conservatives, that the present is a distinctly inopportune time for involving the town in all the turmoil and expense of a contested municipal election. It will be remembered that last year the Liberals, although they had an excellent opportun- ity of winning seats, seeing that there were six Conservatives retiring from the Council as compared with three Liberals, consented to a compromise, in view of the epidemic which then prevailed. On the present occasion when the position of the parties is exactly reversed so far as the number of retiring Councillors is concerned the Tories do not seem dis- posed to reciprocate, although the distress that un- fortunately prevails is seriously affecting every ward in the town, and shopkeepers and ratepayers in general are but ill able to bear the large expense which an all-round contest will throw upon the rates.</p>
        <p>-- While deprecating a contest under the circum- stances named the Liberals are not at all disposed to take a gloomy view of the prospects of the con- test If the Tories attack Liberal seats, Liberals will make an, equally vigorous attack on Tory seats and it is not at all unlikely that their opponents will find themselves in a worse plight at the end of the encounter than at the beginning.</p>
        <p>St. Austin's is one of the wards which the Tories are disposed to regard as a stronghold, and they have again adopted the retiring councillor,Mr. Ilep- herd as their candidate. There was a large and influential meeting of the Liberals of the ward on Thursday night, at which it was resolved that in the event of the Liberal seat being opposed by the Conservative party, it was decided to con- test St. Austin's Ward, and Mr. A. IL Crosfield was unanimously elected as the Liberal candidate. It may be remembered that, three years ago, Mr. Hep- herd only secured the seat by eleven votes against his opponent, Mr.Titchett. During the three years that have since elapsed, Mr. Hepherd has not dis- tinguished himself as a Town Councillor, and it is practically certain that Mr. Crosfield willl be able to wrest the seat from him in the event of a con- test.</p>
        <p>In Whitecross Ward the Conservatives have at length secured a reluctant candidate to oppose the return of Councillor 11. Roberts. Mr. R. T. Smith, after much pressing and more than one refusal on his part, has at length consented to stand up to be knocked down—in a purely figurative sense, of course. Mr. Roberts canvass of the ward is now almost completed, and in addition, of course, to the solid Liberal votes, he has secured many promises from Conservatives,who announce their determina- tion to support the" best man." A public meeting will be held next Thursday evening at which Mr. Roberts will give an account of his three years work in the Council, and a forecast of his future policy. It is not unlikely that he will invite his opnonent to a friendly debate on municipal affairs, and this if it takes place will, no doubt be an excelent opportunity for the electors " to sample " the two candidates.</p>
        <p>As yet we have not heard of any Tory aspirant to the honour of representing Orford ward. The retiring Liberal Councillor is Mr. Walker who does not seek re-election. At a meeting of the Liberal Committee of the ward, held last night, Mr. Walker was thanked for his past services and the com- mittee further decided to invite Mr. Wiliam Pierpoint, J.P., to contest the seat— if a contest be challenged. The re-appear- ance of Mr. Pierpoint on the Town Council will be welcomed by all the Liberals of the town, and by many besides who remember the good work he did there in former years.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Liberal Committee of Bewsey Ward was held on Wednesday and the retiring member for their ward, Councilllor Shaw Green, was again invited to the stand. Mr. Green thanked the Committee for their confidence in a short and pithy speech, and after (waling with much of the work of the past six years in which he had taken an active tart, re-, marked that in becoming their candidate he did so with a full sense of the responsibility attaching to the office. Mr. Green entered into some details with reference to the recent suggested appointment of a profesional auditor and surprised everyone by the statement that first suggestion of hisfltness for the offie came from members of the Conservative party. In becoming a member of the Council he said it was to him a personal sacrifice but he had a prompting within that it was a duty and so long as he was promoting the welfare and good govern- ment of the town he was willing to make the sacrifice.</p>
        <p>It is said that Mr. John Baird has been adopted as a candidate by the Conservatives, but it is quite possible that Mr. Baird will think " discretion the better part of valour" before the Ist of Novem- ber.</p>
        <p>Councillor Fleming will again stand for the Liberals in Town Hall Ward,and he will be opposed by Mr. Shillcock, landlord of the" Bay Horse.' Mr. Fleming's excellent services in the town council during the many years he has been a member may be relied upon to secure his re-election.</p>
        <p>In regard to Howley Ward it was stated in our columns last week that Councillor A.Browne would not seek re-election on the same ticket as three years ago. This was a printer's error which was inadvertently overlooked. Mr. Browne has been again invited to champion the Liberal cagse in the ward, and we understand that he will consent to do so, and it may be confidently expected that he will repeat his victory of 3 years ago. His Conservative opponent is to be Mr. Arthur Bennett. -</p>
        <p>Liberal candidates will probably be selected at meetinizg in tt e early part of next week for St Johns, Fairfield and Latchford wards; The Conser. cativo Candidates selected for these for these ward- are St. Johns, Councillor W. J. Shaw ; Latchfords Councillor Ashton; Fairfield, Mr. a J. Longton.</p>
        <p>A WARRINGTON LAD'S GOOD CONDUCT. In a letter received by Sergeant Chancier from Father Nugent's School, Liverpool, enclosing a photo of Joseph Leonardy, a Warrington lad, who was sent there for not attending school, a word of praise is given him for his good conduct. It ap- pears that a prize of 10s. is given for the best con- ducted boy in the school, and on a vote being taken from each boy in the scnool, Leonardy re- ceived 24 votes, and others six and eight.</p>
        <p>• --- - ALL HOUSEHOLD LINEN, hemmed and marked, free of aharge.—J. and W. DUTTON, elankey-st. —Advt. THE RAGGED SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>A harve-si:fes-ti-v-SlWris-hWd-i-n-tne, schoolroom on Sunday, the room having been tastefully decorated by Misses Monks and Hardiug,assisted by the other teachers. There was a good attendance at both afternoon and evening services. In the afternoon Mr. Willis. jun. (the rector's son), presided, and gave an excellent address to the children and parents and to the teachers and workers of the school, encouraging them to press on with the work in which they were engaged. In the even- ing Mr. Thomas Burton presided , and on Monday evening, a fruit banquet was held, when there were nearly 400 persons present. A miscellaneous con- cert was held, Mr. F. G. Owen presiding. At the close each child was supplied with fruit. The com- mittee also distributed over 100 cabbages, cauli- flowers, and other vegetables to the poor people present, the remainder being sold at a small price. NO REASONABLE OFFER REFUSED, If made at once at MARSH'S Furniture Warehouse, Bridge Foot, Warrington, —See fuller Adv., page 4.—Advt.</p>
        <p>BANKRUPTCY OF A RUNCORN TRADESMAN. At the Warrington Bankruptcy Court, yesterday, before Mr. Registrar Ridgway, John Owen, 20, Bridge-street. Runcorn, draper and smallware dealer, appeared to undergo his public examination. His total liabilities amounted to .£157 N. 7d., and his assets to .£l4B 9s. 7d. The bankrupt, in 1889 opened a shop for drapery and smallwares which his wife managed He never had any capital. He stated that his deficiency arose owing to his shop not having paid, and that trade had been bad in Runcorn and that the rates bad swallowed up all the small profits. He only became aware of his insolvency last June. Since then he had contracted a few debts, which he explained to be owing to a hope that trade would improve. The assistant official receiver said that he was of opinion that the bankruptcy was caused by misfortune, and not by any fault of the bankrupt. He also complimented him on the way in which his books had been kept since last April. The examination then closed.</p>
        <p>I 1,1:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>DISTRESS IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>DISTRESS IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE POSITION OF THE WORKS.</p>
        <p>The distress in Warrington has considerably in- creased since last week owing to further stoppages of men at the various works. Houghton's Sankey wire mills which have been at full work practically up to the end of last week, have at length followed in the wake of other large works,and this week the steel furnace and the forge have been completely stopped, and the rest of the works are going short time. This will throw a considerable number out of employment. Messrs.Monks,Hall and Co. ironworks are now at a complete standatill,even the mill which ' has been working during the portion of the coal strike,l eing now stopped. Whitecross Wire Works are also on short time in the wire department, and the forge is stopped altogether. THE SOUP KITCHENS. • Owing to the increase of the unemployed, the work at the soup kitchens has increased, and unless funds come in much more rapidly, this source Qf relief will have to be closed, when numerous families will have before them the alternative of starvation or applying for Union relief. The condition of the funds is so serious, that the follow- ing appeal has been issued by the executive of the relief committee :—Town Hall, Warrington, 3rd October,lB93. —Dear Sir,—lt is with the deepest concern and regret that the Executive are compelled to make a further urgent appeal for subscriptions. The number of cases have increased enormously during the past few days,and most of the large works have entirely stopped. Upwards of 4,000 persons are now absolutely dependent upon the efforts of the Relief Committee, and the sad condition of hundreds of families is most deplorable. The funds at present at the disposal of the Committee are barely suffi- cient to last another week. We therefore shall have to rely upon our friends and fellow-townsmen for their prompt and generous assistance, we are, dear sir, yours faithfully, Ed. GHEENALL, Mayor and hon. treasurer. F. MONKS Chairman and Executive. _ . .</p>
        <p>On Saturda.y in addition to 4,120 of bread, 905 two oz. packets of tea and 946 half lbs ot sugar supplied by Messrs awen, and Son, grocers, Sankey-street, were dis- tributed. No. 2 district has now given up Buckley-street as a place of dktribution and now carry on their opera- tions in the Drill Ball, a change which has worked more satisfactorily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RELIEVING OFFICERS EMPLOYED BY THE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>RELIEVING OFFICERS EMPLOYED BY THE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The committee on Friday decided to request the Guar- 1 dians to allow the relieving officers to assist in the distri- bution of the relief. On Wednesday, the Guardians unanimously granted permission, and the cfficers entered II upon their duties yesterday. It was said at the 11 meeting of Guardians that this would be a means of saving upwards of £3O per week. THE PRUDENTIAL INSURANCE COMPANY AND THE DISTRESS.</p>
        <p>During the pinch ot the present coal strike the Prudential Insurance Company have come to the assis- tance of its policy holders over the affected districts in a manner which is highly creditable to its management. The liberal treatment of the company's p•licy-holders will affect thousands of people in Warrington and its locality. The system adopted by the company is to pay claims on policies lapsed through the strike for a period 6f two months after its termination, and they will revive without evidence of health,all policies lapsed through the same cause, some months after the dispute has been settled. In many cases, where death has ensued during the continuance of the strike, and the policies have already lapsed owing to the prevailing poverty,the action isf the company in recognising and settling these claims must be very acceptabte to the holders of the policies. We understand that at the contusion of the strike, when necessarily thepeople who have been affected will be still suffering financially,the company intend to make liberal concessions of a very advantageous character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>SALE OF WORK IN AID OF THE FUNDS.</head>
        <p>A SALE OF WORK IN AID OF THE FUNDS.</p>
        <p>A children's bazaar in aid of the Distress Fund, and instituted by three little Warrington girls named Maw Perris, aged 11 ; Annie Ward, aged 10; and Marie Ran- dall, aged 9, was opened yesterday afternoon by Mr. Thomas Morris, F. G. S. The endeavour, as at first intended, was to be of a private character, but with the help of ceveral ladies it was thought desirable to make it public and accordingly the sale of work was held in the schoolroom belonging to the Particular Baptist denomina- tion, in Legh•street. There were four stalls, comprising the general stall, containing useful and fancy articles; the refreshment stall ; flower stall ; and a book stall. The ladies who had givenitheir assistance were Mrs. Perris, Mrs. Randall. Mrs. G. Ward, Miss Legge, the Misses Lawton, Mrs. Bonehill, and Mrs. Dill. Gifts of articles had been received from friends of the ladies in distant parts of the country. During the day Mr. Percy Randall, and his son and daughter, gave selections of music. As a result of the sale of work it is expected a considerable sum will be handed to the treasurer of the distress fund A word of praise is due to Mr. A. l'erris for his unstinted efforts to make the sale of work a stlecess. WEEKLY MEETING OF 'TEIE COMMITTEE. STRONG ArFEAL FOR FLINM</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of the general relief committee was held yesterday in the Council Chamber of the Town Hall, the Mayor (Alderman Edward Greenall) occupying the chain—Mr. F. Monks in giving the weekly statement said that the relief distri 'silted during the week had been 20,972 pints of soup and 24,220 lbs of bread, also 905 two ounces of tea and 946 half pounds of sugar. With reference to the experiment of the tea and sugar, they found that it had entailed £8 or £9 extra cost. Whether they would continue with the experiment, would depend on the meeting. The total amount of money received up to date was £725 Ele. and the amount expended was £528 13s. id., leaving a balance of £196 13s. lid., against £136 19s. gd. last week, being an increase of £6O for the week. The executive wished to point out the desirability of increasieg the weekly subscription list which, after all was the backbone nf the funds. The expenditure he was glad to say was less this . week to some extent owing to the extra expenditure of District 1, and the cost of this week's distribution WOUld be £145 ss. 9d. He wished to make a very strong appeal to his fellow , townsmen to support the relief fund, because it was quite evident that the strike was not going to end just yet, and as he had said before, relief would have to be given after the workmen got to work. Supposing the funds were to cease, he was quite certain that a very large number of people would be brought face to face with absolute starvation. He would impress this fact upon those of his fellow townsmen who either through thoughtlessness, indifference or absolute selfishness had not contributed to the fund. He wished to point out to them the seriousness of the position if the funds were not forthcoming. Be said it with all restraint and reserve that there were gentlemen in Warrington which had amassed large fortunes in the town wile had not contributed a single farthing to the funds. The Committee had received fetters and one in particular in which excuses were made because they • were finding, employment for their work people, but there were statements in that letter which he believed to he utterly untrue. He thought the time had come when they ought not to hesitate to call upon their townsmen to do their share, and not leave it to those who were , contributing from time to time, and having the whole of I the burden upon their shoulders. The rich men who had made large fortunes were the greatest culprits in this matter. The balance they Lad new in hand would last abeut ten days. He had hoped that the Committee could do more than give bread and soup as so many children would be absolutely barefoot; and he thought that if the funds were larger, and they could give something in addition to the bread, it would be greatly appreciated. (Hear hear). The Mayor as treasurer said that he must thank the gentlemen who had contributed so largely to the fund. He could corroborate Mr. Monks as to some of the rich men he mentioned. The Rev. Owen Watkins urged that pressure should t brought to bear on the matter of rent. He was afraid that otherwise many people would be houseless and homeless in a very short time. He mentioned a case in which a person had had notice to quit owing to being unible to pay the rent. The Revs. E. C. E. Carleton and J. Tongs also spoke in the same strain. Mr. Monks said that this had been before the Commit- tee,but they thought that it might have a bad effect upon those who could pay. The Rey. E. T. Reed moved, and the Rev. John Tongs seconded, the motion that the three secretaries of the districts Mr. Edelsten, and the Revs. Owen Watkins and W. H. Drummond be addcd to the Executive Commit- tee.—Carried. Rev. E. C. E. Carleton moved, and the Rev. J. Tongs seconded, that tea, sugar and bread be distributed to- night as was the case last week instead of bread and soup.—Carried. SANKEY BRIDGES. The work of relieving the people in distress at Sankey Bridges was continued during the present week. On Satur- day afternoon 150 men sat down to tea, after which the usual entertainment was given. The chairman was Mr. J. Evans, and the programme was as follows :—Song, " Safe in our Father's home," Mr. N. Cook, song, " Just before the battle ,:" Mr. C. Pritchard, recitation, " The bridge keeper's story," Miss A. Morrell ; comic song, " Down by the sea," Mr A.B.Bailey ; speech by Mr.Prin- cep ; song," The bright winning side," Mr. T. Woolley : reading, " The captain's gent,' Mr. Miles ; song, " Sally," Mr.E.Wilkes ; song, Mr.N.Cook. Mr.T.Brough was the accompanist. A vote of thanks to the chairman 11 proposed by Mr. J. Summersgill, seconded by Mr. J Corradine, concluded the meeting. On Tuesday, 430 children were provided with a tea. On Wednesday 190 women were similarly entertained. 430 children again sat down to tea on Thursday. Among the flits received during the present week have been 30 loaves from Mr. Stringer, baker, Green-street.</p>
        <p>RESIGNATION OF ST. HELENS OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Lightfoot, who has officiated as secretary to the St. Helens Young Men's Christian Associ tion for five years, has tended his resignation. Mr. T. Massy, police-court missionary for the St. Helens and Prescot district, has also resigned his post. He has received an appointment in connection with the Navvy Minion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
