        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>SWIMMING GALA AT WiItRINGTON</head>
        <p>SWIMMING GALA AT WiItRINGTON</p>
        <p>BATHS. A SCENE : JUDGE V. SPECTATORS.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding a prevalent idea, that, owing to a depression of trade,a poor attendance would turn up at the swimming gala on Thursday night, there was an audience as large as at the previous gala. Every available space, on which a spectator could find standing room was filled. Indeed many more 'persons who were so unfortunate as to appear at the entrance door a few moments late, were reluct- antly turned back, because there was not a vestige of a chance for them to squeeze in amongst that enthusiastic audience. Accommodetion necessary for the officials and others taking part in the gala, had been provided in an excellent manner, chiefly through the instrumentality of those two indefati- gable gentlemen Mr. S. Wilson, and 31r. T. G. Grounds (hon. secs.) The programme was a capi- tally arranged one, although perhaps a trifle too long. All through, however, the enjoyment which was derived from the various performances, never flagged, whilst to those interested in the" secrets of the art," the source of pleasure simply increased. There were many well known persons present amongst whom were the Mayo! and Mayoress (Alderman E. and Mrs. Greenall), Major and Mrs. Pickmere,Alderman Sutton, and Councillors Plump- ton, Muir, Heaton, Smethurst, Hill, Bolton, Dr. Cannel', and Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), and Airs. Whittle, Mrs. Joseph Harrison, Dr. Eisby,. Dr. Adams, Dr. and Mrs.. Peacocke, and Mr. T. Morris. The first event on the programme was a two lengths race for youths under 143 and after a good race of four heats the final was won by W. Ashton. Followini, this came a two lengths, handicap open to members of the W.S.C. There were four prizes given for this, one being presented by Mr. S. Xenyon. In the first heat there was a close run between J. B. Storey and U. r. Harding,tke former getting the prior claim, however. The second heat was won by Mr. Creighton, but at the start T. Locker scratch was favourite. He made a good. /hush and came in abreast of the other swimmers. The next heat was a splendid race Frank Turner being the scratch man and he also failed to take anything but the third place, his predecessors being W. Abhton (1) a very promising performer, and Critchley (2)• Then with the fourth heat there came another famous—shall we say veteran! —T. G. Grouinle—who could scarcely have been ex- pected to tike the necessary place, against thastart that was given his competitors. However, he made a bolder bid for the place than was expected and also afforded to thosewho were not acquainted with his excellent points as &amp;swimmer, an opportunity of " sizing " him up. The heat was won. by Owen and Clarq, who came in "a dead heal." Clare WOS objected to as not being a member of the club, but this objection was waived temporarily ' and referred to the Committee. The next heat was won by W. Walker, and strange to say, when the final came on Clare and Owen came in a dead heat again in the first place followed by Creighton (2), Walker (3), W. Ashton (4)</p>
        <p>THE " SCENE."-JUDOR V. SPECTATORS.</p>
        <p>'lt was in the final of the next event—an open two lengths handicap—that occurred a most regrettable in- cident. The race was confined to those who had not won a first prizethis year, but despite this stipulation there were entered in the ten heats many of the best swimmers of the leading clubs of Lancashire, as well as some of the best of our local swimmers. There were five prizes offered and there were 54- entries. The heats went off very smoothly and all went well until the final, in which there were six competitors J. Dixon, T. Locker, Gar- side, Moorehouse, Condor, and Langton. When the con- testants had ranged themselves on the wall ready to make the start it was found that Dixon was missing. Mr. G. H. Barker, hon. sec. to the N.C.A..5.A., who was the starter and judge announced that he shonld start without Dixon. Fair Turner hereupm brought word ' that the missing competitor would be ready in a moment but the judge proceeded to make the start, a perfectly justifiable action no doubt, but one which the audience took with a disfavour which they did not conceal. Half way down the last length Garside was leading, but Locker gained upon him and both touched the pipe at the finish together amid loud cries of " dead heat," " tie," ire. This being the case the audience were considerat ly surprised when the judge announced that Garside was fifth. That this; was a mistake was apparent to the whole audience, for the three men now put before Garside were obviously a yard in his rear when he touched the pipe. The audience cried for an alteration in the decision, but Mr. Barker seemed to be irritated by this interference and said he would not be interfered with as a judge, and spoke to the effect that until Warrington people knew how to treat judges they should not hold galas at their baths. The audience resented this rather passionate speech, and the whole place soon resounded with cries of disapprobation. When he got an opportunity Mr. Barker announced that the other two judges had both given in the same decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>POLO MATCH.</head>
        <p>THE POLO MATCH.</p>
        <p>The principal event of the evening was of course the final of the polo competition for the Championship Cup, presented by proprietors of the " Athletic News." The two teams were Osborne (the present holders) and Leaf- street. Much extra interest was lent to the match because of the appearance in the Osborne team of J. H. Tyers, the long distance amateur champion of England. Both teams played their full strength, Osborne being sanguine of success, whilst L af-street Club just as strongly fancied their chance. The following are the teams :—Osborne: W. R. Murray, goal; T. Coe and T. Harris, backs; W. Mills, half back; J. H. Hicks, J. IL Tyres and H. Silversides, forwards. Leaf-street : E. Moss, goal ; J. Goddard and M. Brown, backs; A. Smith, half back ; F. E. Calow, J. Brown and 11. Calow, for- wards. Referee : A. Thompson (N C A SA). Time- keeper: F. B. Edwards (N CAS A). Goal judges : .T. Molloy and H. Radcliffe. From start to finish toe play ot the Osborne team was far superior to that of their opponents, and all the players on the winning side were seen at their best. Moss, for Leaf-street, played a splendid game in goal, although beaten so many times, but the other players did not show the same dash as their opponents, the most conspicuons amongst the losers being H. Calow, M. Brown and A. Smith. The game resulted in Osborne scoring 9 goals to one. It was an exceedingly interesting game to the spectators, especially as polo is just now being popularized in the town. The last event was a four lengths open race. There were six heats. In this mei there was given a gold medal for the competitor making the fastest time. This was won by J. H. Tyers, 67 2-sth secs. He was closely pressed by W. Ellis. It was ten o'clock by the time the whole of the events had been decided. The prizes were presented to the winners by the Mayoress, Mrs. E.Greenall. The following are the officials to whom mach praise is due far the excellent manner in which the competitions were arranged and decided : Judges : Messrs. G. H. Barker, hon. sec. N.C.A.S.A. ; Roderick Williams, V.P., N.C.A.S.A. and H. Bolton. Handicap- per to open events : Mr. d. H. Barker. Handicappers to local events : Messrs. G. A. Barlow and Stanley Wilson. Starter : Mr. G. H. Barker. Check starter : Mr. H. Tot C. Referee : Mr. W. S. Haddock.</p>
        <p>Appended are the winners of the various events : Youths race, under sixteen, two lengths: 1, H. Ashton ; 2, E. Jewkes ; 3, H. J. Ashton. Two lengths' handicap (members of the Warrington Swimming Club) : J. Clare and T. Owen, dead heat; 2, W. Creighton ; 3, M. Walker; 4, W. Ashton. Maiden handicap (two lengths open) : 1, T Locker; 2, G. Longworth ; 3, H. noorhouse ; El Conder ;5. F. Garside. Four lengths open race : W. A. Reid (1), F. M. Whitehead (2), J. Molloy (3), W. Ellis (4), W. H. M. Myers, (5).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>L/ MM.</head>
        <p>L/ MM.</p>
        <p>Kim:Ns INSPIRITS.—There is in the possession of Mr. Levi Jackson, greengrocer, Lymm, four kittens, pre- served in spirits. All the animals are connected together, and present a strange appearance. They lived for a few days and were then drowned, and, as stated above, pre- served in spirits by Mr. Jackson. THE SUICIDE OF A WA [MOUTON TAILOR.—AO inquest was held on Friday afternoon, at Warburton, concerning the death of John Johnson, a Warburton tailor, the cir- cumstances of which have already been reported in the Examiner. Mr. H. C. Yates, county coroner, conducted the inquiry.—Evidence was given by deceased's brother, who stated that he was a tailor carrying on business at Warburton, and the deceased had assisted him in his work.—The man Davies gave evidence as to the finding of the body in a field behind the hedge.—Mrs. Barff, Saracen's Head, stated that the deceased had called at i her house for some whiskey on the day n question.— Mrs. Bell, at whose house the deceased had lodged, gave further evidence, and P.C. Bebbington spoke to dressing the man's throat and cohveying him to his lodgings.—A verdict of " Suicide whilst temporarily insane" was re- turned by the jury. Local. Boaap.—The monthly meeting of the Lymm Local Board was held at the Board's Offices on Tuesday evening, when there were present—Mr. J. Gregson (chairman), Messrs. Appleton, Perrin, Mclntyre, White- legge, Brazendale, Harrison, Holt, and E. flolt. Mr. Ridgway (law clerk), Mr. Mullard (clerk), Mr. J. Mort (surveyor), Mr. W. Severs (gas manager), and Mr. E. Rouse (fire engine captain).—A letter was read by the Law Clerk from the churchwardens of Sion Chapel, calling the Board's attention to the insufficient lighting of Cherry-lane, especially in front of the chapel. The attention of the Board was also called to the fact that lamps were put in front of all other chapels in the vil- lage. The Chairman said the Gas Committee had had the matter under consideration, and strongly recom- mended the erection of a lamp in front of the Sion Chapel.—Mr. Brazendale moved, Mr. Appleton seconded, and it was carried that the matter be attended to.—The Fire Brigade captain submitted his annual report, in which he stated the brigade had had 21 drills. The damage by fires had amounted to £4O, and the ex- penses of the Fire Brigade were £8 7s. 7d. The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>Mr. Alfred de Rothschild possesses a lion cub, which he has trained to follow him about his house and grounds. Its food consists of rice and boiled mutton, oaf which latter article it readily consumes upwards of 51bs. per day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The porter at the Workhouse gates has been get- ting under the displeasure of the officials in Lon- don. Mr. Moorson, inspector of the Local Govern- ment Board informed the master ef the Workhouse, that he had driven through in acarriagewithout be- ing challenged by anyone. The matter has not however troubled the local Guardians at all. Mr. Davies one of the members said that Mr. Curry knew the Inspsctor, and would not like to chal- lenge such an august personage. It seems to be a rather ridiculous complaint, as if the porter knew the Inspectors he would have no need to challenge him. He might as well challenge the chairman of the Guardians, and the Guardians very rightly treated it in that light.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Monks made a very earnest appeal yester- day, to those rich men who have amassed large fortunes in Warrington, but who have not given a single farthing to the relief fund, to come forward and aid in relieving the prevailing distress in the town. It appears that these men are the grerter culprits in not contributing to the funds. They ought to be rather ashamed of themselves when they hear that according to the Mayor, a large number of the most regular contributors have a comparatively small interest in the town. Surely the appeal of Mr. Monks will stir up these gentle- men trom their thoughtlessness; indifference or selfishness, or whatever may be the cause of their strange behaviour. It is satisfactory to know that their is .£6O more in hand than there was last week. Mr. Monks, however, wishes to provide shoes for the children who will soon be altogether baretooted and if this is to be done a much larger amount of of money will have to be forthcoming.</p>
        <p>Now that the relieving officers have come to the assistance of the commit eee, there will be no doubt a decrease in the imposition which has been prac- ticed on the committi-e, and it is satisfactory to know that even this week the officers began their work a notable decrease has occurred. The Rev. Owen Watkins' suggestion to prosecute anyone who i is caught imposing on the committee s a very good one, and if carried out will act as a deterent upon others. As to the idea of bringing pressure upon rent collectors and landlords not to be so strict in getting their rent, I have an opinion that the land- lords would rather have their own way in the mat- ter. Mr. Monks, however, told the tenants a way to be on equal terms with their landlords. He ad- vised them not to go out when told to do so, and he did not think that any magistrates would grant an eje. ctman t order it they knew it was a case of genuine distress.</p>
        <p>The members of the Town Council seemed to have entered into a conspiracy for the rapid des- patch of business on Tuesday evening. The Watch Committee had tosome extent cleared the way by the withdrawal of that injudicious resolution of theirs_ with regard to the alleged want of" courtesy "on the part of the licensing magistrates. The Watch Committee has not added to the lustre of its repu- tation over that business. It displayed a desire to frustrate the action of the magistrates but only succeeded in manifesting its own impotence. To vary a familiar phrase, we may say that it was wil- ling to wound, but unable to atnke. It has to I some extent retrieved its position by its " grate- ful " retirement from the scene by the withdrawal I of the lesolution.</p>
        <p>The improvements at Longford depots of wMch an accurate account was given in the " Examiner" last week was, as might expected, the topic of some conversation, but so txpiditious had the Council been in •' passing the minutes" that Coun- cillor Beaman, the Chairman the Sanitary Works Committee, had not had time t. get into his place to hear the complimentary things that were said, nor to answer the queqtions which some inquisi- tive councillors seemed disposed to put. MERLIN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>CURIOUS ADVENTURE:</head>
        <p>A CURIOUS ADVENTURE:</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a curious adyenture befel a delegate from Manchester to the Warrington branch of the Wiredrawers Society. About 7.45 he was proceeding up Cheshyres-lane for the purpose of crossing the Market Place to the Blue Lion. When opposite a pair of large gates which are situated in the wall•of the passage, two men pounced upon him and after blinding him for a time by throwing pepper into his eyes, they rifled his pockets of £2l 44., which he was taking to a meeting of the Branch at the Blue Lion. He groped his way to the market and was then taken to his destination. He was unable to give a description of the men, and the police, who were informed of the matter have up to the present procured no traces of the thieves.</p>
        <p>114 M</p>
        <p>-kV</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL SANITARY AUTHORITY. •</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL SANITARY AUTHORITY. •</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Authority was held yesterday at the Workhouse. There were present the Rev. G. F. Roberts (in the chair), Messrs. W. Long, W. Houghton, J. Houghton, A. Jolley, Smith, Mather, and S. Sutton (clerk), W. Owen (surveyor), and Dr. Sephton (medical officer).</p>
        <p>THE NEW DESTRUCTOR AT LONGFORD DEPOT. A letter was read from the Town Clerk, inviting either members of the Authority or their medical officer to inspect the new destructor at Longford Deaot. The Clerk mentioned that the Sanitary Works Committee had put up a new destructor which was one of the means by which they intended to remedy the nuisance which had been complained of. They had yet, however, to build the chimneys cmsiderahly higher than they now were, to complete the undertaking which they had given to the deputation which had waited upon them. A dis- cussion followed as to whether the Corporation meant to allow their undertaking to rest at erecting the new des- tructor, but upon the suggestion of the Chairman the matter was allowed to remain in abeyance for another month.</p>
        <p>ORFORD WATER SUPPLY. Another letter was read from the Town Clerk of War— rington, stating that the Water Committee regretted that they were unable to accept the terms submitted by the Rural Sanitary Authority for a supply of water to the township of Orford. The sum of £35 which the Committee asked the Authority 0 guarantee would mean an annual loss to the Committee until the water rates oc.'eed that amount.</p>
        <p>THE AUTHORITY AND TIIE UNEMPLOYED. The CLERK read a communication from the Local Government Board, asking the Authority if they could offer any work for the unemployed in their district.— Mr. A. JOLLEY suggested that the roads which it was intended to make in the neighbourhood of Liverpool- road should be commenced, and the ironworkers out of employment in the district put upon the work.—The CLERK said he could not see how they could put the men. to work of that character, for he considered that iron workers would not be of much service in roadmaking.— Mr. JOLLEY said that the Corporation of Warrington were employing the men to do similar work for them, and he did not see why that Authority striuld hesitate to do so.—Mr. W. LONG asked the opinion of the Sur- veyor upon the subject.—Mr. OWEN said they could employ the men at the work, but not satisfactorily or economically. The men employed by the Warrington Corporation were doing their work as well as they could ; they were most cell tinly doing their best, but then it would not pay. They were now in the same position in respect to expenditure in the making or new roads. The whole of the ratepayers of Warrington would have to contribute to the cost of the new roads made in the town. In respect to the new roads in the Autho- rity's district, the cost would be charged pro rata on the householders with property on the new roads, and they mild object to the charge if the work had not bem done ' with an eye to economy.—The CHAIRMAN NVell,is there no other kind of work we can get these men ?—Mr. SMITH said they were not a relief committee, and they had no right to interfere with disputes between masters and men.—The matter was then allowed to drop.</p>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT. Dr. Sephton reported that during the month there had been 36 births and 26 deaths in the district. The death rate was 23.3 per 1,000. He also reported that since his last attendance at the Board the smallpox epidemic had completely disappeared.</p>
        <p>THE W ARRINGTON Y.M.C.A. The varioias classesin connection with the Young liens Christian Association, at Friars Green House, may now be said to be in full swing. On Monday evening the Young Mens Christian Association singing class, and Mr. Browne's shorthand class co.nmenced. The Warrington Chess Club com- menced their season on Tuesday night, in their room at the Y.M.C.A., where they meet on Tuesday and Saturday nights. A tournament will be com- menced almost immediately, and it is hoped that a trophy will be offered for competition. The Sun- day school teachers preparation class was com- menced last night, when the Rev. E. T. Reed was the leader. The subject was "Redemption in, Christ."</p>
