      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>CHURCH AFFAIRS AT ORFORD. •</head>
        <p>CHURCH AFFAIRS AT ORFORD. •</p>
        <p>A petition addressed to the Bishop of the diocese has been largely signed by the parishioners of Orford, expressing their regret at the removal of their ex-chaplain, the Rev. I. Phillips, M.A., and desiring the Bishop to appoint him, if possible, to some church in the diocese. THR VICAR AND TITS NEW ARRANGEMENT. . .</p>
        <p>1 The completion of the arrangements whereby the parish of Orford becomes amalgamated with the district or Padgate was witnessed on Sunday morning, when the Rev. J. Sa Langham occupied the pulpit of the Orford Church, and preached from the w ards," For we preach not ourselves, but Christ Jesus our Lord." 'ln conclud- ing his sermon, the rev, gentleman remarked that he declined to enter into the circumstances leading to .the chang. because with that he had personally no concern, but when the trustees determined that the change should take place it became necessary that he should decide what was to be done. On that point he ,sought the advice of the Bishop. lie impressed on the Bishop his (the Vicar's) willingness to have Orford cut off and have it constituted a separate parish, or that it should be worked with a curate as part of the parish of Padgate. The Bishop was of opinion that the population of Orford was not large enough to constitute a separate parish, and he decided on the latter alternative. He asked them to chide by the Bishop's decision, and for their sympathy and help, and to continue their good work in the future as in the past for Jesus Christ's sake.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>SOCIAL OATFIERING.</head>
        <p>SOCIAL OATFIERING.</p>
        <p>Asocial gathering took place in the school-room at Orford last evening week, when the inhabitants of the district assembled to bid farewell to their chaplain and his wife, Mr. and Mrs. Phillips, who have been nine years at Orford, and are leaving under circumstances already reported. A selection of glees was effectively rendered by the members of Mrs. Phillips' various glee parties. Messrs. Walter and William Povey and William, Richardson gave their popular trios, and Miss Grounds, I Miss M. Richardson, and Mi. Charles Grounds rendered solos. Tea, coffee, and other refreshments, were sup- ' plied, this department being in the hands of Mr. C. Grounds, who was assisted by the Misses Pollitt, Wool- ner, Grounds and Mather. Towards the close of the evening Mr. Phillips alluded in grateful terms to the good work done by many kind helpers in the Sunday school and parish from time totime.—Mr. Pllitt alluded to the work done amongst the young people of Orford by Mrs. Phillips, which had endeared her to all. He as- sured Mr. Phillips of the great grief of the people of Or- ford at parting with himself an/ his wife, and of their 'gratitude for all they had done for them. On behalf of the 55 subscribers, which, he explained, was a large pro- Portion of the householders of Orford, he handed the purse to Mrs. Phillips, with every good wish and many sorrowful regrets. The purse was of Russian leather, with silver mountings, and contained £22 10s. in gold. The gift was accompanied by a list of the subscriber's names.—Mr. Phillips, to whom, as well as to his wife, I the presentation had come as a complete surprise, was visibly affected. He assured them that the gift gave him the deepest pletsure.—Mrs. Phillips in a very few words expressed her grateful thanks, and said it was a..real sorrow to her to leave Orford, where she had received so much kindness. There were about 150 persons present. Mrs. Beamont wrote that she was unable to, attend through indisposition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ST. HELENS CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH.</p>
        <p>THE NEW PASTOR.</p>
        <p>- The pulpit of the St. Helens Congregational Church, which has been vacant for fifteen months, will shortly be occupied Sy the Rev. J. A. Mitchell, 8.A., of Friar-lane Chapel, Nottingham, whose acceptance of the unanimous call gent to him was made known at St. Helens on Wednesday night: Mr. Mitchell will probably commence his pastorate at St. Helens with the new year. A retrospect of Congrega- tionalism in St. Helens may beef interest in connection with tho new appointment. In 1710 the first Congrega- tional Chapel was ejected in St. Helens on the spot where the present chapel now stands. The seceders bad separated from their brethren,and their first minister was the Rev. Joseph Gellibrand, wbo remained for thirty years. He was succeeded in 1740 by the Rev Jonathan Mercer, ciho had the pastor- ate for 27 years. The ttev. Samuel Bray: brook, seven years, and the' "Ifev..--baire• forty-six years, brings us to the erection of the new chapel in 1826. The Rey. William Vint was succeeded by the,Rev. John Edmonds, and in 1860 the Rev. J. 4. Macfadyen (afterwards 'Doctor) was appointed. After three years service he removed to Manchester, and was succeeded by the Rev. R. J. Ward, who in June 1892, resigned his pastorate, and in November went out as a missionary to India. Since that time supplies have been obtained for the pulpit, and there has been considerable anxiety regarding the appointment of a successor. About six weeks since the Rev. J. A. Mitchell B.A. ,came as a supply, and the impression be made upon the church and congregation was so marked that a special meeting was held. It was then unanimously decided to invite Me. Mitchell to become the pastor, and on Tuee- day a letter was received from the rev. gentleman stat- ing that he regarded it as the plain path of duty, arid had decided to accept the invitation. The decision wee received with much pleasure, and a kosolution was passed acknowledging the communication and the satis- faction it had given. The salary -is £6oo;and is raised by endowments and pew rents.</p>
        <p>The late Duke of Saxe.Coburg had a pretty wit. It is well known that he very liberally conferred the Ernestine Order on musicians, and it is said that after a-certain operatic performance some years ago he conferred it upon the prompter, sard)nically remarking that this useful individual had done quite as much work as the leading tenor.</p>
        <p>The Medical Annual advisee Practitioners to remember that when recommending COCOAS as food and beverage for Invalids, the name CADBURY on any packet of Cocoa is a gutrantee of puritv.—Advt.</p>
