        <p>ST. HELENS TOWN COUNCIL. MR. SINCLAIR AND THE MAYORALTY. COUNCIL CIIA4BER CROWDED WITII UNEMPLOYED. The monthly meeting of the St. Helens IT Council was held on Wednesday, The Mayor (' A. Sinclair), presi led. " Mr. Morgan referied to the decision of the , Brigade Sub-Committee regarding several nn which had occurred during the month. The rePtioto dealt with the turning out of the brigade. an .ttee conduct of the men at the fires. The cornan found that, generally speaking, the time of turr out would seem to have been good, and the belles of the brigade satisfactory. The committee renown ed that a younger driver be appointed, and that A not desirable any member of the Town Council sr interfere,ln the discharge of duties by officials aa vents Of the Corporation. The Watch Cotninittee curred in the' transfer of the management of the Brigade to the Watch Committee.—Mr. James Batt 'the councillor 'referred to in the minute, said there' afire a few weeks ago at Kurtz's chemical works. put carthorses to the engine, and after stirtit animals made for the hoards opposite the Tocre They then went for the "Echo" Office, in Hard-ille` and then went behind the Old Church. If they going to get a young driver, he thought _they have to get an instructor as well. At Pilkington4 he (Mr. Hatton) helped to get the horses ready. B. Leach said he did not wish to see such a scene It was a most disgraceful thing tit tie engine atia,i, could not be properly turned out from the Town 19'' INVASION BY THE UNEMPLOYED. f At this stage of the proceedings, the lmiin door o, Council-chamber was thrown open, and Mr. M'Kinley, who had been interesting himself in ti find work for the unemployed, walked in at the i about 100 of the unemployed. Mr. M*Kinley round until he reached the corner near to the alder chairs. The members of the Council were astonise7 this strange invasion, and the general opinion they had come to petition the council to core"- relief works.—The Mayor said he supposed a