      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>TIER DESERVING POOR t.</p>
        <p>at the Warrington Borough Police Court on Tuesday. a middle aged man named Martin O'Brien was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Horsemarket-street. The defendant was fdund in the street drunk, and creat- ing a disturbance, and refusing to go home. Inspector Spinks said the defendant had got a ticket upon him for relief for each day during the week, at the soup -kitchens. Defendant was fined 10s. and costs.—Patrick Flannigan was also charged with being drunk and dis- orderly. From the evidence it appeared that the defen- dant had been ill for a number of months with rheuma- tism, and had been living on the charity of his brother. On Monday,the defendant recived his pension £1 103.9 d. and when picked up by a constable the same evening for being drunk and disorderly in Irlam.street, it was found that he had one penny left. The defendant admitted having spent- the whole of the money in the public- house, in" treating." He was fined ss. and costs or seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MAGISTRATES DISAGREE.</head>
        <p>MAGISTRATES DISAGREE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court on Monday,Edward Marshall, a Ship Canal navvy was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Old-road on Sunday night. P.C. Burrows who proved the case said that prisoner was creating a disturbance near the lodging house in Old- road. Prisoner in defence said that he had never been drunk in his life and had served his time in the army Without anything against his character. Be had paid for his lodgings in the Old-road lodging house and when he came on Sunday night, he was refused admittance. He was very excited but not drunk when the officer arrested him.—Mr. W. Pierpoint (one of the magistrates) asked if it was usual for lodging house keepers to keep the money of people who came drunk—Mr. Bunt (chief constable) said that it was very common and amounted almost to an unwritten law—Mr. Pierpoint was of an opinion that if a man paid for his lodgings lie ought -either to be admitted or have his money back. He was Inclined to think that prisoner had been badly used by the lodging house keeper.—P.C. Borrows said that the lodging house keeper had denied that prisoner had paid for that night's lodgings, but he was not in court.—Mr. Pierpoint said that they had prisoner's word that he had paid and he did not think that because a man was in the box in which prisoner was, that his word should not be believed. Be believed that prisoner had paid and that the disturbance arose owing to his not being admitted into the lodging house.—Alter some consultation in which it was apparent that there was a division amongst the magistrates,Mr. F. Monks (presiding magis- trates) said that the bench by a majority had decided to fine prisoner 2s. 6d.—Mr. Pierpoint: I think you have been troated very hardly my man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>ALLEGEH THEFT OF Rom.</head>
        <p>ALLEGEH THEFT OF Rom.</p>
        <p>William Farrell and James Jordan were charged at the Borough Court, on Tuesday, with feloniously entering the cellar of the Royal Arms in Bank-st.,at 8.20, the pre- vious night,and stealing a jtr containing ab3ut five gal- lons of rum, valued at £3 155., the property of James Thomas. Mr. Hunt, in his statement.said the two de- fendants were s'yled as Ship Canal labourers, and were natives of Warrington, and resided at Brick-st. and Dolman's-lane. They were seen near to the Royal Arms early in the evening, and dispersed by Inspector Spinks and P.C. Woods. At al5 Sarah Partington, a domestic servant in the employ of Mr.Thomas.was in her bedroom dressing,when she saw in a mirror the shape of a man in the back yard. On going to the window she saw Jordan looking in at the cellar door and heard him say, "I will keep nix." Ile then ran from the cellar door to the pas- sage leading into Bank-st. She opeued the window and saw Farrell dragging the jar of rum after hun through the trap door of the cellar. When Farrell arrived in the yard with the rum Jordan got hold of the other handle and they went down the entry into Bank-at. The servant went downstairs and told Mr. Thomas of the theft. Mr. Thomas went into Bank-st. and found the two prisoners lying on the County Court steps, with the jar of rum in their poss(ssion. The police were communicated with, and the men were arrested and brought to the Police Station. Both prisoners were drunk,and a broken bottle which had contained port wine was found in the cellar. When charged with the theft,they each replied,"l know , nothing about it." Evidence in corroboration of the Chief Constable's statement was given by James Thomas, ' Sarah Ann Partington, James Davidson, and P.C. I Meacock, The prisoners were remanded to Liverpool ; Quarter Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>MIN olt CASES.</head>
        <p>MIN olt CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUCirI POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY —Before the Mayor (chairman), Alderman Harrison, Councillor Roberts, Messrs. W. Pierpoint, T. Birchall, F. Monks, J. W. Hayward and S. Wright. TEEKED HIMSELF UP FOR THENIGHT.—Edward Crosby Was charged with being found sleeping in a straw luit</p>
        <p>in Mill-st., and having no visible means of subsidence on Saturday morning —P.C. Hollingsworth said that he found prisoner lying on . he straw loft early on Saturday morning with his shoes off.-41derman Harrison.: He had tucked himself up for the night.—Prisoner was committed to gaol for 14 days. His 72ND APPEARANCE.—DanieI Crosbg, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bank-st., on Satur- day afternoon, also with assaulting P.C. Meacock and doing damage to his whistle to the extent of la—Pri- soner had been ejected from the 'Royal lame, by the officer, and commenced to use abusive language. The officer told him to go away quietly, but he commencea to kick the officer on the leg and also broke his whistle. This was pris.mer's 72nd appearance,and the magistrates determined to make an examule of him, and sent him t'- gaol for one month's hard labour for bein4 drunk and disorderly, and one month's hard labour for assaulting the police. , ALLEGED ASSAULT ON A FEMALE.—Thomas Griffin was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Winwick- road, on Saturday evening. and with assaulting Mar- garet Griffin on July 29.—The prosecutrix WAS yet un- able to appear, and prisoner was sent to gaol for 14 days for being drunk and disorderly and the other charge was adjourned. Anorana GARDEN ROBBERY.—George Stockton, 4, Back Church-st., was charged with stealing from the greenhouse of John Randalls, Lovely-lane, on Saturday night, a watering can, flower pit, and a quantity of flowers, valued 75.—From the Chiet Constable's state- ment, it appeared that Mrs. Randalls heard her dog barking furiously, and thinking that someone was in the garden, she went across and got the assistance of Richard Green and John Caldwell who lived opposite The two men went into the garden in different direc- tions, and Green met prisoner coming out. Prisoner then dropped a watering can which he was carrying and struck at Green. Green, however, seized him, and Cald- well also came to his assistance. Prisoner was held until Mr. Randalls was brought to the house, and the three then took him to Sanke3-st., where he was given into P.C. Hollingworth's charge.—Mr. Randalls wished the Bench to make an exempla of prisoner. as there were so many robberies in his and his neighbour's gardens. The Chief Constable said that there were so many robberies in Lovely-lane that the 'inhabitants ha,d formed an association to assist each other to capture the marauders.—Prisoner %vas fined 10s.and costs or 14 days. .</p>
        <p>A DRUNKEN FREAK.—James Bate,'l, Cobden-Fit., Was charged with stealing a cock from Matthew Isherwood, on Saturday evening.—Mr. A. Browne appeared for the defence.—The Chief Constable stated that Elizabeth Owen, stepdaughter of complainant noticed that the hen cote door was closed, and on going to open it, she saw prisoner inside. The prisoner ran away and the cock was found lying dead in the corner of the hen cote with its neck pulled out.—Mr. Brownb contended that there was no evidence of larcency as prisoner did not take the bird away. It was simply a drunken freak as he had been drinking all afternoon, and was very drunk and not accountable for his actions.—The prisoner was fined 10s. and costs or 14 days. TRAVELLING WITHOUT A RAILWAY TlCKET.—Frederick Tipping, of Liverpool, was charged with travelling on the Cheshire Lines frvm Sheffield to Warrington, on Saturday night, without having paid his fare, and was fined 10s. and costs or 14 days. SMASHING DRINKING GLASSES.—Wm. Robert Steven- son, was charged with being drunk and disorderly on Saturday night, and also with breaking four glasses valued 2s. Bd., the property of Mr. Rollingworth, land- lord of the Woolpack.—Defendant had gone into the Woolpack on Saturday night the worse for drink, and the landlord refused to serve him with drink. This appeared to irritate the prisoner and he lifted up his umbrella and made a sweep with it at some glasses. The barman knocked his hand away and this prevented him from knocking 50 glasses off the counter, but four were knocked off never- theless. He was ekcted, and P.S. Taylor took him in charge.—Prisoner was fined ss. and costs, and also ordered to pay th‘ cost of the glasses. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Jotm Shields, Rebecca Riley, Wm. Balky, and Frederick Davies, were dealt with for this offence.</p>
        <p>MORE VEGETABLES STOLEN.—Alfred Pugh, senr., Alfred Pugh, jun., Alfred Tomkins, and John Pugh, all of Hoyle-st., were each charged with stealing from a field of Wm. Wright, Folly-lade, early on Sunday morn- ing, a quantity of potatoes, turnips and carrots, valued 2. 6d.—From the evidence it was shown that P.C. Bruen was on duty and was hiding in Bewsey, when he saw Alfred Pugh, jun., corning along. When he saw the officer, he dropped something which proved to be newly pulled carr..ts. The officer gave chase but lost sight of him, but soon afterwards found out his address When the house was searched a quantity of potatoes were found,and in Tomkiu's house some vegetables were found. The prisoners were brought to the police station and the younger Pugh made a statement saying that Tornkins asked the others to go to Mr. Wright's field for some potatoes and they went to the field. Pugh, sen., said he was not aware that the potatoes were in his house.—The magistrates dismissed the charge against Pugh, sen.,and fined the others 53. and costs. Ma. FLEXING'S FIELD AGAlN.—James Ford, John Burns, and John Goulding, were each charged with be- ing rogues and vagabonds, found sleeping in the straw stack of John H. Fleming, at Arpley, early on NI miday morning.—P.C. Lewis gave evidence of the capture of the prisoners and Mr. Fleming said that his man went to the .fiekl3 on Saturday night and found some men in his stable, and he had to get them out with a pickel as they refused to go without some forcible persuasion. It was not safe to go to the' fields alone at night, as any- one that did so ran in great danger of getting a good thrashing from these men.—Prisoners were sent to gaol for 14 days. Taassrces.—The following transfers were granted : Charles Whittaker to the off licens6. 7, Hoyle-st. ; Mrs. Pritchard to 'the "Griffin," Horsemarket-st. ; John Shaw, to the Stranger's Arms, Dial-st. ; Maria Little- wood to the Unicorn, Winwick-road Thomas Burke, was granted temporary authority to sell in the "Bird in Hand, Bank-€t. OBSCENE LANGUAGB.—Margaret Mannion, 15, Buckley at., and Mary Ellen Grady, 14, Silver Square, were fined 53. and costs for usingobscene language on Septerhber 21, to the annoyance of passengers in Pinners Brow.</p>
        <p>TIIESDAY.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (chairman) Dr. T. Starkey Smith and Mr. E. J. Guest.</p>
        <p>TRANSFER OF LlCENSE.—Temporary authority was granted to John Conroy to sell at the Royal Arms, Bank I street' in place of James Thomas. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—The following persons were dealt with for this offence ;—Charles Hogan and Edward Sauce in Queen-street, 5. and costs ; Thomas Cannel!, in Buttermarkrt-street, 10s. and casts ; Edward Griffin, in Horsemarket-street, 10s. and costs ; Thomas Glavin, in Bank-street, 10s. and oosts. ASSAULTING THE POLlCE.—Margaret Fleming, fustian cutter, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Scotland-road, and also with assaulting P.C.'s Bell and Clough, whilst in the execution of their duty. P.C. Hell said he was in Scotland-road, when he had occasion to speak to the prisoner about her conduct, when she corn- menced using bad language, and also bit him on the hand several times and kicked him. He had to get the assistance of three policemen to bring her to the Police Station. P.C. Clough corroborated, and said the prisoner also kicked him on the legs. As the prisoner had been out of the town since 1889 and not troubled the police, she was discharged on promising to leave the t)wn again. Mn. G. RILEY'S CASES.—The following persons were fined for neglecting to send their children regularly to school :—Richard Booth, ss. •, William Evans, Si.; John Fiddler, ss. ; John Jackson 2s. ed. ; Richard Jackson, 21. Bd. ; Elizabeth Leyland:2s. 6d. ; Frederick Pritchard, 2s. 6d. ; Thomas Shaw, Ss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <p>5'</p>
        <p>TBE XAMIN UP. S URDAY, OCTOBER 7. 1893.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. John Fairclough (chairman) and Mr. John Geddes. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Edward Murray,Ship Canal labourer, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Mersey-st. He was dismissed with a caution.—Wm. Carliss, and Ellen Carliss,his wife, were charged with a similar offence in Bank Park, the previous afternoon. Evidence of the offence was given by Mr. Ashton, park keeper, and Mr. Parker,head gardener, Each defendant was fined ss. and costs, or seven days. A DESERTER.—MichaeI Cordy was charged with being a deserter from Her Majesty's 40th South Lancashire Regi- ment. P.C. Corcoran said he was in Horsemarket-st. the previous evening, when he met the defendant with two other men. He (witness) stopped him and asked him if he had been in the array. His answers not being satisfactory, he was brought to the Police Station, and on the way he admitted being a deserter from Orford Barracks. The prisoner was remanded to await an escort, and P.C. Corcoran was allowed the usual reward. THURSDAY.—Before Alderman Sutton (in the chair), and Major Ridgway. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Thomas Lynchand John Hinsitey were charged with this offence in Bridge.st., the previous evening. Lynch was fined 10s, and costs, or 14 days, and Findley ss. and costs or seven gays im- prisonmtnt. TRAVELLING WITHOUT TICKET.— Peter Keegan, who said he was a harvest nsan from Ireland, was charged with riding on the C.L.C. railway the previous day,from Penistone to Warrington without paying his faro. Pri- soner pleaded guilty. He was fined 20s. and costs or 14 days hard labour. Hisal7Tll APPEARANCE.—Annie Quinn. who made her 17th appearance was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly in Buttermarka-st., the previous night, and also charged with breaking a utensil in the police cell value 33. 6d. P.C. Delahay gave evidence of the first named offence,and P.C.Baxendale said at 7.15 a.m.when he visited the prisoner's cell, he found she had shattered in pieces a cast iron utensil. She was discharged on pro- mising to leave the town. Fainay.—Before Alderman Harrison (in the chair), Alder- man Wright, Dr. Gornall, Messrs. H. Roberts, W. Pierpoint, J, W. Hayward and S. Wright. ASSAULTING THE POLICE AGAIN.—John Kelly was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Rylands-st., the previous afternoon, and also with assaulting P.C. Newey at the same time and place, whilst in the execu- tion of his duty. P.C. Newsy in his evidence said he was kicked on the chest and legs, and he had to get the assistance of P.C. Clough. The magistrates imposed a fine of 10s, including costs or seven days. BEGGING. —Patrick Tyrrol, a youth, living at Town's- end was dismissed on a charge of begging. Mn. FLEMING'S STACK.—Thomas Green, a stranger in the town was charged with being a rogue E nd vagabond, and sleeping in a haystack in Arpley, belonging to Mr. Fleming. Prisoner was discharged on promising to leave the town. REFUSING TO QUIT.—Joseph Shillcock, landlord of the Bay Horse Inn, summoned Jane Ellison, Brown's-yard, for being drunk on his licensed premises, and refusing to quit the same when requested so to do on the 3rd inst. Mr. A. Browne prosecuted, and said the defendant came into Mr. Shillcock's house, and they refustd to serve her as She had had enough. She refused to leave the house, and had to be ejected. Mr. Shillcock took a summons oat against her, and the fo.lowing day the defendant again entered the house and used most filthy language to Mrs. Shillcock and threw the summons at her.--Ihe defendant said the complainant had spite against her as lie was a Tory and she a Liberal, and she had had a row with him on the election day. She was fined ss. and costs or seven days.</p>
        <p>MARVELLOUS RESULTS. If we said these things of Ourselves the public might well doubt, but never has a remedy received the un- qualified endorsement, as " Homocea has received. We ask one arid all to read these testimonials and ask themselves should they be without" Ilornocea " in their homes.</p>
        <p>MEDICAL AND °ITER TESTIMONIALS. RHEUMATISM.</p>
        <p>LORD COMBERMERE writes :—" Carlton Club, London, 9th Nov., 1887.—1 have tried your • Flomocea ' upon myself for Rheumatism, and I found it do more good than any embrocation I have ever used ,and several of my friends have benetitted by its use.</p>
        <p>COMBERAf ERE.</p>
        <p>BRUISES AND FLESH . WOUNDS.</p>
        <p>BISHOP WILLIAM TAYLOR writes : " High Barnet. 28th Ow., 1891.—My dear old Friend,—l have used Homocea,' and proved its healing virtue both for severe bruises and flesh wounds, and also to kill the virtus of mosquitoes and chig,gou (jiggers).—Yours very truly, (Bishop) Wst. 'Daunt, American Methodist Epis- copal Mission."</p>
        <p>eagerness lost a golden opportunity right under the posts, Warrington having to be content with a minor. Following some play by Bradbury,Buckley,and Raybold, the home backs got to work, a minor -ensuing from a strong run by Aeltworth. Then,whilet play was located on the centre line, Mason got possession of the oval, dodged his would be tacklers, and dashed away down , the field, threw splendidly across to Houghton, who in turn passed across to Lawless, and the latter putting on top speed rounded his inen,gaining a splendid try. This reverse nettled the home team, who at. once rushed into into the visitors' territory. The game was very fast and exciting,the ball travelling rapidly about the ground,the young blood in the team showing up very well, and ulti- mately Bandies dribbled into the home quarter, where Farriman saved daringly. Wilcox, Monks, Freeth, and Teare, showed up well at this stage, a try almost being obtained, and half time arrived. For a start the home team had somewhat the best of matters, a fine run by Fearnley looking dangerous until Lawless tackled finely. Westall made a strong rush,the visiting side now having a tarn of attacking. The Wartington forwards were very smart, but although Warrington pressed, nothing more was scored, and they retired winners of a hard and ex- citing game by 0-1-2 to 0-0.1. Warrington team : Ed- lesten,back ; Raybold,Lawless,and Coggins,threequarter backs •, Mason and Houghton,half backs; Freeth,Wilcox, West all, Buckley,Ed wards, Teare,Randles,Bradbury,and Monks, forwards. STOCKTON HEATH T. WIDNES RANGERS.—ThiS match ' took place at Stockton Heath on Saturday, in the pre- sence of 1,000 spectators. The homesters kicked off,and ,soon began to press. The game had not long been in progress when Cross intercepted and gained a brilliant try. Glover making a good bid for goal. On the drop- out, play lsecame very open, and the passing on both Sides being good. The four three-quarter adopted by the Rangers was shown to success. Baldwin and Cross now commenced a movement in the loose, and smart fol- lowing up by the home backs resulted in Barber ground- ing under the posts, Glover converting. The homesters still continued to pres, Gregory haying exceedingly hard lines, and at half-time were leading by one goal, one try to nil. The second portion of the game was again very fast and open, but the visitors were never dangerous; the passing, however, on both sides fell off considerably. The only score was a try gained by Bald- win, who obtained from a scrimmage, and feigning to pass, dodged in under the posts, Glover again convert ing. On the whistle sounding for time, Stockton Heath had run out victors by two goals, one try to nil. WARRINGTON RANGERS V. PEMBERTON " A".—Played at Warrington on Saturday last, Warburton started for the home team and after the usual exchanges, the game raged at midfield. From a scrimmage Clarke gave to Jackson, who made a good run and passed to Bradbury who dashed over the line and scored a clever try, War- burton landing a goal. Upon the kick out the visitors played up, Green and Winstanley, being conspicuous by good play. Not to be denied,however,the homesters again pressed and Thompson secured an unimproved try, which was followed with another by J. Savage. The Rangers who were now playing splendidly, and elated by their success did not relax in their endeavours to ' score, as from a passing movement, Thompson again got hold and cleverly rounding his opponents, grounded the oval between the posts, Clarke increasing the point ' to its full value. Half-time was called with the score : Rangers two goals two tries; Pemberton, nil. After the interval the game continued to be onesided, the Pember- ton defence being quite iacapable of coping with the combination of the home team, who, showing greatly improved form, further augmented their score with tries by Thompson (2) and Andrews (1). whilst Savagee dropped a goal,Clarke also placed goals in two instances. The game finally ended : Rangers five goals three tries ; Pemberton nil. RoCIIDALE Sr. CLEMENTS V. WlDNES.—Rochdale St. Clement's paid a visit to the chemical metropolis and were welcomed by an attendance of 3,000 people. The game was a much more interesting one than that of the previous week with Lancaster, being of a more open character. Both teams were well represented and were evenly matched. They were stronger on the defence than the attack, and neither team had notched a point at the conclusion of the seventy minutes' play. The home team lacked that combination which charaeterised their previous matches, and when near the line their play was erratic. Brennan deserves a word of praise for his play at full back. 'rime after time in the second half, when a score seemed inevitable, he came to the rescue of his side, and he did his work much more scientifically than his opponent, He was well supper ed by Barber, but, the other backs were only moderate. Forward, Forster, Johnson, and Kenyon were the most prominent. The "Saints" were best represented by the Brothers Rigg, Halliwell, and Waugh, but the whole team showed superior combination to that of Widnes. Sr. HELENS RECREATION V. RUNCORN.—St. Helens Recreation were entertained on Saturday at Runcorn,the game being very keenly contested. Houghton was the chief porformer, his defensive work being simply grand The play was principally forward, but now and again glimpses of bank play were shown. Faulkner's centre play was a treat, and the try he scored was a strong effort. The Recs, deserved a penalty given against them. 'rhe referee held off a long time,but the one he gave was under the posts' and Warder,the goal-kicker of the team placed a goal. Seddon and Molyneux were in fine form and did a lot of work, but Pilkington was very erratic. Fields, Dolan, and Gill worked as if the whole matter was in their hands. Forward, the Runcorn men were very level, Abrams in the last few minutes being very dangerous. Warder had few chances, but ho did his level best. The score of two goals to nothing indicates the game.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON (2ND) v. DROYLSDIrte HORNETS (ISt).— Played at Wildersnool Causeway on Saturday. Speak- man and Clare played a good game for the home men, against whom a goal was scored in the early part of the game owing to being penalized. Later en,in consequence of another infringement Warrington were penalized again and another goal scored against them. Shaw afterwards scored a gut for Warrington. Flalf-time saw the Elvrnets leading by two goals to Warrington one goal. Somo good play was witnessed in the second half, the visitors being the winners by three goals (one penalty) one try ; to Warrington one dropped goal. • WARRINGTON Sr. MARY'S V. WALLASEY FERRIES.—This match was played at Warrington on Saturday: Wallasey kicked off. The ball being well returned saw the home- sters pressing. Tries were scored freely, and at half time St. Mary's were leading by one goal three tries. After the re-start, the game was of a one-sided nature, Wallasey not having the slightest chance with their opponents, and at the call of time the score stood :—St. Mary's, two goals five tries ; Wallasey, nil.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN V. ST. FIELENS RECREATION, A TEAM.—At St. Helens. The visitors won by four points to three..</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RECS. V. RUNCORN HORNETS.—On the ground of the latter and the Recreation won by two tries ts nil.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>PENYRTIX FRIENDS v. OLD BOYS' FIELD CLUB—These teams net to settle their little differences on the school ground, on Saturday. The school quite overplayed the Old Boys in the first half, and half-time arrived with the scores. Friends three goals, Old Boys nil. Smith as usual had his share in the scoring. The last half was played during a shower of rain, and the Old Bays were evidently more in their element, as they scored as many goals during this half as the Friends viz : two and the final result was Friends, five goals; Old Boys, two. WARRINGTON V. WIGAN.—It was Warrington's day out on Saturday and they made no mistake about it, and ware most merciless to their friends the enemy of Wigan. The match was played at Warrington, in fine weather and before a fair number of spectators. Five minutes after the start Connor scored, and from this</p>
        <p>point the Warringtonians completely outplayed their opponents. Williamson (1). Felinelly (1), and Connor (3), brought the score at half-time to s—nil in favour of the homesters. During-the last hslf Warrington for- ward line put on six more goals through tbe instrument- :thy of Connor, Williamson and Norman. The game which was of a one-sided .character .throughout, was pleasantly fought. Final score—Warrington, 11 goals; Wigan, n,l.</p>
        <p>PENKETH v. RITNCORN.—On Saturday the Penketh team got as far as Runcorn, and added yet another solid victory to their splendid record. The game was very one sided, Penketh having matters pretty much their own way. The first goal Was scored by Penketh within five minutes after starting, the Runcorn men never having touch the ball. The final score was 13 goals to one. Runcorn reserve played Penketh reserve, at Penketh, and they went back beat-n by six goals to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Messrs. T. J. Gillespie and W. . .</p>
        <p>McCorquodale.</p>
        <p>JURY LISTS.—The jury lists for the following townships were sanctioned by the magistrates upon the application of the several overseers :—Southworth-w ith-croft, Foul- ton-with-Fearnhead, Houghton-with-Middle Arbury, Haydock, Burtonwood, Warrington, Golborne, Newtm- with-Makerfield, Rixton, Glazebrook, Cueedlev, Pen- keth, Great Sankey, and Woolston-with-Martinscroft. _ . .</p>
        <p>Cowans AND THEIR HOME FUEL—Joseph Cunliffe, Thomas Harrison, and Joseph Travis, colliers, were charged with stealing timber to the value cif 3s. the property ol Messrs. Pilkington and Co.—Mr. J. 0. Swift, St. Helens, appeared for the prosecutors, and Mr. Stewart for the defendants.—Mr. Swift in opening the case said that the defendants had been caught by P.C. Blackwood, whilst carrying away a large part of a tree trunk which had been felled near to the new railway. It was the property of Messrs. Pilkington and/ Sons, who had suffered a great deal from similar thefts, during the coal strike.—P.C. Blackwood depcsed to arresting the de- fendants whilst they were carrying the timber from the new railway.—Crors-examined by Mr. Stewart, witness admitted that there was a large crowd collected about, where the timber was being felled; he did not think the wood was only part of a branch, and did not think that Ihomas Humphrey's, the prosecutor's overseer, had given permission for the branches to be removed.—Mr. Stewart for his defence contended that his clients were s;mply carrying away a branch of a tree that had been felled and he wonld prove that Mr. Humphrey's, the overseer to the prosecutu.s. had shouted out to the crowd who were watching the felling operations "You can take the branches but do not touch the trunk." 'The defend- ants were amongst the crowd, and with others took ad- vantage of Mr. Humphrey's cffer.—A witness named Thomas Littler swore to hearing Humphrey's give per- mission to the crowd to remove the branches. He thought that the timber removed by the defendants, a sample of which was produced in court, was only a part of a branch.—Mr. Swift denied that the timber was part of a branch, but in cross-examination Humphrey's ad- mitted that the timber was part of a branch.—The Bench dismissed the case.—Michael Dowd and Wm. Dowd,were charged with a similar offence.—Mr. Swift said that as this case was exactly like the other, he thought after the deci.ion of the Bench he should only be doing what his clients would wish him to do, in withdrawing the case. He would remark, however, that he hoped Mr. Hum- phrey's, who had informed him that the timber was part of the trunk, would be careful in giving permission on such matters again.—The case was not continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ADJOURNED 13REWSTER SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>ADJOURNED 13REWSTER SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Before the ordinary business transacted at Newton Sessions on Saturday the Adjourned BrewstPr Sessions were continued, before Messrs. 'l'. J. Gillespie and McCor- quodale. On the application of Mr. H. J. Longton, a billiard license was granted to Mr. G. Anders, Bridge Inn, Burtonwood ; and upon the application of Mr Jen- kins, of Messrs. Jenkins and Unsworth, Warrington. a wine Ircense was granted to Joseph jenkinson, of the Golborne Inn. This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. Henry Reynolds Mr, Alfred Thomas, and Mr. Thomas Rigby.</p>
        <p>„ ASLEEP IN CHARGE.—Martin Bratt was summoned for being asleep while in charge of a horse and lurry at Sutton. He was fined ss. and costs lls. 6d, or seven days. DismssEn.--:Elizaheth Cross, Mason-street, was sum- moned for assaulting Mary Batchelor on the 25th. Evid- ence was conflicting and the case was dismissed. Two 13en CHARACTERS.—MatiIdA Holland, an old offender was fined ss. and costs Bs. 61. for using obscene and profane language and was sentenced to three months hard labour:for stealing 31lbs. of beef belonging to a navvy named John Rich, on Saturday night.— Ellen Coffee made he 42nd appearance and was sentencl d to three months hard labour for being guilty of disorderly conduct on Saturday night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>SEVERE INFLIMAIATION.</head>
        <p>SEVERE INFLIMAIATION.</p>
        <p>U. M. STANLEY (the great African explorer) says " flomocea " is a most soothing and efficacious unguent ...... ...It is as soft as oil and almost instantly mollifying in the case of severe inflamation.</p>
        <p>SKIN ERUPTION OF -FOURTY-FOUR YEARS' STANDING.</p>
        <p>A lady writes from Stockport, 17th April, 1889 : " flomocea 'is not known as it oucht to be for skin disorders. I myself, have suffered very severely for forty-four years from Scurvy or Eczema left by small- pox. Have always been able to have first medical ad- vice, bnt doctors have not been able to do all these years what Homocea has done in three weeks. My doctor is surprised at the result. I earnestly desire to assist others to the relief I myself have received." Lord Combermere, Lord Carrick, Bishop Taylor, and many others also bear witness to the marvellous effects of" Homocea.,'</p>
        <p>" flomocea " sold by most Chemists at and Zs. 9d. per box, or sent post free on receiptlof stamps to 21, HAMILTON SQ., BIRKENHEAD. 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>• FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>• FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note tha% reports of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the head office, Bewsey-street. Warrington, not later than ten o'clock on Wednesday morning.</p>
        <p>RUGBY. WARRINGTON V. TYLDESLEY.</p>
        <p>The matches against Broughton Rangers and Stock- port having been productive of disastrous results, James and John Bate and Berry all being injured, rendered the Warrington combination in a somewhat crippled condi- tion on Saturday when they met fyldesley. Faith was placed in the juniors who were given a trial, and the victorythat ensued was of a brilliant description.Tyldesley were represented by the same fifteen who had so satisfactorily accounted for Rochdale Horneta the pre- vious Saturday, and their success was looked on as next to a certainty. At 340 the game was started by Shaw kicking off towards the pavilion goal, and Pntt's reply travelling to the Tyldesley captain again ,the latter's reply was by no means a creditable one. The home forwards soon made a bold front,and several rushes, led by Nevins Higham, Thorniley, and Turner, were productive of the locals taking up the aggressive, a pcsition they main- tained nearly the whole of the first half. Repeatedly did J. Berry get the oval away, only to find Evans, [Leeson], and Boscow judiciously return. A free kick to Tyldesley was met by Boscow, who found touch at the quarter flag. A pass to HPPSOM was upset by Evans ( fyldesley). and following this the visitors reached the half-way. Then came a pretty hit of passing by Lally, Evans, and Heesom, which resulted in the latter punt- ing high to Gibb-ms. The Tyldesley custodian was speedily floored, and a general melee taking place opposite the posts, Higham at last made a desperate effort, and successfully grounded near the corner. and Turner made an excellent attempt at the place. Nevins was almost in direct from the drop-out, and Dakin charging a kick, the Tyldesley goal at this junc- ture had rather a w irm time of it. Shaw gained relief with a short kick, which the energetic Lally promptly neutralised, and thsaigh the visitors displayed soi F' pretty passing amongst their three-quarters. Potts raced across and floored Taylor just as he had every appear- ance of getting clear. A minor followed to Warrington, and then Carey had his first real chance of displaying his abilities, and a short run and kick showed him to be a clever exponent. No further scoring had been done when half time was called. Furrier restarted the second half, and after Potts, Heesom, Evans, and Turner had showed up in the opening phases, the lyldesley men eventually worked their way into the home quarters, where the Warrington defence was taxed to its utmost capacity. The two Berry's kept Shaw and his companion constantly employed, but all came alike to the ‘Varringtonians, and n.ne of their opponents were allowed to travel far. Shaw tried to make a breach in the defence with lofty kicking, but Boscow was especially safe, and though Fearnley got clear, and left the in ijoritv of the locals behind, the Warrington back awaited his arrival with remarkable coolness, and a capital tackle upset the rosy prospects of the visitors. Give and take play followed for a quarter of an hour. At length Turner led a. rush along the popular side, whilst Thorniley with a characteristic run and kick caused the Tyldesley men to fall back on their lines, and a spell of exchange kicking following. Shaw and Gibbons hesitated at taking the ovaf, and Dakin taking advan- tage of the error romped in under the posts, a point which Heesom improved. It only required ten minutes from call of time, and during that period Heasom al- most got in after pretty dribbling. Final : Warrington, a gal and a try (eights points) ; Tyldesley, nil. Teams: Warrington J. Boscaw, back ; C.Potts, F. Heesom, and P Carey, threequarters ; J. W. Evans (captain) and T. Lally, half-backs ; Fair. Turner, J. Jolley, W. Nevins, J. Thorniley, J. Dakin, T. Donohue, J. Bighorn. E. Bailey, and J. Taylor. forwards. Tvldesley : J. Gibbons, hack; J. Fearnley, E. Evans, F. Shaw, and H. Taylor, three- qua rters ; J. Berry, and W. Berry. half-backs ; G. Wood- ward, J. Shepherd. A. Smith, J. Tinker, J. Worthington, W. Roberts, W. Sutcliffe, and J. Fell, forwards.</p>
        <p>11'Am-en:am; A Y.TYLDESLEY A.—These two teams met at Tyldesley for the first time this season, the Warring- ton team having quite a number of new recruits,so that the result of the game was not awaited with quite as much confidence as otherwise would have been the case. But to the game. Tyldesley kicked off,but the Warring- ton forwards, headed by Buckley, E wards, Westall,and Randles,rushed splendidly into the home quarters,where a try was obtained by the latter, hut disallowed. Mason and Houghton were soon at work, by some pretty cross passing, the homesters being subjected to a very heavy pressure, and it took the Tyldesley hacks all their time to keep out their rivals. Just after Houghton had been floored on the verge of the line,Mason passed to Raybold who in turn passed splendidly to Lawless, but over</p>
      </div>
