        <p>TETE FATI 9LD. 110 ItSLS</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AN " I.NIERCEPTINO DEPOT." Joseph Stockton, Stockton lleathi was charged at the Warrington Borough Court, on M otiday, with cruelly illtreating a horse by travelling it wile in an unfit state on September 20, and Willialtr Batley. Liverpool, was charged with causing the same to be so injured.—Mr. P. Davies appeared for the defence and asked for all witnesses out of court, and a scene occurred between him and Inspector Dowty, R.S.P.C.A., owing to the la,ter refusing to leave the court en the ground that he was the complainant and h.d a right to remain in the court. The magibt,ates' clerk said that he had a right to remain, and the magistrate,' accordingly over-ruled Mr. Davies' objection.—P.C. Bell who was the first witness said that at 11 30 p.m. on the 20th ult., ht was in company with P.C. Turner in Arpley road, in front of Arpley station. They there saw defendant Stockton riding a brown mare which they noticed was very lame. They followed him and caught him near Friars Green and stopped him. They drew his attention to the lameness of the horse and defendant said he was not going far with it. He fu, then said that it belonged to Mr. Bailey in Liverpool and that it was to be converted into a well-known essence of meat. Bailey soon after- wards came up and the officers then discovered that the horse had bean bought from a farmer named Brockle- hurst in Bold. On examining the legs of the horse they found that one of the fetlocks was shot and gave way every time it touched the ground. They ordered the horse td be put in a stable and then communicated with Inspector Dowty.—ln cross-examination witness said that the horse was not very fat. It was in fair condi- tion. It was going at the rate of about 2i miles an hour when he saw it and it was his opinion that it was cruelty to walk the horse.—P.C. Turner corroborated the last witness but said that it was BaDey who said that the horse was intended for meat essence although Stockton might have said so also .—lnspecter Dowty gave evidence as to the lameness of the horse. He also said that War- rington was an intercepting depot for old horses which were taken on from here by the Cheshire Lines railway. —Mr. Davies in cross-examination asked whether witness knew that it took an hour to catch the horse in the field at Bold. Witness replied that they were very poor men who required an hour to catch that horse.—Mr. Davies was proceeding to ask witness whether he had not told Bailey that it was no use of him employing a solicitor as he (witness) could best any solicitor in Warrington when Inspector Dowty appealed to the bench whether such questions were fair, as he had never said such a thing.— The bench ruled that the questions were out of order.— Mr. Davies for the defence submitted that there was prejudicial evidence in the case. Mr. Bailey was a respectable tradesman in Liverpool. He asserted that the horse was suffering from no pain when the officers stopped it, and could have travelled to Manchester without any pain. With regard to the assertion as to the meat essence, the horse was in good coudition and he supposed that horse meat was as good as any other meat. (Laughter.)—Mi. Brocklehurst, farmer, of Bold, said that the horse was in very fair condition but the foot was a little deformed. It took three mi..n an hour 1 to catch the horse when it was in the field. In cr )ss- examination by Inspector Dowty, witness became very excited and was frequently called to order by the magis- trates and by Mr. Davies. It was, however, elicited that he sold the horse for 305.—A witness named Good- enough, who bad the care of the stables at which the horse was kept during the night in question, denied that the horse was in pain, and said that Inspector Dowty did not examine it properly.—The magistrates decided to fine each defendant 10s. and costs or one month's imprisonment. _ ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>4111 s 4</head>
        <p>4111 s 4</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS XIISIC LICENSES.</p>
        <p>REFUSAL OF 27 RENEWALS. A special session for the transfer of licenses and for the renewal of music licenses was held at St. Helens Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor (Mr. A. Sinclair), Aldermen J. Harrison and T. Cook Messrs. B. A. Dromgoole, 11. Martin, J. Forster, and D. Chadwick. The following applications for trans- fers were made :—Licensed Victuallers : Lion Hotel, Higher Parr-street,from John Errington to Helen Erring- ton; Victoria Vaults, Pocket Nook, from John Elliott to Robert Gourley ; Heskett Arms, Church-street, from George E. Cassell to William A. Duxbury ; Primrose Vaults, Park-road, from James Morris to Mary Morris ;1 Druids' Arms, Ashcroft•street, from Thomas Derbyshire to Mary Derbyshire. Beersellers : Railway Inn, Fleet- lane, Parr, from Thomas Clubb to James Johnstone ; St. Patrick Inn, Liverpool-street, from Michael M`Ghee to William Pritchard; Stanley Arms,New Cross-street ; from John Shortland to James Wilding; the Crown Inn, Brook-street, from Daniel M‘Lees to Edward Williams; Telegraph Vaults, Salisbury•street, from John Gray to Ellen Gray. Off licenses: Robins-lane, from Eliza Mahony to MariaFisher.—Chief-constable Wood objected to the granting of .the license of the Railway Inn to James Johnson on the ground that the former tenant had been convicted during the year.—ln reply to Mr. Swift who appeared for Johnson, the Chief Constable said he had no complaint against Johnson's character. The bench retired, and on their return the Mayor said that the license of the Victoria Vaults was refused, and that of the Railway Inn was refused, on the ground of not having satisfactory evidence ot character. All the other applications were granted. Mr. Swift then applied under the old Act for the grant- ing of a license to Johnson as the new tenant of the house, and called evidence, including Chief Constable Wood, to prove that Johnson was a man of good chars,- ter. He urged that the bench were obliged to grant the license. Mr. Swift also asked if the bench were prepared to reconsider the case of the Victoria Vaults. He asked the magistrates if they would grant another temporary authority to Gourley until the next transfer day, for the circumstances to be investigated.—The bench again ietired, and on their return the Mayor said: We takethe responsibility of declining your two or three requests.— Mr. Swift submitted plans or proposed alterations to the St. Patrick Inn, Liverpool-street, and asked the approval of the bench thereto.—The magistrates adjourned the consideration of the matter for inquiries to, be made. The applications for renewals of music licenses to public houses were next considered. The magistrates closely questioned every applicant as to the accommoda- tion of his rooms and the nature of the musical enter- tainments.—Mr. H. L. Riley, solicitor, applied for a li- cense for the performance of sacred music on Sundays in the People's Palace, St. Helens, and stated that such licenses were granted to the Volunteer Hall, Engineer Hall, Gymnasium, and other halls.—After a consulta- tion the Mayor said : The great bulk of the applications for music licenses made to-day have, as in previous years, been asked for apparently, and have been used apparently, under the idea that the mere use of a piano by a member of the family or by a chance custo- mer came under the words "public music or other pub- lic entertainment." We do not hold with that view of the meaning of the license. They need only be asked for where the houses are really going to be used for pub- lic entertainments, and the consequence is that we de- cline the great bulk of the applications made to us, but have granted a few where the houses are used distinctly in that way. We wish the people to whom we have granted thee licenses to understand that we propose to be much more particular in getting information as to the ventilation and accommodation of their houses, and whether or not they are adapted for such uses, on the occasion of future applications for renewals. Our in- formation is not much upon these points to-day, but we propose to pass these with that notice for the future : White Hart Hotel, Wellington Hotel, Clarence Hotel, Lord Nelson Hotel (John Gaskell), and Turk's Head Hotel (J. C. Rhoden). In reference to the application of Mr. Riley asking for the Feople's Palace to be put in this respect on the same footing as the Town Hall and the Volunteer Hall, we are not prepared to grant the appli- cation, but we are satisfied that the licenses which are now held by the Town Hall authorities and the Volunteer Hall authorities do not entitle them to make use of the rooms in the manner in which they have often been used in the past, and for which Mr. Riley has asked us to grant a license to the People's Palace. We find that the Palace is in no worse a posi- tion than the other buildings, and we therefore decline the application. The decision of the magistrates means the refusal of music licenses to 27 public houses.</p>
        <p>IL ZOLA'S IMPRESSIONS OF ENGLAND. The "Echo de Paris" has published a report of an interview with M. Zola, who is giving his impressions on his visit to London is represented as being thoroughly enraptured with his subject: "England "he declared, "is a marvellous country, with which we are but too inadequately acquainted. Englishmen love us, and I regret to find France offering such an illrequital of the delicate attentions which they daily bestow upon us." He added that while Englishmen detested the Germans, who were overrunning her workshops and factories, they cordially sympathised with the French, who were wrong in failing to reciprocate the sentiment.</p>
        <p>Dr.Talmage referring to the standard anecdotes about Rowland Rill's making shoes in the pulpit by cutting off the tops of a pair of boots; Whitfield's prophesying the doom of a sinner by the result of his catching a fly ; Spurgeon's riding down the rail of the pulpit in the pre- sence of his audience to show how easy it is to go into sin; and Mr. Beecher's wiping the perspiration from his forehead and exclaiming, "It's hot !--hot These, says Dr. Talmage, are" lies! all of them lies l" PUBLIC NOTICE.—Not one but HUNDRIOS of people (A both sexes with wrecked and debilitated constitutions are yearly restored to sound and robust health again by Messrs. Wilkinson. 3 and 4, Fitzallan Square, Sheffield. Write to them for advice free when suftering from weakness, loss of flesh, appetite, disturbed rest, pimples, blotches, indiges- tion, pallid skin, nervousness, and general debility. Posi- tive cure for all Blood and Skin Diseases, acquired or COnSti- tutional. Sixty years' experienco. One address (as above) for over 30 years. The" A 1 Mixture," 4s. 9d. the case. The Magic Mirror in envelope, four stamps. Sufferers dissp poititeo. with Elect.ric Belts should write to 113 at once.— A v</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>SHOOTING HOMING PIGEONS.</head>
        <p>SHOOTING HOMING PIGEONS.</p>
        <p>A SCANDALOUS PRACTICE. At the West Bromwich Police Court, on Monday, Arthur Martin, 18 years of age, of Richard-st., South West Bromwich, whs charged with shooting a pigeon belonging to David Mills, of Union-st., Spon-lane.—Mr. J. S. Sharpe, who appeared to prosecute on behalf of the Oldhatn Homing Pigeon Society, explained that on Sun- day, the 10th ult., the pigeGn was released by George Perry in a field near Bromford Ironworks. The defen- dant and several others were lying on the ground near. Directly the bird was liberated the defendant shot it. He was instructed to ask for a heavy penalty, as the whole- sale shooting of the trained pigeons was becoming a very serious matter.—The prosecutor stated that the bird was worth £2,and its parents had flown distances of 490 and 530 miles.—The defendant was fined £3 25., including costs, or in- default one month's imprisonment.—Mr. Dunn said the bench deprecated the flying of pigeons on Sundays, and if this bird had been shot on the Saturday the fine would have been much heavier. There were six other days in the week on which the bird might be flown and they (the bench; very much regretted that Sunday should be selected for the practice.—Mr. Beach (another magistrate) said the practice of shooting these homing pigeon was becoming a very serious matter in the Black Country, and the magistrates were detertnined ti sup- press it if possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>DEATH OF SU?. A. BLACKWOOD.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF SU?. A. BLACKWOOD.</p>
        <p>Sir Stevenson Arthur Blackwood, Secretary to the General Post Office, died at about eleven o'clock on Mon- day night, at the Great Eastern Hotel, Parkeston, Har- wich, whither he was brought recently in a prostrate condition from Rotterdam. The deceased was born in 1832, served during the Criwn war as Deputy Assistant Commissary General, and had held his appointment at the General Post, Office for many years. Be married in 1858 the widow of the sixth Duke of Manchester, and her ladyship, together with one of his daughters, was with him at the time of his death. Sir Arthur some time since went to the Engadine to recruit his failing health ; but, finding no relief, pro- ceeded to Ems. His condition however, became more critical, and it was decideil that he should return home ; but when he reaehed Harwich on Monday he was almost unconscious, and never rallied. The news of his death , was received at, the Post Office with the deepest regret, as he had always taken a great interest in everything that concerned the employees. He was president of the Post Office Total Abstinence Society, and took an active interest in the Postal Telegraph Christian Association.</p>
        <p>SENSATIONAL CASE OF BLACKMAILING.</p>
        <p>-- The Central News says that a sensational case of black- mailing has just been discovered, and is likely to lead to extraordinary revelations. The blackmailers have throughout, aimed at very high game in this conntry and on the Continent. A leading part in the business was taken by a woman who has been connected with certain scandals in Paris and with a notorious case in London. Very distinguished personages will be called as witnesses.</p>
