      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Ktattl to the Obttor.</head>
        <p>Ktattl to the Obttor.</p>
        <p>VACCINATION AND LEPROSY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your brief though characteristic review in a recent issue of "The Recrud-scence of Leprosy and its Causation," by Mr. William Tebb, F.R G.S., leads me to venture to send for publication, with your kind permis- sion, the following medically certified cases of leprosy due to vaccination, for which I am indebted to the memoranda in the Case Book of Dr. Roger S. Chew, of Calcutta ir—" Mabel P., a Scotch lassie, aged seventeen, a leper for the last eight years, was brought by her mother, who eated that she was vaccinated when she was seven years and a half old. About six months after vaccination, which was succe isful, symptoms of leprosy began to develop, and she flew here and there to every medical practitioner to save her child, but to no avail, as the disease kept increasing." Daidas, a native palki bearer, was a leper 20 years. He was a healthy young man. At twenty-one he was forcibly vaccinated, and a year after leprosy commenced at the seat of the vaccine mark. Jahoorie was a twenty years' leper. Previous to vaccination his history was healthy. When he was seven Yesrs old, the operation was carried out, 'and the disease first showed itself six months after, commencing as a white patch over the vaccine site. The description of the symptoms of this easels painful and disgusting. Turning to Dr. H. Hall Bakewell's evidence before the Vaccination Committee of the House of Commons, Eng- land, I.find testimony, though not certified, that I think quite as convincing, if we desire not the colloquialism of being hair-splitters. Question 2,564 and Dr. Bakewell's answer is as follows :--Question—" Have you had ex- perience of any case in which leprosy has been intro- duced ?" A nsirer —" I have seen several cases in which it seemed to be rhe only explanation. I have a case now under treatment cf the son of a gentleman from India, who has contracted leprosy, both the parents of English origin. I saw the case of a child last sear, who, though a creole of the Island of Trinidad, is born of English parents, and is a leper, and there is no other cause to which it ii attributable. Sir Ronald Martin, who is a great authority on these points, agreed with me that leprosy arose from vaccination " Since by the best leprosy specialists in the world, the alleged factors for the causation of leprosy, such as here- dity, contagion insanitation, syphilitic cachescia, a fish diet, malaria, L., are set at naught, it is worth while to give the names of some of the authorities who admit that vaccination is capable of transmitting and aiding the progress of this much dreaded malady. I cite the names of only a few out of the hundreds I could produce were it necessary. They are :—Sir Erasmus Wilson, Dr. John Hillis, Dr. Liveing•Prof essor W. T. Gairdner, Dr. Tilbury Fox, Sir Gairn 'Milroy, Dr. A. S. Black, Dr. Edward Arning, Professor H. G Piffird, Dr. Walter Gib- son, Dr. Blanc, Dr. Sidney Bourne Swift, Dr. Peter Hetet, Professor Henri Leloir, Dr. S. P. Impey, Dr. H. S. Orme, and those already quoted from. The most important cases of my cognisance as to the invaccination of leprosy are those of Professor Gairdner's adduction, first pub- lished in the British Medical Journal, and to be found also at pages 139, 144 of "The Recrudescence of Leprosy and its Causation," wherein there is brought together for the first time some of the most incriminating facts, com- prising some 100 pages of closely marshalled testimonies, nearly all medical, and in many cases, certified, as to the communication of leprosy by means of vaccination. In the face of Mr. Tebb's carefully prepared statistics and thorough reasoning, backed up as seldom argument has been backed up before, it would, in my opinion be the height of insanity to maintain that arm-to-arm (compul- sory) vaccination is not to blame as a part cause of the leprosy revival. Since, too, Mr. Tebb has given the sub- ject deep study and careful inquiry, if for nothing else than to arouse the public mind to the absolute necessity of thoroughly Investigating this pressing problem, lie deserves, not the abuse, but the thanks' not bnly of the medical profession, but of everybody else.—Yours faith- fully, JOSEPH COLLINSON. Front-st.. Wolsingham, Co. Durham, Sep. 27th, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>TETE COAL CRISIS.</head>
        <p>TETE COAL CRISIS.</p>
        <p>•• The way to emancipation :lies through the ballot box.".</p>
        <p>H. Whitham.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Whitham is perfectly right, but it must be remembered that about one half of our male population cannot gain access to the ballot box through various un- surmountable impediments.. You can only gain access through the circumlocution office. First you must occupy a house, and have your name on the register for two years at least as the tenant of that house. At the last general election a man intended to vote for the Liberal candidate 'for Warrington but found that his name was riot on the register,although a householder for over three years. The reason assigned was that in the three years' -occupation he had lived in two houses, having removed from a side street to the front of the road. He had lost lois chance on the first res;crence and had not occupied the second "long enough" according to circumlocution, to be entitled to a vote. Is there commonsense or justice in such a registration law as this? If the landlord does not pay the taxes in time the tenant loses his vote. The lodger franchise is another delusion. The value of the ledg,ings, whether much or little,ahould have no qualify- ing or disqualifying effect. The justice of the case is met by the character of the claimant for the vote. A moan of sound mind, of mature age, and blameless repute, after six months' residence is in common justice entitled to the franchise as his inalienable birthright, and nothing but misconduct should deprive him of it, whether he lives in a mansion or with Diogenes in a tub. Intel!. genre and conduct are the only moral,and should be the legal qualifications. I may mention another ease. An industrious, sober, man fell out of work ; times were had and work was un- attainable ; he had, as poor men often have, a numerous family of school age, but the fees were not forthcoming, and the children did not attend. Going to school with- out the fees they were sent home, and then the trouble began. Summoned before the committee and threatened with pains and penalties for breach of the Education Act and compelled to apply for the fees to the Guardians with consequent disfranchisement. This is circumlocu- tion law, and "the way to emancipation through the ballot box" is effectually blocked by it. How long shall this wretched anomally last P As long as workingmen return Tories to Parliament and give the reins of power into their hands so long shall the present injustice prevail. The Tories want no accession of vot- ing power or extension of the franchise to take place. An extension of the franchise by a million votes would materially alter the present state of matters. They have always oppased everything of the kind, and will do so while they are supported by the Tory workingmen to whom they never gave a single political privilege and never will. In fact, the Tory workingman does not de- sire any reform ; the present registration laws,and land laws, and labour laws are good enough for him. The Primrose League is the institution par excellence in his estimation, and he will support it and let other matters</p>
        <p>The war between the federated coalowners and the Miners' Federation shows hut little sign of truce or cessa- tion, and the industry and commerce of the great in- dustrial centres is paralysed, and the workers stilking the streets in idleness and hunger. Homes are being smashed and ruined, and can never be replaced in the lifetime cf their owners. The /misters show a determina- tion worthy of a better cause. Surely mischief enough has been done, and if property has duty as well as right it should he compelled to do its duty Coal has gone up in price 100 Der cent., and cannot he had at that price. If the reduction in wages means 61. per ton to the miner say one shilling all round, will not the consumer grant that shilling if it cannot be managed for less ? I know that we who are out of work feel the want of our wages, and what they provide for us, quite as keenly as other people. The great industries of iron manufacture, chemistry, and kindred trades are stopoed,or only parti- ally working. Is the present game that is being played worth the candle 2-11 oping to see it soon over,l am, sir, yours, _</p>
        <p>C YMRO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>WARRINGTON ACADEMY AND ITS</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON ACADEMY AND ITS</p>
        <p>SUCCESSOR. To-morrow (Sunday) evening next the Rev. W. 11. Drummond, 8.A., is announced to give the first of two sermons on "The Warrington Academy and its Successor at Oxford." The subject is suggested by tlle opening of the buildings of Manchester Col- lege at Oxford on the 18th and 19th inst. The College, which was founded as the Manchester Academy in 1786, is the lineal descendant of the older Academy at Warrington. Not only is there a continuity of tradition between them, for Manchester College took over the material assets of the earlier institution, and the nucleus of the valuable library which will now be housed in the hatatiome Tate Library of the College, towards the ,cost of which Mr. Henry Tate has given £lO,OOO, once bad a humbler residence in our own town. To the Cairo- st. congregation the approaching ceitbration at Oxford is one of special interest on account of its former inti- mate association with the Academy. and an address of congratulation will be presented on the occasion, on its behalf. It is not without local interest to note that the Principal of the College, the Rev. Y. Drummond, M.A., Litt. D., is the father of the present minister at Cairo-st.; and the Vice-Principal, the Rev. Y. E. Carpen- ter. M.A., is the nephew of one who was long a friend e and pioneer in all good causes in Warrington, w name is still held by many in ever grateful remembrance, the Rev. P. P. Carpenter, minister from 1346 to 1862.</p>
        <p>" Cocoi has. in a remarkable degree. those natural elements of sustenance which give the system endur- ance and hardihood, with a steady action that renders it a it acceptable and reliable beverage."—linel4. t</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>NOM ON tiAMES AD</head>
        <p>NOM ON tiAMES AD</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>There were many persons on the foctball field on Sat- urday who were loud in their prophetic ejaculations. Warrington were going to lose again according to these uuauthorised prophets. They may have been somewhat justified in arriving at this conclusion by reason of the fact that many of the best players of the home team were away. But they were not justified in making the prophecies until they had a better reason—the reason of knowing Warrington would lose.</p>
        <p>The two Bates, Massey and Berry were all absent— being busily engaged attending to gratuitious gifts of " barked shins "and miscellaneous bruises, obtained in the match with Broughton. Tvldesley were made aware of this and are said to have been pretty sanguine of victory, in consequence. Nevertheless they courted without—well, without the substitutes. These latter came out in a manner worthy of the highest compli- ments, and to qua,' " Tittlemcuse " of the " Athletic News," " Warrington's defence was al ways sure and the tackling superb. The forwards played well to a man and there was not a shirker in the team." This I-can corroborate, heartily.</p>
        <p>The defence of Warrington in the second half was indeed brilliant. For twenty minutes the Tyldesley men kept up a vigorous attack, which never amounted to-any sort of danger, because of the manner in which the invaders were repelled. WarringtOn's passing through- out was so bright and compact and clever that the audi- ence launched itself upon flights of enthusiastic ejacula- tions which cheered the hearts of the locals and brought foreboding into the minds of their opponents. The climax or " finis," so to say, was not reached until within a few minutes of the call of time, when the leaders of the locals male a special spurt, and Dakin— (may I repeat with the gate " Good old Dakin)—scored right under the posts. Fleesom did the necessary by sending the oval for a fly over the bar. Thus did the stalwart men of Warrington vindicate themselves and make a triumphant victory of their second League match. May it often be repeated. I should like to mention en passant that although the spectators are welcome to show their approval of a win by shouting " good old Warrington," or otherwise calling the team names, but they are not invited to add injury to insult by howling that latest and cruellest of comic (save the mark!) songs entitled " Daisy, Daisy."</p>
        <p>Last week, by the way I mentioned the lack of com- bination which was apparent in the play of the team at Broughton. Well, "It's quite another colour now." There was a c.omplete change on Saturday. The new men, were certainly ep to the requirements, whilst the remainder of the team displayed a consistency of com- bination that was pleasing to observe. Boscow was in splendid form again,and the judgment which he showed in his retaliatory kicks, was even a credit to the estim- able "Surefoot." Nevins, Dakin, Turner, Thorniley, Lally, Evans, and Heesom noticeably shone in their performances.</p>
        <p>the competition stands thus at present : PIO. Won. Lost. Drn. UT. G.T. Pte. AO. For. Broughton Rangers 2 ... 2 ... 0 ... 0 ... 5 1 ... 1 0 ... 4 Wigan Oldham 2 . 1 . 0 1 ... 9 1 ... 0 1 ... 3 Swinton 2 . 1 . 0. . 1 . 3 2 ... 0 0 ... 3 Warrington Salford Tvldeeley 11,-ehde,e Hornets Barrow .. ........... Broughton 2 . .0 . 2 0— 0 1 ...10 4 ... 0</p>
        <p>The champion A team had only a very narrow victory over Tvldesley Aon Saturday. They took a poor team with them as some of their men were wanted to do duty in the first so that, taking this into consideration, it was as satisfactory as could be expected. This victory how- ever was .a slight contrast to the terrible drubbing they gave the same team at Warrington two years ago. The A team has done very well so far this year, as they have only been beaten by a try to nil by Widnes, which was to say the least a mild surprise, but to-day they have foe- men worthy their steel to meet. The Wigan team of the future will pay them a visit, and Warrington will have to fight their' hardest to win. They have a defeat of last year to revenge which will no doubt urge them to work their hardest to taste the sweets of victory. Should Warrington have their full strength, the game will be wall worth going to see,</p>
        <p>The first team of the Warrington Rangers achieved a complete victory, on their own ground, over Pemberton "A" on Saturday. Now, when these teams met last season, the Rangers gave the Pembertonians a beating to the extent of over six goals to nil. They had not for- gotten this defeat, for on their arrival at the headquar- ters of the Rangers club with a good team, on Saturday, they hinted that the Rangers wouldn't repeat their pre- ViOIIS victory in the same tune. Well, they didn't quite, hut very nearly so, for the score at the call of time read Rangers 5 goals, 3 tries; Pemberton A. nil. This rather disheartened the Petsiberton boys, and one of them was heard to say that their first team would be a better match for the Rangers. I hear that to-day (Saturday) the Rangers will meet more than their equal,. in Stan- dish (South grid) the junior cup holders.</p>
        <p>The St. Mary's team, had a very pleasant, though rather one-sided game on the ground in Liverpool-road on Saturday, when they met Wallasey Ferry. From the commencement of the game to the finish, the Wallasey men hadn't a "look in." On the other hand St. Mary's scored tries freely, and at half time they had recorded one goal and three tries to nil, and at the call of time this score was augmented to two goals, five tries to nil. By the way, the St. Mary's team have a very promising player in C. Burton who scored well on Saturday. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>CYCLISG,'</head>
        <p>CYCLISG,'</p>
        <p>Warringten wheelmen--1;;;;; -finished their club runs now. Ihe membersof the Warrington Cycling Club had their last run on Saturday, Knntsford being the destina- tion. The unsettled state of the weather reduced the number of riders, compared with the average muster which usually turns out. Only 20 members put in an appearance, and these were agreeably surprised to find a change in the weather as the day wore on. During the afternoon a football match (Association code) was played on the Beath between the Warrington wheelmen and their friends of the Sale C.C. The visitors ham Warrington, captained by Mr. W. B. Bellian, proved to he the superior team, beating their Sale friends, under the captaincy of Mr. Walton, by five goals to two.</p>
        <p>Tea was subsequently partaken of at the Red Cow Hotel, the country quarters of the w.c.r.. where Mrs. Williamson made the wheelrnen entirely at home. The tea was followed by a social concert, in which the fol- lowing took part :—Mesars.J.Suxton (who gave "Massey Joe "), G. Smith, G. Whittam. J. J. &amp;Man, and others. The accompaniments were effectively rendered by Cap- tain Mounfield. Shortly after nine o'clock a start was made for home, and after a pleasant ride Warrington was reached at ten o'clock. Well, the club have had a successful season. Much of this is attributable to changes which were made in their rules and also to the indefatigableefforts of the officials, chief of whom is the general secretary Pickavance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SWI1N1311N•.</head>
        <p>SWI1N1311N•.</p>
        <p>Let llla congratulate the Committee of the Warrington Swimming Club upon the splendid success which at- tended their efforts in the second swimming gala on Thursday night. It was a thoroughly organized fete in every respect, The officials are especially deserving of praise for the way they dealt with their work. There was only one regrettable incident in the whole affair,that was the " scene "which unfortunately occurred between Mr. Barker, hon. sec. of the N.C.A.S.A., who acted as judge, and the speetators. It arose through Mr. Barker and the other two judges deciding that F. Garside, who came in second in the members handicap, only took the fith place.</p>
        <p>The whole thing seemed to have been an aceidmt. I was an eye-witness to the race and together with the good number ot pressmen who were present, can co- incide with the spectators in their assertion that Garside came in at the same time as T. Locker, the winner, and that the other three competitors who were given a place beiore him, came in a yard behind. How the judges fell into such a remarkable error I do not know. I thought however, that Mr. Barker in addressing the audience in the passionate way he did, because of their interference, was rather unwise. He was palpably in the wrong, and a Warrington audience such as that one have far too much respect for fair play to allow an error of judgment of that class to pass them. It was nil to be expected that they would take the " sitting upon" which Mr. Barker offered them in the docile manner in which perhaps he expected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>PENKFITE</head>
        <p>PENKFITE</p>
        <p>AranoLawn CLASS.—On Thursday night a meeting of members was held in the Conservative club to arrange for the coming winter. It was decided t continue the classes under the direction of Dr. Murray. A nursing class for females was also talked of.</p>
        <p>MOTUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY.-011 Wednesday even- ing, a meeting of the above was held in the Wesleyan school, to draw up a syllabus and arrange matters for the coming session. Mr. C E Parker, president, was in the chair. A very interesting programme is being prepared and the meetings will begin in November. Several lec- tures by able men are booked, and a successful session is anticipated. CONCERT.—On Monday evening, a concert, promoted , by the Penketh Conservative Association, was held in the Conservative Club Room. Penketh. There was a moderate attendance., Mr. R. Scarisbrick was advertised to appear, but was unable to be present, and the chief attraction being thus withdrawn, the c.oncert was hardly so successful as it might have been. The artistes were the following: H. Grounds, L. Foster, J. Moss, and Miss S. Moss, etc. Mr. W. Houghton occupied the chair, and also took part in the programme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>WARRINUTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINUTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>" ; The fortnightly_ ,meetin'g of the Warrington Board of Guardians wag held in the Board room of the Workhouse on Wednesday. There were pre- sent Messrs. G. Ratcliffe (chairman),R. Heaton, W. Houghton, W. Fairclough, S. Davies, G. Pilling, W. J. Shaw, A. Jolley, A. J. Jolley, J. Hill, J. Turton, J. Muir, W. Balharry, J. Clarke, T. Mather, P. Wur- burton, Dr. Patterson, J. Houghton, 1-1. Hibbert, H. J. Longton, J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (deputy clerk), C. H. Ward (master), H. M. Mines (superin- tendent of Padgate Schools.)</p>
        <p>THE CIISQIISS.::-THS SI</p>
        <p>" MISTAKEN."</p>
        <p>In the list of cheques was an accont for £5 for paint- ing the gates at the entrance to Padgate Schools.—Mr. CLARKE and Mr. PILLING drew attention to the fact that no notice had been given to the Board of any intention to have that work done.—Mr. PILLING said it was time that such things should be taken notice of as they were getting too common.—Mr. MINES (superintendent) said that men were engaged revaiting other parts of the schools, and some of the committee were of opinion that as the gates wanted painting it would be as well to have them dcne at the same time. That was the reason that they were painted.—Mr. W. IleoGigroN said that he was one of the members of the School Committee who with two or three others was leaving the school on the afternoon Mr. Mines mentioned. Two or three members suggested that it would be a better job to have the gates painted, and he (Mr. Houghton) expected that the Superinten- dent would bring the matter forward at the next com- mittee meeting. He was surprised at.the next meeting of the Committee to know that the gates were done.— (Mr. PILLING In A shower.of rain).—The CLERK said that for small repairs it was not necessary to apply to the Committee, but when the repairs involved an expen- diture of £5 it was clearly a matter for the committee to deal with.—Mr. flizavoN thought that there were no Guardians to blame in the matter. The officer thought he had a right to order certain things which he had not.— Mr. DAVIES corroborated Mr. Houghton's version of the affair.—The CLERK was of opinion that the mistake was, that the superintendent had acted under misapprehen- sion. He should have understood that the gentleman at the gates meant that the painting must not go on until he had instructions from the Committee at their next meeting.—The matter then dropped. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>GIFTS TO TEM MAXIMS.</head>
        <p>GIFTS TO TEM MAXIMS.</p>
        <p>The MASTER reported the following gifts : A small parcel of tobacco from Mr. Perris,Sankey-street ; bundle of papers from Mr. H. M. Ashton, Sankey-street ; fruit and flowers from the Rev. B. Prescott, Christ Church, Latchford.</p>
        <p>THE PORTER AND HIS DUTY.-AN lINEDALLENGED VISITOR.</p>
        <p>The MASTER further reported that Mr.Moorsom,iLocal Government Board Inspector, had informed him that he had driven through the gates of the Workhouse without being challenged by anyone.---klr. Davuge said that at the time the Inspector came, a cart had just passed through. Mr. Curry would perhaps be backward in stopping such august persons as the Inspector. (Laugh- ter.) He thought it vas a very trivial affair.</p>
        <p>SCHOOLS COMMITTEE.—MEMBERS AND THEIR WALK Te •</p>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>The minutes of this Committee recommended the tender of Mr.R.J.Ward for six new hand instruments at a charge of £ll 16i.11d. be accepted.—Several members complained of the walk to Padgate during the present bad weather.—Mr. DAVIES said that the last time he went there he got wet through and had to change his clothes when he got home.—The CLERIC said that there was no power for the Board to hire a conveyance to go to the schools. Each member would have to pay for himself.—Mr. Mom said that some years ago the mem- bers did go in a conveyance, and he thought that the members had to pay for themselves.—The CHAIRMAN: That was not so ; they were told "Don't do it no morel" (Laughter.) THE RELIEVING OFFICERS AND THE PREVAILING DISTRESS- The CLERK read the following leiter which the chair- man had received from the chairman of the relief com- mittee for the distress at present prevailing in Warrington ' —" Town Clerk's Office,Warrington,Sept.3o, 1893. Dear , Sir,—At a meeting of the General Committee held on Friday last, it was resolved that the Itxecutive be requested to avail themselves as far as possible of the services of the relieving officers, and, as chairman of the Board, we should be extremely obliged if you would consent to their doing what they can in investigating the cases sent to them. It is believed that this would be the means of preventing, a great deal of imposition we are at present suffering from.—Yours Ste., F. Monks, chair- man of executive of the relief committee. To the chairman of the Board of Guardians."—The Clerk said that the relieving officers were entirely under the control of the Guardians.—Mr. Davies hoped that the Board would not object to the relieving officers rendering all the assis- tonce they could. The imposition had been so great in the town that it had really become disgusting to those who knew the truth. He believed that if the relieving officers assisted, it would be the means of saving about £3O a week.—Mr. W. HOUGHTON also thought that the officers would render material assistance as there were 4.000 persons dependent upon this relief.—Mr. HEAvori , felt certain that the Board would offer no opposition to the relieving officers giving all the assistance they possi- bly could. They must bear in mind, however, that this committee had been in existence five or six weeks vet they had not asked for the officers before. He thought they had not questioned the people enough. He then men- tioned several cases of imposition which he had per- sonally noticed.—The CHAIRMAN moved that the request of the committee be acceded to.—Mr. Davies seconded. —Mr. A.J. Jor,,r.sy in supporting the motion said that no doubt there had been occasional cases of imposition similar to those Mr, Beaton mentioned, but there had been a vest amount of distress, and though there might be some unwofthy cases, the committee had done its work well.—The motion was carried without opposi- tion. THE GUARDIANS AND THE COAL MIER.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. J. LONGTON next moved the following resolu- tion:—"That this meeting of the Warrington Union Board of Guardians regrets extremely the prolongation of the coal strike which is paralysing the nation and causingan immense increase of poverty and suffering to the indus- trial community, and this Board trusts that steps will be taken to bring about a resumption of work in the coal mines without further delay, also that a copy of this resolution be sent to the owner's and miner's federations.' In support of this he quoted some figures as to the relief admini9tered at the workhouse. During the present week there had been 389 cases, the corresponding week last year 203. During last week there had been 344 against 181 last year. and the week before last there had been 360 against 169 last year. That in itself brought the matter into their hands as administrators, and as guardians of the ratepayers' money.—Mr. HEATON did not think that it was right of the Board to dictate to the masters.—The CLERK did not know of any other Union having passed a similar resolution. He strongly recommended the Board never voluntarily to interfere in matt-rs of this kind, unless their duties were affected in some degree. He would) advise Mr. Longton to with- draw his resolution.—Mr. LONGTON, alluding to Mr. Willia in Houghton, said that as he had found a seconder he would press his resolution. He thought that the figures he had quoted bore out his contention that the Board was affected by the strike. His resolution did not express any opinion on the matter of disput,., but simply called upon both sides to come to a settlement.—Mr. HEATON did not think the figures showed anything at all. The number of cases relieved depended upon race meet- ings and pleasure fairs. Sometimes before race meetings at Manchester, they would have 1130 or 200 to keep dur- ing the night.—Mr. W. HOUGHTON said that he had been inclined to second Mr. Longton's resolution but he would rather not interfere with the dispute.—Mr. Longton upon this withdrew his resolution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>COUNTY RATES.</head>
        <p>THE COUNTY RATES.</p>
        <p>The CLERK MadehiS half yearly statement of estimates for the half ' year ending Lady Day, 1894, and referring to the large increase in the county rate, he said it was certainly a disagreeable experience. Ile thought that admitting the work of the County Councils to have increased in importance, it was to be confessed that there was no convincing evidence of the enforcement on their part of economical methods of administration had so far been given. In connection, however, with the Lancashire rate it was to be acknowledged to have been largely caused by serious redaction in the exchequer contribution occasioned by a tall of revenue under heads from w hich the contributions were made.—The estimates were adopted and ordered to be printed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT OF THE HEALTH OF</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT OF THE HEALTH OF</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Daring the week, which was the 39th of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered :—Births : Warrington, 35; Latchford, 6; total,4l. Deaths: Warrington,23 ; Latchford, 4 ; total 27. The excess of births over deaths was 14. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 25.4 per 1.000, against 299 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 19.2 the corresponding week of last year. The death-rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 75 per 1.000, against 12, the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 2.8 the corresponding week of 1892. The ages of mortality were :-0 to 1, 9-3 ; 1 to 5, 4-1 ; 5 to 15.1; l5 to 25. 1; 25 to 60,8-1 :60 up 0 ; total, 27. The deaths included-5 from diarrhea ; 3 from enteric fever; 2 from phthisis ; 4 from brain disease; 4 from lung disease ; 2 from heart disease. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported dur- ing the week :—Scarlet fever, 7; enteric fever, 3. Meteorologs : The mean temperature was 51.5 Fah. and therefore the same as the preceding week. The maximum temperature 61 F., was recorded on the 30th inst., and the minimum temperature 38 F., on the 24th inst. The rainfall measured .92in. The general direction was S.W.. the reading of the anemometer denoting 1,300 milea.—JOHN H. GonNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>One hundred and fifty theu,and Odd FPliows have died since the organisation of the Order in 1830.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>EXAMINE H, SATUR DA Y. OCTOBER 7, 1893. THE COAL TRADE CRISIS. HARVES FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINE H, SATUR DA Y. OCTOBER 7, 1893. THE COAL TRADE CRISIS. HARVES FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>Thomas Garrity and John Moran, of Bold-st., were on Wednesday each fined ss. or seven days for stealing coal from the St. Helens Collieries Company. James Kelly, Barber-st.,for stealing coal from the United Alkali Com- pany and for assaulting P.O Whalley,was committed for 28 days. This prisoner assisted Toole, who was dealt with on Tuesday, to escape from the police. SCOTCH MINERS CONTRIBUTIONS. At the Miners' Cc nference at Glasgow it was resolved that the miners of Scotland be recommended tr contri- bute 6d. per week per man towards the supp tt of the miners on .ctrike. DECREASE OF TRADE IN ST. HELENS AND WIGAN. We understand that the mineral receipts in the St. Helens and Wigan districts of the London and Norrh- Western Railway for the month of September show.a decrease of about £125,000 compared with September last year.</p>
        <p>S. PAUL'S CHURCH. The harvest thanksgiving services at the above-named church were continued on Sunday. The preacher was , the Rev. T. M. R. Young Hughes, 8.A., and at the chil- dren's service in the alt:Titoon the Rev. J. Forbes, B A., preached. The Rev. S. Wilkinson, M.A., formerly vicar of the church, and now vicar of Timperley, preached at the evening service. The choir sang the anthem" He watereth the hills," (Spinney). There were good con- gregations at the services, and the collections, which are to be devoted to the improvement of the church vestry and tower, have realised nearly £3O. PENKETEI WESLEYAN CHAPEL. On Sunday the harvest thanksgiving services were held at the Wesleyan Thapel morning and evening. Ihe chapel was very prettily decorated with corn, flowers, vegetables, some lovely palms and maiden hair ferns from Mr. R. Garnett's gardens. The decorations were made under the direction of Mrs. R. E. Garnett, assisted by Misses Moses, Routledge, Hobson, Wigfield, and Boardman. The preacher was the Rev. Cowan Watkins one of the new ministers, and the large congregation tes- tified to his popularity as a preacher. Two anthems were rendered, the solo parts being in the able charge of Messrs. W. T. Wilkinson and J. Smith. Mr. J. Wigfield presided at the organ. Substantial collections were realized, which will be devoted to the general ex- penses.</p>
        <p>RIXTON WESLEYAN CHAPEL. The harvest festival services were held in the above place of worship on Sanday. Two excellent sermons were preached by Mr.Fl.o.Brigg, of Lymm, whose even- ing subject was "Israel's decline and restoration." The sanctuary had been artiaticaily decorated by many will- ing hands. Miss Clarke, Miss Johnson, Miss Evans,Miss Downs, Miss Bartholomew, Mr. Downs. Mr. Bate, Mr. Wright, and Mr. Thomason rendered great assistance in the decorations. A well-finished corn stack was made by Mr. J. Rimmer. The choir sang "Cry aloud and shout," in a praiseworthy manner. Toe services were well attended, the weather being propitious. The col- lections, in aid of the circuit funds, were in advance of , last year's. 1 ELLESMERE-STREET MISSION. The first harvest *festival in connection with this mis- sion was held on Sunday when two excellent sermons were preached in the afternoon by the Rev. G. D. Mason, and at night by Mr. W. C. Nicholson. The Mission Hall was filled to overflowing at the services, and dur- ing the evening service, Mr. Levi Richardson sang 1" Lead ki rally light." The mission was tastefully de. with fruit, vegetables and flowers by Miss Bar- low, Miss Gaukroger, Miss Isaac, Miss Roff and other members of the congregation. On Monday evening, a fruit banquet was held, Mr. John Ellison taking the chair. The place was again filled, and a miscellaneous entertainment was gone through, coffee being provided. The vegetables were sold and the fruit distributed. The collections at the services, which were to be devoted to the building fund amounted to £4 12s. WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH. • At the Warrington Parish Church the harvest thanks- giving services were continued on Sunday, when the pulpit was occupied morning ani evening by the Rev. W. Black, of Cowley, Oxford, a very eloquent preacher. In the afternoon on address was given by the Rectoor to children and others. There was an unusually large con- gregation in the morning, but in the evening even so early as six o'clock, every seat was occupied, and many were unable to get in the building at all. Forms were placed in the aisles, and every available standing space also occupied. The anthem in the morning was "See what love hath the Father," from Mendelssobn's " St. Paul," assistance being rendered by the students from the Training College. The communion service is taken to Dykes in F., and Sir John Stainer's setting from "The Cathedral Prayer." At night the anthem was "Fear not 0 Land," by Sir John Goss. The Magnificat and Nunc Dimittis were sung in Brunnet in F. Special harvest hymns were sung at each service, the Volunteer Band accompanying in the evening. Mr. W. S. Woods was at the organ. The collections, which were in aid of the clergy fund, amounted for all the services to over £5O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>HELP FOR THE MINERS.</head>
        <p>HELP FOR THE MINERS.</p>
        <p>The "Daily Chronicle" on Thursday publishes a com- plete list of subscriptions to its fund opened tl help the miners. The total amounts to £2,373, and includes one subscription of £2OO and five ot £lOO.</p>
        <p>COLLIERY PONIES AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A number of ponies driven to the St. Helens colleries on Thursday gave rise to the impression thas the pits were being prepared for the resumption of work. Such, unfortunately, is not the case. The ponies since the commencement of the disputes hsve been grazing in a field near Windle Hall, and as the weather is now t 0 cold they are being returned to their workings, where it i is considerably warmer. THE MAYORS' INVITATION ACCEPTED BY THE, MINERS.</p>
        <p>The Sub-Committee of the Miners' Federation met on Thursday, at Manchester, and after a long discussion, decided that the invitation of the Mayors of different towns to attend the joint conference at Sheffield on Monday, to endeavour to arrange for a settlement ot the coal strike, should be accepted. A SERIOUS AFFAIR AT WIGAN. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>On Saturday and Sunday Drs. Bradbury, Earnisk, and Benson were engaged in attendance upon twenty-one persons residing in Top Croft, Wigan, who were suffer- ing from the effects of drinking soup which had been distributed by a local tradesman. The persons concerned were in a critical state until medically attended. The symptoms were vomiting and spasms. The only explana- tion received is that the soup was prepared in a new galvanised boiler. Samples have been taken for analysis The sufferers are progressing favourably.</p>
        <p>. RAILWAY TRAINS STOPPED FOR COAL. The " Morning Leader" says :—Passengers on the Great Northern Itailway,on Wednesday evening had an unpleasant example of the inconvenience which the coal strike is causing railway managers. The 8.40 train from llitchen could get no further than Potter's Bar, and the driver gave up the attempt to reach King's Crass. He was using foreign eoaLand could not get it to supply the necessary heat. A sisoilar,flifficulty has occurred more than once recently on he same local railway service. The passengers for LOridoll took over two hours to travel a distance of 32 miles.</p>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY SCENE AT A ST. HELENS COLLIERY..</p>
        <p>At the Frodsham Colliery, St, Helens, belonging to the Liverpool and St. Helens Brick Company, lively scenes took plate on Monday. This is the only colliery in the district where the old rates laave been offered, and the pit on Monday morning crowded with people who came to buy the coal which wail being raised, and also applicants for work. The owners ban decided to work two shifts, and 130 men were working on the first shift on Monday. This double is the number of men formerly employed at the colliery. The little Frodshara Colliery, St. Helens, belonging to Liverpool and St. Helens Brick Company, presents a busy scene of activity. Double shifts of men are at work, and the owners are beseiged with orders for household coal. A TOWN COUNCIL TAKES ACTION,</p>
        <p>The Leicester Town Council on Wednesday evening, on the motion of Alderman nskip, secretary of the National Union of Shoe Operates, adopted a resolution expressing regret at the Associated Coal Owners' refusal to confer with the Miners' Federation without conditions believing that a Conference would have led to the speedy settlement of a disput,, paralysing trade in all industries, and causing untold misery and suffering to thousands of innocent persons. The resolution also endorsed the intervention of the Mayors, and hoped it would be crowned with success. It was carried with three dis- sentients.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>MR. BEECHAM'S RELIEF SCHEME.</head>
        <p>MR. BEECHAM'S RELIEF SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Mr. Beecham, St. Helens, has placed an order for 1,200 pairs of black worsted E toc k ngs with Mr. J. H. Houghton, of Ormskirk-st.. In that town, and an order for 1.200 pairs of clogs with another tradesman. The clogs and stockings will be delivered at the various schools in the town, and given to those children who on Thursday morning last were found at school shoeless and stockingless. This scheme was a little surprise on the part of Mr. Beecham, who without any preliminary notice had requested the numbers to be immediately supplied to him. The average attendance at the St. Helens schools is 12,000, so that the number of children without shoes and stockings on Thursday last was just 10 per cent.</p>
        <p>A MEEPING OF COALOWNERS. A REDUCTION INSISTED UPON.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Coalowners' Federation was held at , Westminster, on Tuesday, to consider the resolution passed by the Miners' Federation at the Chesterfield meeting last week. It was decided that no settlement could be made which did not include a reduction in wages. The meeting also had before it a proposal of the Mayors of Leeds, Sheffield. Bradford, Nottingham, Derby, and Barnsley, that the Owners' Federation, and the Miners' Federation should send deputations to a meeting of those gentlemen to be held next Monday with a view to devising a basis for the settlement of the dispute ; and it was resolved that three leading coal. owners and the secretary of the Federation should at- tend the meeting and report to the Executive. THE ATTITUDE OF ST. HELENS EMPLOYERS. Humour having gained currency that some St. Helens coalowners were prepared to open their pits at the old rate of wages, Mr. Glover, J.P., miners' agent, wrote asking for confirmation or otherwise. Mr. France, man- ager for the St. Helens Collieries Company, replied on Wednesday, that so far as their collieries were concerned any rumour of the kind was not true, Mr. Turner, man- ager of the Whitecross Company, says he has had no in- structions to restart at the old rate of wages, and does not think any such step is contemplated. Mr. Jackson, manager of the Collins-green and Bold Collieries, replies that he has said nothing to anyone about their men re- turning to work at the old rate. Mr. Thomas Penning- ton, m wager of Ashton's-green Colliery, says the only instruction he has had is that when the men go it will be at the reduction of 25 per cent. LORD SALISBURY AND THE STRIKE. 1 Replying to a correspondent who called his attention I to the groat distress and suffering and the serious dislo- cation of trade caused by the prolonged dispute in the coal trade, Lord Salisbury says he has watched with sleep regret the progress of the conflict between the coal- owners and the miners. He fears the injury which it will do to both sides will be profound and lasting. It is, he adds, to be regretted that an opportunity was not given for a full discussion Of the question in the House of Commons, for such discussions are of the greatest value in forming public opinion. Referring to a sug- gestion that Parliament should intervene, Lord Salisbury says he is not aware that any plan has been suggested by which legislation could arrest or mitigate the injuries under which the coal industry is suffering.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>BRUTAL WIFE ASSAULT AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BRUTAL WIFE ASSAULT AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED IJ!.;E, OF THE KNIFE. At the Warringtm Borough Court on Thursday morning,a Ship Canal labourer named John McCone living at 4, Friars'-lane, was charged with unlaw- fully wounding his wife by kicking her, and stab- ing her on Tuesday night. Mr. Hunt (Chief Con- stable) in his statement said the complainant was Mary McCone, wife of the prisoner, who lived with him at 4, Friars'-lane, Warrington. On Tuesday, the prisoner didn't go to his work, and was drinking all day. On going home in the evening he commenced quarrelling with his wife, and kicked her several times, and she was afterwards imind by the next door neigh- hour lying in a pool of blood on the floor. The police did not hear anything of the matter until four o'clock on Wednesday afternoon when D. S. Monag- han and Inspector Spinks went to see her. They found her lying on the sofa downstairs. Blood was about the house, and there was a large wound on her leg two or three inches long, and as though it had been done with a knife. The police went in search of the prisoner and found him. They took him to the house, and the wife repeated her state- ment in the prisoner's presence, saying that he had used a knife, and that she had seen it in his hand. The pris- oner said he had never used a knife, but said he had done it with a kick. There were also bruises on the woman's right arm. Dr. S. Smith was communicated with, and after examination, he believed the wound on the leg was inflicted by a knife, or some such sharp instrument. A knife was found upon the prisoner, but no blond marks could be seen. Dr. Smith said the woman would not be able to appear until Tuesday, and he (the Chief Constable) asked for a remand until then. D.S. Monaghan gave evidence as to visiting the house and apprehending the prisoner. Be said the stockings of Mrs. McCone were completely saturated with blood. He charged the prisoner with wounding his wife, and he replied that he had done it with a kick. Inspector Spinks said it was a clean cut and deep. The prisoner was remanded until Tuesday next.</p>
        <p>• oldest soldier in the British Army is Field-Marshal ir Patrick Grant. He is 89 years old, and joined the Army in the year the Duke of Cambridge was born. .0 AC</p>
        <p>:;DA:RSAO</p>
        <p>ItTeiV9LI3U t3PIRALI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCRES-WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCRES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>GOLBORNE-STREET BAPTIST CHAPEL The autumnal services in connection with the above named chapel were held on Sunday. The pastor, the Rev. D. W. Roberts preached morning and evening, to fair congregations. Special anthems were sung by the choir, under the conductorship of Mr. A. Fieldhouse. The anthems sung were " Semi out Thy Light" (Gounod) and " Come unto me, ye weary," the solo parts being taken by Mr. and Miss Fieldhouse. Mr. F. Hickman accompanied on the organ with his usual ability. Col- lections were taken at each service in aid of the church expenses, and realized nearly £6. RIX fON WESLEYAN CHAPEL. The annual te L meeting of the Rixton Wesleyan Chapel was held on 'fliesday at the chapel. About 50 sat down to an excellent tea, after which a meeting was held, the Rev. S. Goodier, circuit minister, taking the chair. A quartette was sung by Messrs J. Bate, W. Shaw, P. , Thomason, and G. Wright, and a duet was also sung by Miss Clarke and Mr. 'l homason. Mr. Bate also sang the well known song " Throw out the life line," Mr. Shaw accompanied. Recitations were given by the school I children and Mr. S. Blythe also addressed the meeting. After the meeting a sale of fruit took place, the proceeds of which are to devoted to the chapel fund. THE P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE CHURCH. The usual P.. A. meeting in Wycliffe Church on Sun- day afternoon was well attended. The total member- ship now exeeedi 900. Mr. T. Hawthorn presided, and in a short address said that the P.S.A. movement had gone a long way towards solving the problem " Why do the working-classes hold aloof from the churches." He also refuted the assertion that working men were averse to listening to the gospel. Mr. W. Light, of Warrington was the speaker for the afternoon, and he delivered an excellent gospel address basing his remarks on the text " For what is your life" James 4c, 14v. Mrs S. W. Jeffery was the soloist, and she sang as a solo "There's a friend for little children." The meeting was also taken part in by the Rev. J. Yonge, Mr. H. Barker, and Mr. B. Farrar. Mr. F, R. Roberts, M.A.,was the accompanist on the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>WEDDING OF MR. T. T. MOORE.</head>
        <p>WEDDING OF MR. T. T. MOORE.</p>
        <p>• On Wednesday at St. Francis Liverpool, the marriage of Mr.</p>
        <p>Moore., fourth son of the late George Townend Moor and Mrs. Moore, of Palmyra Square, Warringtoa, with Mary Beatrice, eldest daughter of Dr. Callen. M.D., was solemnized in the presence of a large congregation by Father Brown, S.J. nuptial mass being offered up by Father Borscht, S*J. The bride was led to the altar and given away by her father, her sister. Miss Callon,attehding her as bridesmaid. The bridal dress Was white satin handsomely trim- med with chiffon and orange blossoms. She wore a magnificent diamond star brooch the gift of the bridegroom and carried a shower bouquet of the choicest flowers. The bridesmaid's gown was of white crepon and she also carried a beautiful bou- quet. Mr. G. T. Monre, brother of the bridegroom, acted as hest man. After the ceremony the happy pair left Harwich, en route to the continent. A reception was held at the residence of the bride's father on Saturday afternoon last, at which 150 invited guests were pre4mt. The wedding presents which were very numerous and costly were dis- played on tables in the drawing room, and among others comprised the.following:— _</p>
        <p>Bridegroom to bride, diamond star ; Bride to bridegroom, diamond and pearl pin ; Dr. Callon. two c.indelabra and silver goblet ; Mrs Callon, pearl brooch ; Miss Callon, cut glass fruit dish; Miss G and MIAS I Callon, two bronze figures; Mr Wand A Callon, four Fitzan china vases; sister, water colour painting; Mr C H Fa irclough, cheque: Mrs Fair- dough, cage of silver salts and Spoons; Mrs I‘l4,ore, cheque ; Mr J T Moore, Liverpool, oil painting ; Mr G T Moore, cheque ; Miss Moore; silver salts in case ; Miss Monica Moore, Dresden china Vass; Mr C Moore, case of silver Apostle spoons; Mrs Powell, silver crumb scoop; Mrs Young, London, cheque; Mrs Dan Powell, cushi-n : Mrs Holland, London, cheque; Miss Churton, hand nainted dessert service; Miss Connelly, pair of silver candlesticks ; Mrs Lernmens, silver toast rack ; Mrs Williams, six t,ble vases DI and Mrs Whitford, silver sugar basin and sifter ; Mrs Butler, silver fish carver ; Mr and Mrs Charles Kelly, silver teaspoons in case ; Mr and Mrs Furness Preston, case con- taining one dozen dessert silver knives, forks, nut crackers, crane scissors, and fruit spoons ; Mr and Mrs Palin, Meols Hall, Southport, silver liquor stand, decanter, and glasses ; Mr E A Weiner, Sunder- land,silver mounted bread knife,fork and cheese scoop ; the Misses Weiner, case four silver knife rests ; Mrs Lightbound,three silver table vases; Mrs and Miss Kelly, cushion ; Miss Fletcher, china art pot ; Mr and Mrs A Forshaw, spirit tantalas : Mr and Mrs Grimsbaw, case four silver salts •, Dr and Mrs Shepherd, folding photo frame; Mr and Mrs Daley, silver teapot, cream jua,and sugar basin ; Mr A Meger.case silver teaspoons:and sugar tongs ; Miss Henson' two silver serviette rings ; Mr and Mrs Hugh Quinn, silver mirror ; Miss Froes, afternoon tea cloth; Mr A Powell, water colour painting ; Mr and Mrs Smith, Newton case of cutlery and table silver ; Mr and Mrs French, two water colour paintings ; Mr and Mrs Barff, London china dinner service ; Miss Quig- ley, silver jelly dish and spoon ; Lieutenant and Mrs S;veeney,R.N.,Crown Derby dish ; Mrs and Miss Wilson, china vase; Mrs Purgold, folding photo screen ; Mr W Powell, Bamboo flower stand ; Mr Kufeke, silver cake basket; Mr and Mrs J. J. Yates, silver biscuit box ; C. Young, china flower basket ; the Misses Reckliffe, silver mounted clothes brush ; Mr and Mrs Heywood, afternoon tea cloth ; Mr and Mrs F Taylor, silver bread fork; Mrs Gamble, blotting pad ; Mrs Gill. silver cruet stand ; Mrs Cohen, silver ink stand and pill box ; Mrs Lewis, silver mounted scent bottlr ; Mrs Riley, glove box ; Mrs Lowells, glove and handkerchief box; Mr 0 Powell, two handpainted gauze fans ; Mrs W Roberts, photo frame; Mr. R: Leetham, bangle; Mrs Brindle, case of silver serviette:rings., 113V0 i3.l3ollAti JJA</p>
        <p>„00 A MOTT/1H .11 .0</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOA RD OF IMPROVEMENT COMMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held at the Town Hall on Thursday. Mr. S. Taylor presided, and there were present Messrs. F. Wiswall. H. J. Grine, J. Pritchard, J. Meadowcroft, J. W. Lightburn, J. Dob- son, W. Collier,E. Imison, W. Leach, D. Bisbrown, W. Hankinson, E. W. Humphreys, R. Abel, Dr.Man- gan ; F. A. Lake (law clerk ),E. Marshall (secretary) .11r. Barker (surveyor), Dr. McDougall (medical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>HEALTH OF THE TOWN.</head>
        <p>HEALTH OF THE TOWN.</p>
        <p>The Medical ofG-Wie-p&lt;&gt;irte-ci-i-h;i- the number of deaths registered during September amounted to 45. Of these 4 were inquest cases and of the remaining 41 cleaths,three were due to bronchitis,five to diarrhcea, three to convul- sions, five to mesenteric disease, six to enteric fever, three to cancer, two to inflammation of the bowels, two to pneumonia, two to inflammation of the lining mem- brane of the brain, thee to heart disease, and one each to inflammation of the wind pipe, exhaustion, kidney disease, debility, jaundice, ulceration of leg, and senile decay. The 45 deaths show an average death-rate of 27 per 1,000 of the estimated population, and exclusive of the inquest cases of 24.6, for the twelve months. Fifteen of the deaths occurred amongst children under one year and six were infants between one and two years. In reply to Mr. Humphreys Dr. McDougall said there were no insanitary conditions to explain the high infant mor- tality.—Mr. Collier inquired if it were insanitary condi- tions that had caused the outbreak of typhoid fever at Zion Place.—Dr. McDougall thought not ; there was a wave of typhoid fever all over the country, and it was difficult to account for disease of such an extreme char- acter at the present time.—Mr. Collier : Is it the first time typhoid fever has been located in Zion Place.—Dr. McDougall : First time to my knowledge. HIGHWAYS AND BUILDINGS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee recommended that Mr. James Wilding be -appointed Assistant Surveyer for a further term of one year, at a salary after the rate of £l5O a year; that the year date from October Ist, 1893; and that he devote his time principally to the superintendence of the mak- ing ef private streets, and to the work incidental there- to; that the tender of Mr. John Sefton, Runcorn, for work to be done in York-st., Hill-st., Walton-st., and Norman Road. be accepted; that the tender of Mr. Thomas Nor- man for the supply of about 700 yards of 12-in, pipes, about 200 yards of 9-in pipes, and about 300 yards of 8-in pipes, and of bends and junctions be accepted; that the plan of the Marquis of Cholmondeley for the erec- tion of a farm house in a field near the Brine Reservoir in front of Pewitt Hall be approved, subject to the cesspool, ashpit and privy being made in accordance with the re- quirements of the Surveyor.—ln moving the adoption of tne minutes, Mr. Wiswall, said the Committee were about to proceed vigorously with the formation of new streetcand they were appointing Mr.Wildiog with a view of carrying out this work efficiently, not only in the interest of the ratepayers but of the property owners themselves—Mr. Hankinson seconded.—Mr. Bisbrown after disclaiming the slighest personal feeling in the matter, said twelve months ago they were distinctly told that Mr. Wilding's appointment was only for twelve months, and the work of preparing plans and specifica- tions for the new streets, for which he was engaged was finished months ago and there were no public works in hand at the present time. He contended that a surveyor , would be quite sufficient for the whole of the work. The 1 ratepayers twelve months ago stated that the appoint- ment of Mr. Wilding was the thin end of the wedge in order to get two surveyors and the present proposition would appear to show that they were not far wrong. , He thought they might save that £l5O, especially in the depressed state of trade. Mr. Collier endorsed Mr. Big- brown's remarks. He considered they ought to be able to do with one surveyor, and he suggested that the sur- veyor should take one or two apprentices ; they would be a murce of profit to him and of benefit to the town. He contended that Mr. Wilding's appointment would make the cost of supervision of the new streets 2s. per yard, which was greatly too much.—yr. Lightburn said it had been the intention of the Beard that five or six streets should be proceeded with, but when they went to borrow the money and submitted plans the Local Government Board said they must do the whole of the work, and they must have the whole of the plans and duplicates sent on to them. There was thus a very sudden increase in the work. As to having one sur- veyor. it was a well-known fact that there must be a great increase of work during the construction of these streets, and he did not know a town oh the size of Run- corn and engaged in similar work that was only employ- ing one surveyor. Mr. Wiswall said he claimed to be second to none on the Board in his endeavours to save the rates, but Mr. Bisbrown h.d failed to show what could be saved or how it could be saved. There was such a thing as being penny wise and pound foolish. He ccntended that the Committee were acting in the best interest of ail. The minutes were passed, Mr. Collier and Mr. Bisbrown voted against.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>HEALTH COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>HEALTH COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee recommended th tt the tender of Mr. Samuel Crosby for the supply of provender for the next six months be accept3d.—Mr. Imison moved the con- firmation. He hoped it would not be understood that this was the whole of the business gone through last month.—Mr. Lightburn : It is all the ratepayers know about, Mr. Chairman.—The Chairman : We sat from half-past seven until half-p Ist ten o'clock on the Health Committee.—Mr. Crrice seconded the adoption of the minutes.—Carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>WATEM COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WATEM COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee recommended that Mr. Imison be appointed a member of the Committee ; that George Littleinore and William Sulmes be engaged as Engineers at the Pumping Station at 4s 4d., and 4s 2d. per day respectively, and that they be allowed to occupy the houses at the Pumping Stati•-u free of rent and rates, and to have a free supply of coal ; that William Bolland and Robert liewls be eugagtd as stokers at 3s ed. per day ; that Job Hancock be engaged as Engineer at the Halton Pumping Station at 35 ed. per day, and that he occupy his time when not engaged as engineer,as fitter or In earrying out any other duties which he may be re- quired to discharge; that Frederick Hancock be engaged as fitter at 45 2d. per day, and that he occupy his time when not working as fitter, in carrying out such other duties he may be required to discharge; that the above- named appointments be subject to termination on one week's notice being given by either party ; and that the two boilers at the Runcorn Pumping Station be insured for £l,OOO .each, with the Boiler Insurance Co. at an annual premium of £2 105. for each boiler. Mr Meadowcroft moved the adoption Of the minutes.— In reply to Mr. Grice he said the men were engaged on the same terms as paid by the Water Co.—Carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMIT rEE.</head>
        <p>TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION COMMIT rEE.</p>
        <p>This committee recommended that grants be made to the Runcorn School Board from time to time for techni- cal instruction of sums not to exceed £230 in the ag- gregate.—Mr. Wiswall moved the adoption of the minu- tes.—Mr. Pritchard said the eaimate of the science and Art Committee was as follows i—Salaries, Sm. of teach- ers. £200; secretary, £4O ; fuel, light, and caretaker, £2O ; printing and advertising, £l6 ; total. £278, and deducting pupils' fees, £46, left the net estimate £230.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-10-07_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>IMPORTANT FINANCIAL AFFAIRS.</head>
        <p>IMPORTANT FINANCIAL AFFAIRS.</p>
        <p>The Financial Committee reported receipts amounting to £7ll ss. 7d. on general fund and £52 Bs. Id. on Lib- rary fund. The following accounts were recommended for payment :—General fund, £703 Is. ; loan account, £l7 4s. 10d. ; water account, £BO ; and library fund, £2O ss. 10d.—The committee reported that the overseers had informed them that they have been unable to find suitable offices elsewhere and that if the matter could be arranged they desired to remain in their present offices. It was therefore recommended that the overseers be undis- turbed in the occupation of their offices. That further office accommodation be provided at the Town Hall by making the present kitchen into a general office, by tak- ing up the present flag floor, excavating, and laying boarded floor, removing present range and mantel piece and substituting new mantel piece and small grate ; by extending the east side by double bay windows and changing door and providing necessary office fittings. Enlarging secretary's office by double bay window and providing extra office fittings. That the Wages Fund in the hands of the secretary be increased from £2OO to £3OO. That Mr. Walter Jones, office assistant,give secu- rity in the sum of £2OO ; and that a bond be obtained for such sum from the Local Governmmt Guarantee Societv,and that the Board pay the premiums ; also that the secretary's bond be raised to £3OO and the collector's (J. D. Jenes) from £4OO to to £5OO, in consequence of the increased amounts which pass. through ' their hands on account of the water undertaking. That a sum of £4OO he borrowed under the Runcorn Commissiuners' Act, 1893, to pay for tho stock, furniture, &amp;c., of the Runcorn, Weston and Halton Waterworks Co., taken at a valuation ; and that the tender of the Rev. Robert Fisher of Kendal, to loan £l,OOO at 3t per cent, for ten years be accepr ed. That the Rate Office be open for the payment of rates from 9 to 12 o'clock in the mornings, and from 1.30 o'clock to 5 o'clock in the after- noons. On Saturday mornings from 9to 12. That Mr. Charles E. Baker be paid a further sun, of £2OO on account of his costs. That a sum of £BO on account be paid fo the Runcorn School Board for technical instruc- tion purposes.—Mr. Humphreys moved the adoption of the minutes and referring to the proposal to borrow £l,OOO said £5OO would be required for the purchase of stock, &amp;c. ; then they had to meet Mr. Tunstall's and Mr. Newton's charges, the deputation expenses, and the cost of promoting the undertaking. The Committee there- fore considered it wise to take advantage of this most favourable and opportune offer.—Mr. Bisbrown seconded. ,1 —Mr. Collier : Am Ito understand that the waterworks have cost £70,000 with expenses. —Mr. Humphreys said the waterworks cost £67,500 and they would be obliged 'to take the stock and pay certain expenses. As a matter of fact the cost was rather under than over the original estimate.—The minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>THE NEW STREET LOAN. The Chairman said the Board had applied to tke Local Government Board for the loan for making the new streets to be spread over ten years, but the Local Govern- ment Board had decided in their wisdom, that it must only be spread over seven years. The Eliehway Com- mittee had decided that a further application should be made for ten years and had cited cases where this had been allowed. He siscerely hoped the Local Govern- ment Hoard would accede to their request.</p>
        <p>TECIINICAL IieRANT. The Secretary read the fwilowing resolution he hadr; ceived from the County Council : 16 mat a grant if°e, technical instruction purposes be made to the liorn°atii, merit Commissioners of Runcorn for the year equivalent to the amount raised by the rate ef the £ levied by that body for technical instruction poses for the same period." It was stated that formal the grant had been £222; this resolution would mean reduction to £195 or £2OO.</p>
        <p>PLEASURE GROUNDS. A communication from the secretary to the Board was read with reference to the use of the tery field for a recreation ground. The letter statea the field was now in the occupation of Mr. Savage. E that it would shortly be required for the extension, Cemetery.—The letter was referred to the Ple"tbe that int,* Grounds Committee. Mr. Grice suggested fair ground might easily and cheaply be converted a play ground for the children. TaASCr TIE RATEPAYERS ASSOCIATION AND THE INSPECTOR. Mr. J. E. Langshaw, secretary to Runcorn Rat Association, had forwarded the following resolut.° "That this public meeting of Runcorn liateearra convinced of the importance of a thorough and inll administration of all byelaws relating to the of the community respectfully urges upon the corn Improvement Commissioners Board the sity of constituting the nuisance inspector as per°. Lightburn asked what was the reasat making him a permanent offical. He knew tn.! reason for making the appointment year by Yea'it that as part of the salary was paid by the Local do merit Board, the Commissioners wished to keeP officer in their own hands so that they might disPag him when they thought fit if he did not at UP tot requirements.—Mr. Humphreys moved, and Mr. flaS son seconded, that the Health Committee report ufw" matter.—Carried.</p>
        <p>TIE UNEMPLOYED. The circular letter from the Local Government with regard to finding work for the unernoloied referred to the General Purposes Committee. DIVISION INTO WARDS. Mr. Humphreys said Mr. Handley had given no,tictier motion with regard to an application to the (A) Council for the division of the town into wardS.,, reasons known to all of them, and which they. 81' gretted, Mr Handley was not present that, morning' his behalf he (Mr. Humphreys) gave notice of for next meeting.</p>
        <p>A GRAVE NUISANCE. Mr. Dobson called the attention of the Board foul smell arising from the street grids in Greenwa; Higher Runcorn, Lowlands-road, High-,t., and 0 parts of Church-st. These smells were considered t, very injurious to health, and there was no doubt the town was in a very unhealthy state, but bo' these smells were responsible, he could not say. Dr' the past twenty years he had studied sanitation, haps more than any member of the Board, but he, not prepared with a cut and'dry scheme to remedY nuisance. He hoped the Health Committee wouldi the subject full consideration —Mr. Lightburn sal` opinion of experts was decidedly in favour of the4e as opposed to shafts.—Mr. Grice suggestel thit grids should be frequently cleaned and kept sal with water.—ln reply to Mr. Collier, the SurveYar those streets lying between High-st. and Church-stZt net be flushed.—. Mr. Imison said if Mr. D years' study was not prepared with a specific ford smells, the Health Committee could not be expe,,ete tfj, apply or remedy all at once. At the last meenng tioo Health Committ tw 2e bad formed some strient resolu with regard to flushing and the disinfection ar,A ' sewers, and a proper record of what was done viou,', kept. He ventured to think that under this !le.': rangement there would not be so much complaintl' future as there had bean in the past. This concluded the business.</p>
